1,batch_new500_0240,train,Authentication,NIST.SP.800-177r1.txt,What should an enterprise do with DKIM keys after the end of a contract lifecycle?,"At the end of the contract lifecycle, all DKIM keys published by the enterprise must be deleted or modified to have a blank p= field to indicate that the DKIM key has been revoked.","When using DKIM with cloud or contracted services, an enterprise should generate a unique key pair for each service. No private key should be shared between contracted services or cloud instances. ๠is includes the enterprise itself, if email is sent by MTAs operated within the Security Recommendation 4-10: A unique DKIM key pair should be used for each third party that sends email on the organization's behalf. Likewise, at the end of contract lifecycle, all DKIM keys published by the enterprise must be deleted or modiﬁed to have a blank p= ﬁeld to indicate that the DKIM key has been revoked. ๠is prevents the third party from continuing to send DKIM validated email. Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting and Conformance (DMARC) SPF and DKIM were created so that email sending domain owners could give guidance to receivers about whether mail purporting to originate from them was valid, and thus whether it should be delivered, ﬂagged, or discarded. Both SPF and DKIM oﬀer implementation ﬂexibility and diﬀerent settings can have diﬀerent eﬀects at the receiver. However, neither SPF nor DKIM include a mechanism to tell receivers if SPF or DKIM are in active use, nor do they have feedback mechanism to inform sending domain owners of the eﬀectiveness of their authentication techniques. For example, if a message arrives at a receiver without a DKIM signature, DKIM provides no mechanism to allow the receiver to learn if the message is authentic but was sent from a sender that did not implement DKIM, or if the message is a spoof. DMARC [RFC7489] allows email sending domain owners to specify policy on how receivers can verify the authenticity of their email, how the receiver can handle email that fails to verify, and the frequency and types of report that receivers should send back. DMARC beneﬁts receivers by removing the guesswork about which security protocols are in use, allowing more certainty in quarantining and rejecting inauthentic mail. To further improve authent",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"that sends email on the organization's behalf. Likewise, at the end of contract lifecycle, all DKIM keys published by the enterprise must be deleted or modiﬁed to have a blank p= ﬁeld to indicate that the DKIM key has been revoked. ๠is prevents the third party from continuing to send DKIM validated email. Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting and Conformance (DMARC) SPF and DKIM were created so that email sending domain owners could give guidance to receivers about whether mail purporting to originate from them was valid, and thus whet",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"that sends email on the organization's behalf. Likewise, at the end of contract lifecycle, all DKIM keys published by the enterprise must be deleted or modiﬁed to have a blank p= ﬁeld to indicate that the DKIM key has been revoked. ๠is prevents the third party from continuing to send DKIM validated email. Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting and Conformance (DMARC) SPF and DKIM were created so that email sending domain owners could give guidance to receivers about whether mail purporting to originate from them was valid, and thus whet"
2,batch_new500_0076,train,Authentication,Assessing Security Requirements for Controlled Unclassified Information.txt,What personnel or roles are allowed to have privileged accounts according to A.03.01.06.a?,Personnel or roles as specified in A.03.01.06.ODP[01].,"A.03.01.06.a: privileged accounts on the system are restricted to <A.03.01.06.ODP[01]: personnel or roles>. A.03.01.06.b: users (or roles) with privileged accounts are required to use nonprivileged accounts when accessing non-security functions or non-security [SELECT FROM: access control policy and procedures; procedures for least privilege; list of system-generated privileged accounts; list of system administration personnel; system audit records; system configuration settings; system security plan; list of system-generated security functions or security-relevant information assigned to system accounts or roles; other relevant documents or records] Assessing CUI Security Requirements [SELECT FROM: personnel with responsibilities for defining least privileges; personnel with information security responsibilities; system administrators] [SELECT FROM: mechanisms for implementing least privilege functions] Source Assessment Procedures: AC-06(02), AC-06(05) 03.01.07 Least Privilege – Privileged Functions A.03.01.07.a: non-privileged users are prevented from executing privileged A.03.01.07.b: the execution of privileged functions is logged. [SELECT FROM: access control policy and procedures; procedures for least privilege; system design documentation; system configuration settings; system audit records; list of audited events; list of privileged functions to be audited and associated user account assignments; system security plan; other relevant documents or records] [SELECT FROM: personnel with responsibilities for reviewing least privileges; personnel with information security responsibilities; system developers; system [SELECT FROM: mechanisms for auditing the execution of least privilege functions; mechanisms for implementing least privilege functions for non-privileged users] Source Assessment Procedures: AC-06(09), AC-06(10) Assessing CUI Security Requirements 03.01.08 Unsuccessful Logon Attempts A.03.01.08.ODP[01]: the number of consecutive invalid logon attempts",,,,,DUZELT,YUKSEK,Yanıt gerçek rol/personel belirtmiyor; ODP[01] yapılandırılacak bir parametre. Parametreli kontrol olarak yeniden sorulmalı.,,0.75,A.03.01.06.a: privileged accounts on the system are restricted to <A.03.01.06.ODP[01]: personnel or roles>. A.03.01.06.b: users (or roles) with privileged accounts are required to use nonprivileged accounts when accessing non-security functions or non-security [SELECT FROM: access control policy and procedures; procedures for least privilege; list of system-generated privileged accounts; list of system administration personnel; system audit records; system configuration settings; system security plan; list of system-generated security functions,DUZELT,YUKSEK,Yanıt gerçek rol/personel belirtmiyor; ODP[01] yapılandırılacak bir parametre. Parametreli kontrol olarak yeniden sorulmalı.,,0.75,A.03.01.06.a: privileged accounts on the system are restricted to <A.03.01.06.ODP[01]: personnel or roles>. A.03.01.06.b: users (or roles) with privileged accounts are required to use nonprivileged accounts when accessing non-security functions or non-security [SELECT FROM: access control policy and procedures; procedures for least privilege; list of system-generated privileged accounts; list of system administration personnel; system audit records; system configuration settings; system security plan; list of system-generated security functions
3,batch1000_0565,train,Authentication,NIST.SP.800-63C.txt,What type of encryption does Kerberos use for confidentiality and integrity protection of session data between the subscriber and the RP?,Kerberos uses confidentiality and integrity protection of session data between the subscriber and the RP through the use of a pre-established key that is shared between the Identity Provider (IdP) and the Resource Provider (RP) during an explicit setup phase.,"These resources may include specific devices, files, and information on specific web servers. For example, subject “John” for action “Read” on “Webserver1002” given Authorization statements are beyond the scope of this document and will not be discussed. 11.2 Kerberos Tickets The Kerberos Network Authentication Service [RFC 4120] was designed to provide strong authentication for client/server applications using symmetric-key cryptography on a local, shared network. Extensions to Kerberos can support the use of public key cryptography for selected steps of the protocol. Kerberos also supports confidentiality and integrity protection of session data between the subscriber and the RP. Even though Kerberos uses assertions, it was designed for use on shared networks and, therefore, is not truly a federation protocol. Kerberos supports authentication of a subscriber over an untrusted, shared local network using one or more IdPs. The subscriber implicitly authenticates to the IdP by demonstrating the ability to decrypt a random session key encrypted for the subscriber by the IdP. (Some Kerberos variants also require the subscriber to explicitly authenticate to the IdP, but this is not universal.) In addition to the encrypted session key, the IdP also generates another encrypted object called a Kerberos ticket. The ticket contains the same session key, the identity of the subscriber to whom the session key was issued, and an expiration time after which the session key is no longer valid. The ticket is confidentiality and integrity protected by a pre-established key that is shared between the IdP and the RP during an explicit setup phase. To authenticate using the session key, the subscriber sends the ticket to the RP along with encrypted data that proves that the subscriber possesses the session key embedded within the Kerberos ticket. Session keys are either used to generate new tickets or to encrypt and authenticate communications between the subscriber and the RP. To beg",,,,,DUZELT,YUKSEK,"Soru Kerberos şifreleme türünü soruyor; pasaj simetrik anahtarlı kriptografiyi açıklıyor, yanıtta tür doğrudan söylenmiyor.",,0.89,"a Kerberos ticket. The ticket contains the same session key, the identity of the subscriber to whom the session key was issued, and an expiration time after which the session key is no longer valid. The ticket is confidentiality and integrity protected by a pre-established key that is shared between the IdP and the RP during an explicit setup phase. To authenticate using the session key, the subscriber sends the ticket to the RP along with encrypted data that proves that the subscriber possesses the session key embedded within the Kerberos tick",DUZELT,YUKSEK,"Soru Kerberos şifreleme türünü soruyor; pasaj simetrik anahtarlı kriptografiyi açıklıyor, yanıtta tür doğrudan söylenmiyor.",,0.89,"a Kerberos ticket. The ticket contains the same session key, the identity of the subscriber to whom the session key was issued, and an expiration time after which the session key is no longer valid. The ticket is confidentiality and integrity protected by a pre-established key that is shared between the IdP and the RP during an explicit setup phase. To authenticate using the session key, the subscriber sends the ticket to the RP along with encrypted data that proves that the subscriber possesses the session key embedded within the Kerberos tick"
4,extra_20261108_2022,train,Authentication,Digital Identity Guidelines_ Authentication and Authenticator Management.txt,What mechanism does a password verifier use to make a password guessing attack more expensive?,A password verifier uses an additional iteration of a keyed hashing or encryption operation using a secret key known only to the verifier. This additional iteration makes brute-force attacks on hashed passwords impractical as long as the secret key value remains secret.,"Their purpose is to make each password guess more expensive for an attacker who has obtained a hashed password file, thereby making the cost of a guessing attack high or prohibitive. The chosen cost factor SHOULD be as high as practical without negatively impacting verifier performance. It SHOULD be increased over time to account for increases in computing performance. An approved password hashing scheme published in the latest revision of [SP800-132] or updated NIST guidelines on password hashing schemes SHOULD be used. The chosen output length of the password verifier, excluding the salt and versioning information, SHOULD be the same as the length of the underlying password hashing scheme output. The salt SHALL be at least 32 bits in length and chosen to minimize salt value collisions among stored hashes (i.e., to prevent multiple subscriber accounts from having the same hashed password). Both the salt value and the resulting hash SHALL be stored for each password. A reference to the password hashing scheme used, including the cost factor, SHOULD be stored for each password to allow migration to new algorithms and work In addition, verifiers SHOULD perform an additional iteration of a keyed hashing or encryption operation using a secret key known only to the verifier. If used, this key value SHALL be generated by an approved random bit generator, as described in Sec. 3.2.12. The secret key value SHALL be stored separately from the hashed passwords. It SHOULD be stored and used within a hardware-protected area, such as a hardware security Digital Identity Guidelines Authentication and Authenticator Management module or trusted execution environment (TEE), such as a trusted platform module (TPM). With this additional iteration, brute-force attacks on the hashed passwords are impractical as long as the secret key value remains secret. A look-up secret authenticator is a physical or electronic record that stores a set of secrets shared between the claimant and the CSP. The claimant uses the authenticator to look up the appropriate secrets needed to respond to a prompt from the verifier. For example, the verifier could ask a claimant to provide a specific subset of the numeric or character strings printed on a card in table format. A typical application of look-up secrets is for one-time saved recovery codes (see Sec. 4.2.1.1) that the subscriber stores for use if another authenticator is lost or malfunctions. A look-up secret is “something Look-up secrets are not phishing-resistant. Look-Up Secret Authenticators CSPs that create look-up secret authenticators SHALL use an approved random bit generator, as described in Sec. 3.2.12, to generate the list of secrets and SHALL deliver the authenticator list securely to the subscriber (e.g., in an in-person session, via an online session, through the postal mail to a contact address).",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.95,"keyed hashing or encryption operation using a secret key known only to the verifier. If used, this key value SHALL be generated by an approved random bit generator, as described in Sec. 3.2.12. The secret key value SHALL be stored separately from the hashed passwords. It SHOULD be stored and used within a hardware-protected area, such as a hardware security Digital Identity Guidelines Authentication and Authenticator Management module or trusted execution environment (TEE), such as a trusted platform module (TPM). With this additional iteration",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.95,"keyed hashing or encryption operation using a secret key known only to the verifier. If used, this key value SHALL be generated by an approved random bit generator, as described in Sec. 3.2.12. The secret key value SHALL be stored separately from the hashed passwords. It SHOULD be stored and used within a hardware-protected area, such as a hardware security Digital Identity Guidelines Authentication and Authenticator Management module or trusted execution environment (TEE), such as a trusted platform module (TPM). With this additional iteration"
5,draft_0276,train,Authentication,ARACDE_ Retrofitting Databases with ABAC using External Auth.txt,What is the potential issue when administrators grant more access than necessary to tasks in a relational database using DAC/RBAC models?,"When administrators grant more access than necessary to tasks in a relational database using DAC/RBAC models, they violate the principle of least privilege. Specifically, a specific role for physicians in the cardiology department working at specific hours might require a separate role, but instead, an administrator may create a coarser role granting more access than required, leading to a violation of the principle of least privilege.","Santosh Narecords only within their specialty, during working hours, and from the hospital intranet. Encoding such a policy in a relational database with DAC/RBAC models (using GRANT and CREATE ROLE statements natively available in SQL) results in the creation of a large number of roles. For example, a specific role has to be created for physicians in the cardiology department working at specific hours. Administrators who avoid this proliferation settle for a coarser role, granting more access than the task requires, thus violating the principle of least privilege [37]. Another alternative is to implement access control in application code, which, however, would scatter security logic across services, exposing the database to direct The database community has made some, though limited, explorations towards incorporating ABAC into database authorization. These isolated attempts make suboptimal design choices that require use of external gateways (i.e., proxybased enforcement) or significant modifications to the entire database code. A gateway is meant to intercept connections, but it can potentially be bypassed by anyone having direct access to the database, thereby undermining the overall security. Alternatively, modifying the database engines to support ABAC models involves invasive changes to several thousand lines in the most sensitive code paths, requiring extensive re-integration efforts on every database release. Hence, these approaches can seldom be used in production. It is in this context that we propose ARCADE (Attributebased Retrofit for Context-aware Authorization in Database Engines) – an alternative authorization architecture requiring minimal changes to a database while enhancing it with support for ABAC. Almost all mainstream relational databases provide extension APIs (e.g. MySQL plugins [28] and PostgreSQL hooks [34]), for enhancing their functionality without modifying the database core. To achieve our goal, we identify and develop minimal shim la",,,,,YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,En az ayrıcalık/rol tabanlı veri tabanı yetkilendirmesi.,System Security,0.68,"a policy in a relational database with DAC/RBAC models (using GRANT and CREATE ROLE statements natively available in SQL) results in the creation of a large number of roles. For example, a specific role has to be created for physicians in the cardiology department working at specific hours. Administrators who avoid this proliferation settle for a coarser role, granting more access than the task requires, thus violating the principle of least privilege [37]. Another alternative is to implement access control in application code, which, however, ",YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,En az ayrıcalık/rol tabanlı veri tabanı yetkilendirmesi.,System Security,0.68,"a policy in a relational database with DAC/RBAC models (using GRANT and CREATE ROLE statements natively available in SQL) results in the creation of a large number of roles. For example, a specific role has to be created for physicians in the cardiology department working at specific hours. Administrators who avoid this proliferation settle for a coarser role, granting more access than the task requires, thus violating the principle of least privilege [37]. Another alternative is to implement access control in application code, which, however, "
6,batch500_0037,train,Authentication,The Entire Quantum Computer on a Classical Leash.txt,What is the purpose of the state-preserving classical 2PC used in transforming Auth(|+⟩) into a half-authenticated EPR pair?,"The purpose of the state-preserving classical 2PC is to transform Auth(|+⟩) into a half-authenticated EPR pair b |b⟩⊗Authk(|b⟩) using a linear functionality where on input (b1, Authk(b2)), the 2PC outputs c = b1 ⊕ b2.","We then address the main new challenge: preparing Auth(|+⟩) through classical interaction with the simulation guarantee needed by this reduction. Committer’s Input Choice. Suppose for now that the classical receiver can securely prepare an authenticated |+⟩state on the committer’s hardware. This fixed resource suffices to authenticate an arbitrary input. We first transform it into a half-authenticated EPR pair, then let the committer teleport its chosen input into the authenticated half. The receiver accounts for the teleportation corrections by updating the authentication key. Concretely: 1. The classical receiver helps the quantum committer to prepare an authenticated |+⟩ 2. The receiver and committer transform Authk(|+⟩) into a half-authenticated EPR pair b |b⟩⊗Authk(|b⟩) using a state-preserving classical 2PC with the following linear functionality: On input (b1, Authk(b2)) from the quantum party and a key k from the classical party, the 2PC outputs c = b1 ⊕b2. If the committer inputs |+⟩⊗Authk(|+⟩), they will hold P b |b ⊕c⟩⊗Authk(|b⟩) at the end of the 2PC. They can then correct the half-authenticated EPR pair by applying Xc to the plain half. 3. The committer teleports their chosen input |ψ⟩through the clear half into the authenticated half. They report the teleportation Pauli corrections to the receiver, who updates the authentication key k accordingly. The second step uses the continual security of the underlying authentication scheme. The 2PC functionality decodes the authenticated b2 before measuring its parity with b1. Continual security ensures that the authenticated register remains bound to the resulting logical state, including its entanglement with the unencoded register. Thus, the decoding query used to create the EPR pair does not invalidate the security needed for subsequent operations. Recovery uses the same teleportation idea in the opposite direction, but must preserve the security of the preceding interaction. Simply revealing the authenticat",,,,,YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,Kuantum 2PC ve EPR güvenli hesaplama konusu.,Cryptography,0.92,"Concretely: 1. The classical receiver helps the quantum committer to prepare an authenticated |+⟩ 2. The receiver and committer transform Authk(|+⟩) into a half-authenticated EPR pair b |b⟩⊗Authk(|b⟩) using a state-preserving classical 2PC with the following linear functionality: On input (b1, Authk(b2)) from the quantum party and a key k from the classical party, the 2PC outputs c = b1 ⊕b2. If the committer inputs |+⟩⊗Authk(|+⟩), they will hold P b |b ⊕c⟩⊗Authk(|b⟩) at the end of the 2PC. They can then correct the half-authenticated EPR pair b",YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,Kuantum 2PC ve EPR güvenli hesaplama konusu.,Cryptography,0.92,"Concretely: 1. The classical receiver helps the quantum committer to prepare an authenticated |+⟩ 2. The receiver and committer transform Authk(|+⟩) into a half-authenticated EPR pair b |b⟩⊗Authk(|b⟩) using a state-preserving classical 2PC with the following linear functionality: On input (b1, Authk(b2)) from the quantum party and a key k from the classical party, the 2PC outputs c = b1 ⊕b2. If the committer inputs |+⟩⊗Authk(|+⟩), they will hold P b |b ⊕c⟩⊗Authk(|b⟩) at the end of the 2PC. They can then correct the half-authenticated EPR pair b"
7,draft_0259,train,Authentication,Combining Post-Quantum Cryptography and Quantum Key Distribu.txt,What type of key-delivery interface is used in the ETSI GS QKD 014 standard?,ETSI GS QKD 014 uses a stateless REST with key identifiers as the key-delivery interface.,"D4 QKD topology. How QKD keys reach the endpoints physically: a single point-to-point span, a chain of trusted relay nodes, untrusted-relay protocols (MDI- or TF-QKD) within a span, or multi-path or networked delivery. “Abstract” is recorded when a proof does not ﬁx a topology. D5 Trusted entities. Which entities besides the two endpoints must be trusted with QKD key material: none, trusted relay nodes, the KMS at each site, an SDN controller or orchestrator with key access, or an in-network combiner D6 Key-delivery interface. How the endpoint obtains the QKD key: ETSI GS QKD 014 (stateless REST with key identiﬁers), ETSI GS QKD 004 (stateful), ETSI GS QKD 020 (inter-KMS), a proprietary interface, or, in proofs, an abstract model (shared array, oracle, or explicitly modelled QKD phase). D7 Classical component. Whether a classical ECDH/DH contribution is retained alongside PQC and QKD (three-way hybrid) or omitted (two-way hybrid). D8 Evidence. What supports the design: a game-based proof, a composable proof, a formal-veriﬁcation (symbolic) model, a prototype implementation with measurements, a testbed or ﬁeld deployment, a standard, or informal analysis. A study can carry several evidence types (for example, a proof and an implementation), and we list all The dimensions are orthogonal: a chained-KDF combiner (D2) can appear at the AKE, TLS, or MACsec layer (D1) with PSK or signature authentication (D3), over any topology (D4). Separating D4–D6 matters because they fail diﬀerently: topology determines whether relays exist, trusted entities determine who holds keys in the clear, and the delivery interface determines how a key is bound to a session. Two dimensions deserve further comment. On D2, the distinction that matters for security is not XOR versus KDF but whether the combiner binds the public values (ciphertexts, key identiﬁers, transcript) so that an adversary who controls one input cannot inﬂuence the output in a way that depends on the other; this is the less",,,,,YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,"QKD anahtar sunum arayüzü, kimlik doğrulama değil.",Cryptography,0.89,"Key-delivery interface. How the endpoint obtains the QKD key: ETSI GS QKD 014 (stateless REST with key identiﬁers), ETSI GS QKD 004 (stateful), ETSI GS QKD 020 (inter-KMS), a proprietary interface, or, in proofs, an abstract model (shared array, oracle, or explicitly modelled QKD phase). D7 Classical component. Whether a classical ECDH/DH contribution is retained alongside PQC and QKD (three-way hybrid) or omitted (two-way hybrid). D8 Evidence. What supports the design: a game-based proof, a composable proof, a formal-veriﬁcation (symbolic) mod",YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,"QKD anahtar sunum arayüzü, kimlik doğrulama değil.",Cryptography,0.89,"Key-delivery interface. How the endpoint obtains the QKD key: ETSI GS QKD 014 (stateless REST with key identiﬁers), ETSI GS QKD 004 (stateful), ETSI GS QKD 020 (inter-KMS), a proprietary interface, or, in proofs, an abstract model (shared array, oracle, or explicitly modelled QKD phase). D7 Classical component. Whether a classical ECDH/DH contribution is retained alongside PQC and QKD (three-way hybrid) or omitted (two-way hybrid). D8 Evidence. What supports the design: a game-based proof, a composable proof, a formal-veriﬁcation (symbolic) mod"
8,draft_0298,train,Authentication,VeriRouter_ Towards Verifiable LLM Routing.txt,What condition must be met for a row to be selected as the first admission during the admission filter process?,A row must be selected as the first admission during the admission filter process if it satisfies the condition \( i = 1 \) where \( i \) is the row index and \( Eni = 1 \) and \( uevict \).,"First, node-order authentication binds the node sequence in UpdSeqW to the C pop trace and the committed graph. For every a ∈[NC pop], the circuit authenticates the padded row (0,uC,pop against cgraph and equates its p-th slot with nia,p. Together with n1 = uL0, this makes every real node in UpdSeqW either the initial entry or a neighbor of a node popped from C. Second, the first-visit filter sorts a copy of (ni,i,Eni)i∈[T] by node and original row index and binds it by multiset equality. Adjacent-row checks mark only the earliest row of each real node, enforcing, for every i ∈[T], Eni = 1 ⇐⇒ni is real ∧∀j < i, nj̸ = ni. Whenever a row is enabled, the circuit binds (ni,scorei) to the node-and-score projection of the unique active row for ni in the level-0 subproof’s padded score table and uses (ni,scorei,τi) for its W update. Since n1 = uL0 is real and has no preceding row, Eq. (25) enables the first row and supplies the initial entry update. Thus, PushSeqW is exactly the firstvisit item sequence in row order. Third, the admission filter links W admissions to candidate pushes. For every row, the circuit enforces i = 1 ⇐⇒Eni = 1 ∧uevict This reuses exactly the admitted rows in PushTraceC with the same node, score-table-bound score, and reserved position. Let Ja = {ia,p : p ∈[B0]} be the row indices assigned to round a. Equations (20) and (26) place its admitted first visits in the B0 positions following the round-a pop. (iii) Stopping rule. The entry update precedes all expansion rounds. For round a, if the round has an admitted row, let i = 1}. The circuit derives the one-hot first-admitted flag in UpdSeqW row order and uses it to select When such a row exists, rows preceding i⋆ a in Ja are rejected and leave W unchanged. By the capacity invariant, the first admission evicts a real item exactly when the round starts with ef real nodes, and that item is the round-start minimum. Equation (27) therefore checks the required threshold using this eviction. A round with no",,,,,ELE_ADAYI,KRITIK,Matematiksel semboller/koşul PDF çıkarımında birleşmiş; i=1 ile kabul koşulunun ifadesi doğrulanamıyor. Ayrıca kriptografik kanıt devresi sorusu kimlik doğrulama alanıyla zayıf ilişkili.,,0.64,"Thus, PushSeqW is exactly the firstvisit item sequence in row order. Third, the admission filter links W admissions to candidate pushes. For every row, the circuit enforces i = 1 ⇐⇒Eni = 1 ∧uevict This reuses exactly the admitted rows in PushTraceC with the same node, score-table-bound score, and reserved position. Let Ja = {ia,p : p ∈[B0]} be the row indices assigned to round a. Equations (20) and (26) place its admitted first visits in the B0 positions following the round-a pop. (iii) Stopping rule. The entry update precedes all expansion rou",ELE_ADAYI,KRITIK,Matematiksel semboller/koşul PDF çıkarımında birleşmiş; i=1 ile kabul koşulunun ifadesi doğrulanamıyor. Ayrıca kriptografik kanıt devresi sorusu kimlik doğrulama alanıyla zayıf ilişkili.,,0.64,"Thus, PushSeqW is exactly the firstvisit item sequence in row order. Third, the admission filter links W admissions to candidate pushes. For every row, the circuit enforces i = 1 ⇐⇒Eni = 1 ∧uevict This reuses exactly the admitted rows in PushTraceC with the same node, score-table-bound score, and reserved position. Let Ja = {ia,p : p ∈[B0]} be the row indices assigned to round a. Equations (20) and (26) place its admitted first visits in the B0 positions following the round-a pop. (iii) Stopping rule. The entry update precedes all expansion rou"
9,kaggle1000_0032,train,Authentication,NIST.SP.800-82r2.txt,What compensating controls does the organization consider when the ICS cannot support access control to security functions?,The organization considers providing increased personnel security and auditing as compensating controls when the ICS cannot support access control to security functions.,"Control Enhancement Name Separation of Duties ICS Supplemental Guidance: Example compensating controls include providing increased personnel security and auditing. The organization carefully considers the appropriateness of a single individual performing multiple Control Enhancement Name ICS Supplemental Guidance: Example compensating controls include providing increased personnel security and auditing. The organization carefully considers the appropriateness of a single individual having multiple critical privileges. System privilege models may be tailored to enforce integrity and availability (e.g., lower privileges include read access and higher privileges include write access). Control Enhancement: (1) ICS Supplemental Guidance: In situations where the ICS cannot support access control to security functions, the organization employs nonautomated mechanisms or procedures as compensating controls in accordance with the general tailoring guidance. Control Enhancement: (2) ICS Supplemental Guidance: In situations where the ICS cannot support access control to nonsecurity functions, the organization employs nonautomated mechanisms or procedures as compensating controls in accordance with the general tailoring guidance. Control Enhancement: (3) ICS Supplemental Guidance: In situations where the ICS cannot support network access control to privileged commands, the organization employs nonautomated mechanisms or procedures as compensating controls in accordance with the general tailoring guidance. Control Enhancement: (5) ICS Supplemental Guidance: In situations where the ICS cannot support access control to privileged accounts, the organization employs nonautomated mechanisms or procedures as compensating controls in accordance with the general tailoring guidance. Control Enhancement: (9) ICS Supplemental Guidance: In general, audit record processing is not performed on the ICS, but on a separate information system. Example compensating controls include providing an au",,,,,KAYNAK_KONTROL,ORTA,Artırılmış personel güvenliği ve denetim örnekleri görev ayrılığı bölümünde; güvenlik işlevi erişimi için pasaj özellikle otomatik olmayan mekanizmaları söylüyor. İlişki belirsiz.,,1.00,"compensating controls include providing increased personnel security and auditing. The organization carefully considers the appropriateness of a single individual having multiple critical privileges. System privilege models may be tailored to enforce integrity and availability (e.g., lower privileges include read access and higher privileges include write access). Control Enhancement: (1) ICS Supplemental Guidance: In situations where the ICS cannot support access control to security functions, the organization employs nonautomated mechanisms o",KAYNAK_KONTROL,ORTA,Artırılmış personel güvenliği ve denetim örnekleri görev ayrılığı bölümünde; güvenlik işlevi erişimi için pasaj özellikle otomatik olmayan mekanizmaları söylüyor. İlişki belirsiz.,,1.00,"compensating controls include providing increased personnel security and auditing. The organization carefully considers the appropriateness of a single individual having multiple critical privileges. System privilege models may be tailored to enforce integrity and availability (e.g., lower privileges include read access and higher privileges include write access). Control Enhancement: (1) ICS Supplemental Guidance: In situations where the ICS cannot support access control to security functions, the organization employs nonautomated mechanisms o"
10,extra_20261108_715,train,Authentication,The 3DS Oracle_ BIN Attack via the Network.txt,What does the consistency of identical identifiers (acsTransID ≡ dsTransID) in Cohort E suggest about the authentication process?,"The consistency of identical identifiers (acsTransID ≡ dsTransID) in Cohort E suggests that the authentication process under [Req 411] created the authentication response on the issuer's behalf, indicating a Directory Server stand-in attempt pattern.","Most cohorts separated the knownissued anchor card from all adjacent same-range candidates; a uniform challenge-required class was also observed across all inputs in Cohort G. For Cohort A (Reason 05, Expired card) and Cohort C (Reason 13, Cardholder not enrolled in service), both anchor cards exhibited distinct transaction identifiers (acsTransID̸ = dsTransID). These outcomes are consistent with ACS-associated processing rather than a DScreated stand-in, supporting conditional tier attribution under EMVCo [Req 104] and [Req 105]. In Cohort E, identical identifiers (acsTransID ≡dsTransID) are consistent with a Directory Server stand-in attempt pattern under [Req 411], where the scheme creates the authentication response on the issuer’s behalf. For comparison candidates returning Reason 08, distinct transaction identifiers (acsTransID̸ = dsTransID) are consistent with ACS-associated negative responses after the adjacent comparison PANs passed the Directory Server range check under [Req 97]. These fields support conditional tier attribution, not direct observation of a ledger query or definitive proof of no-account-record status. Candidate issuance was not independently established, so these results show bounded response-class separation for adjacent-card probing, not a cardvalidity classifier. They are also consistent with sparse issuer sub-allocation beneath a participating 6-digit BIN/IIN range, where only some next-two-digit sub-ranges appear more likely than others to correspond to issued accounts. Full PANs are withheld; only cohort-level results are reported. b) Input-Variation Check Across five input-variation runs, we varied transaction amount, caller-supplied synthetic browser/device telemetry, and submitted expiration/CVV2 values; the initial baseline used the researcher-owned control card’s actual expiration and CVV2. The first-result response class remained unchanged across the tested settings. This is a bounded observation about the clientvisible class, not the complete response or an internal risk decision: without correlated AReq/ARes or ACS records, we cannot determine whether a component ignored, normalized, or used any varied field in downstream processing. c) Returned Fields and Fraud Weaponization authentication-related attempts (Cohort E), the pre-authorization lookup returned authenticate_successful ECI 05) and authenticate_attempt_successful cryptograms without interactive cardholder friction. For an attacker, these outcomes are especially valuable not because they prove downstream payment success, but because they arrive before challenge completion or financial authorization and therefore support early candidate triage.",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.75,"than a DScreated stand-in, supporting conditional tier attribution under EMVCo [Req 104] and [Req 105]. In Cohort E, identical identifiers (acsTransID ≡dsTransID) are consistent with a Directory Server stand-in attempt pattern under [Req 411], where the scheme creates the authentication response on the issuer’s behalf. For comparison candidates returning Reason 08, distinct transaction identifiers (acsTransID̸ = dsTransID) are consistent with ACS-associated negative responses after the adjacent comparison PANs passed the Directory Server range ",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.75,"than a DScreated stand-in, supporting conditional tier attribution under EMVCo [Req 104] and [Req 105]. In Cohort E, identical identifiers (acsTransID ≡dsTransID) are consistent with a Directory Server stand-in attempt pattern under [Req 411], where the scheme creates the authentication response on the issuer’s behalf. For comparison candidates returning Reason 08, distinct transaction identifiers (acsTransID̸ = dsTransID) are consistent with ACS-associated negative responses after the adjacent comparison PANs passed the Directory Server range "
11,draft_0179,val,Authentication,FIPS 140-3 Derived Test Requirements (DTR)_ CMVP Validation Authority Updates to ISO_IEC 24759.txt,What method must the vendor documentation specify for obscuring feedback of the authentication data during entry?,The vendor documentation must specify the method used to obscure feedback of the authentication data to an operator during entry of the authentication data.,"Required Test Procedures TE04.44.01: The tester shall verify the vendor documentation that describes the protection of authentication data. The tester shall verify that the documentation describes how the data will be protected against unauthorised use, disclosure, modification, and substitution. AS04.45: (Operator authentication – Levels 2, 3, and 4) If a cryptographic module does not contain the authentication data required to authenticate the operator for the first time the module is accessed, then other authorised methods (e.g. procedural controls or use of factory-set or default authentication data) shall be used to control access to the module and initialise the authentication mechanisms. Required Test Procedures TE04.45.02: If access to the module before initialisation is procedurally controlled, the tester shall initiate an error on an uninitialised module and shall verify that the module denies access. The tester shall assume the authorised role and verify that the required authentication complies with the documented procedures. The tester shall attempt to assume other roles before the module has been initialised and verify that the module denies access to the roles. AS04.54: (Operator authentication — Levels 2, 3, and 4) Feedback of authentication data to an operator shall be obscured during authentication to anyone other than the operator. (e.g. no visible display of characters when entering a Required Vendor Information VE04.54.01: The vendor documentation shall specify the method used to obscure feedback of the authentication data to an operator during entry of the authentication data. VE04.54.02: The vendor documentation shall specify how, if implemented, the vendor allows an operator to confirm authentication data at the time of entry, while obscuring any useful information to all others. Required Test Procedures TE04.54.01: The tester shall verify from the vendor documentation that the authentication data is obscured during data entry. TE04.54.02: Th",,,,,DUZELT,YUKSEK,Soru hangi yöntem olduğunu soruyor; cevap yalnızca belgelemede yöntemin belirtilmesi gerektiğini tekrar ediyor. Belirli yöntem verilmemiş.,,1.00,"The vendor documentation shall specify the method used to obscure feedback of the authentication data to an operator during entry of the authentication data. VE04.54.02: The vendor documentation shall specify how, if implemented, the vendor allows an operator to confirm authentication data at the time of entry, while obscuring any useful information to all others. Required Test Procedures TE04.54.01: The tester shall verify from the vendor documentation that the authentication data is obscured during data entry. TE04.54.02: Th",DUZELT,YUKSEK,Soru hangi yöntem olduğunu soruyor; cevap yalnızca belgelemede yöntemin belirtilmesi gerektiğini tekrar ediyor. Belirli yöntem verilmemiş.,,1.00,"The vendor documentation shall specify the method used to obscure feedback of the authentication data to an operator during entry of the authentication data. VE04.54.02: The vendor documentation shall specify how, if implemented, the vendor allows an operator to confirm authentication data at the time of entry, while obscuring any useful information to all others. Required Test Procedures TE04.54.01: The tester shall verify from the vendor documentation that the authentication data is obscured during data entry. TE04.54.02: Th"
