29,batch1000_0106,test,Cloud Security,Computer Security Division 2015 Annual Report.txt,What is the primary responsibility of a hypervisor in relation to virtual machines?,"A hypervisor's primary responsibility is to provide the abstraction of all physical resources (CPU, Memory, Network, and Storage) and enable multiple computing stacks (operating systems, middleware, and application programs) called Virtual Machines (VMs) to run on a single physical host.","This Recommendation (SP 800-131A) provides more specific guidance for transitions to the use of stronger cryptographic keys and more robust algorithms. DRAFT SP 800-125A, Secure Recommendations for Hypervisor Deployment The Hypervisor is a piece of software that provides the abstraction of all physical resources (such as CPU, Memory, Network and Storage) and thus, enables multiple computing stacks (consisting of an operating system, Middleware and Application programs) called Virtual Machines (VMs) to be run on a single physical host. In addition, a hypervisor may have the functionality to define a network within a single physical host (called a virtual network) to enable communication among the VMs resident on that host, as well as with physical and virtual machines outside the host. With all this functionality, the hypervisor is responsible for mediating access to physical resources, providing runtime isolation among resident VMs and enabling a virtual network that provides security-preserving communication flow among the VMs, and between the VMs and the external network. To design a hypervisor with the core functionality described above, there are architectural options, with each option presenting a different size of Trusted Computing Base (TCB) and hence, a different degree of ease in providing the required security assurance. Hence, in providing security recommendations for the hypervisor, two different approaches have been adopted in this document– one approach based on architectural options that provide more security assurance, and the second approach based on configuration choices that form part of its core administrative functions, such as the management of VMs, hypervisor host, hypervisor software and virtual networks. DRAFT SP 800-125B, Secure Virtual Network Configuration for Virtual Machine (VM) Protection Virtual Machines (VMs) are key resources to be protected, since they are the compute engines hosting mission-critical applications. Since VMs are end",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.89,"the abstraction of all physical resources (such as CPU, Memory, Network and Storage) and thus, enables multiple computing stacks (consisting of an operating system, Middleware and Application programs) called Virtual Machines (VMs) to be run on a single physical host. In addition, a hypervisor may have the functionality to define a network within a single physical host (called a virtual network) to enable communication among the VMs resident on that host, as well as with physical and virtual machines outside the host. With all this functionalit",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.89,"the abstraction of all physical resources (such as CPU, Memory, Network and Storage) and thus, enables multiple computing stacks (consisting of an operating system, Middleware and Application programs) called Virtual Machines (VMs) to be run on a single physical host. In addition, a hypervisor may have the functionality to define a network within a single physical host (called a virtual network) to enable communication among the VMs resident on that host, as well as with physical and virtual machines outside the host. With all this functionalit"
30,batch500_0432,test,Cloud Security,LanG 2_0_ Energy-Efficient Agentic AI for Semi-Automated Sec.txt,What is the F1 score achieved by the small finetuned models compared to the classical models in alert classification?,"The small finetuned models statistically match the classical models on alert classification, incident grading, and rule writing for 5.6 to 59 times less energy, while the classical models match them on two of three tasks.","Against Claude Opus 5.5, scoring flows against each network’s normal traffic reaches 73.42% F1 on unseen networks with no false alarm on 100 benign flows, against 58.10% with 49.00% false positives at no less than 650 times the energy per flow, and small finetuned models statistically match it on alert classification, incident grading and rule writing for 5.6 to 59 times less energy, while classical models match them on two of three tasks. The frontier model stays ahead only in naming attack families on a new network, until about 50 analyst labels exist. The safeguards held under prompt injection, label poisoning and ledger tampering where text filters failed, and one workstation analyzed 3.33 million records in 6.4 minutes, over 30 million an hour, without deploying a rule. Within these limits, small local models carry most SOC detection, a frontier model and a few analyst labels help on new networks, and the guarantees come from structural safeguards. With small samples and one workstation, real deployments with beachhead users come next. IBM Security and Ponemon Institute, “Cost of a data breach report 2024,” IBM Corporation, Tech. Rep., 2024. L. Yang, Z. Chen, C. Wang, Z. Zhang, S. Booma, P. Cao, C. Adam, A. Withers, Z. Kalbarczyk, R. K. Iyer et al., “True attacks, attack attempts, or benign triggers? An empirical measurement of network alerts in a security operations center,” in Proc. 33rd USENIX Secur. Symp., 2024, pp. 1525–1542. S. Wu, X. Sun, E. Dong, Z. Wang, C. Zhao, and J. Yang, “Alert2Vec: Eliminating alert fatigue by embedding security alerts through subgraph learning,” IEEE Trans. Dependable Secure Comput., vol. 23, no. 1, pp. S. Freitas and A. Gharib, “GraphWeaver: Billion-scale cybersecurity incident correlation,” in Proc. 33rd ACM Int. Conf. Inf. Knowl. Manage. (CIKM), 2024, pp. 4479–4486. Y. Liu, Z. Xu, Z. Luo, J. Shang, S. Zhang, H. Zhang, and T. Liu, “MuSAR: Multi-step attack reconstruction from lightweight security logs via event-level semantic ",,,,,DUZELT,YUKSEK,Soru F1 puanını istiyor; cevap F1 değeri vermeyip enerji/verimlilik karşılaştırmasını anlatıyor.,,1.00,"energy per flow, and small finetuned models statistically match it on alert classification, incident grading and rule writing for 5.6 to 59 times less energy, while classical models match them on two of three tasks. The frontier model stays ahead only in naming attack families on a new network, until about 50 analyst labels exist. The safeguards held under prompt injection, label poisoning and ledger tampering where text filters failed, and one workstation analyzed 3.33 million records in 6.4 minutes, over 30 million an hour, without deploying ",DUZELT,YUKSEK,Soru F1 puanını istiyor; cevap F1 değeri vermeyip enerji/verimlilik karşılaştırmasını anlatıyor.,,1.00,"energy per flow, and small finetuned models statistically match it on alert classification, incident grading and rule writing for 5.6 to 59 times less energy, while classical models match them on two of three tasks. The frontier model stays ahead only in naming attack families on a new network, until about 50 analyst labels exist. The safeguards held under prompt injection, label poisoning and ledger tampering where text filters failed, and one workstation analyzed 3.33 million records in 6.4 minutes, over 30 million an hour, without deploying "
31,draft_0194,train,Cryptography,SILMARILS-MV_ Information-Theoretically Secure Multi-Verifie.txt,What is the maximum probability of an honest verifier accepting a forged or invalid signature in SILMARILS-MV against unbounded adversaries?,"The maximum probability of an honest verifier accepting a forged or invalid signature in SILMARILS-MV against unbounded adversaries is \( \frac{Q}{q} + \frac{1}{q - 1} \), where Q is the number of verifiers that could be corrupted and q is the total number of verifiers.","SILMARILS-MV: Information-Theoretically Secure Multi-Verifier Signatures with Dealer-Free Key Issuance and Linear Aggregation for Permissioned Khadijeh Bagheri, Hassan Khodaiemehr, and Chen Feng Abstract—Permissioned blockchains authenticate transactions, votes and quorum certificates with computationally secure signatures, and post-quantum replacements inflate consensus traffic by orders of magnitude. Information-theoretic alternatives have required a trusted authority, quantum channels, per-signature interaction, or single-use keys with bounded transfer. We present SILMARILS-MV, a multi-verifier unconditionally secure signature in which a signature is the vector α = A e(m) and each verifier holds a secret functional ci = ΓTλ(xi) derived from virtual anchors Γ that nobody learns. Against unbounded adversaries corrupting up to ω verifiers and any signers, and observing every decision, an honest verifier accepts a forged or invalid signature with probability at most Q/q + 1/(q −1), a bound that is tight in its leading term. The scheme is strongly unforgeable, non-repudiable, transferable across unboundedly many hops, and linearly aggregatable. Keys are issued by the validators through perfectly secure MPC for t < n/3, without broadcast. To scale, we let the committee itself sign: in threshold notarization, finality certificates have constant size 32(ω + 1) bytes, honest shares are released only on decided values, and per-validator issuance cost no longer depends on n. Arbiterverified keys give clients 32-byte signatures checked in batches, robust redistribution supports membership changes and share refresh, and verifiers can be enrolled retroactively. In C over F2255−19, a certificate verifies in 2.4 µs. In an analytic normalcase model at 1000 validators, threshold notarization needs 71 KiB of authentication traffic per validator per block, 22× less than ML-DSA-44 within the admitted-verifier setting considered by SILMARILS-MV. In a Rust HotStuff prototype with 100 v",,,,,KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,Kaynak formülü doğrulasa da Q/q ifadesindeki Q ve q tanımları derin teknik doğrulama gerektiriyor.,,0.61,"We present SILMARILS-MV, a multi-verifier unconditionally secure signature in which a signature is the vector α = A e(m) and each verifier holds a secret functional ci = ΓTλ(xi) derived from virtual anchors Γ that nobody learns. Against unbounded adversaries corrupting up to ω verifiers and any signers, and observing every decision, an honest verifier accepts a forged or invalid signature with probability at most Q/q + 1/(q −1), a bound that is tight in its leading term. The scheme is strongly unforgeable, non-repudiable, transferable across un",KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,Kaynak formülü doğrulasa da Q/q ifadesindeki Q ve q tanımları derin teknik doğrulama gerektiriyor.,,0.61,"We present SILMARILS-MV, a multi-verifier unconditionally secure signature in which a signature is the vector α = A e(m) and each verifier holds a secret functional ci = ΓTλ(xi) derived from virtual anchors Γ that nobody learns. Against unbounded adversaries corrupting up to ω verifiers and any signers, and observing every decision, an honest verifier accepts a forged or invalid signature with probability at most Q/q + 1/(q −1), a bound that is tight in its leading term. The scheme is strongly unforgeable, non-repudiable, transferable across un"
32,batch_new500_0356,train,Cryptography,OWASP_Application_Security_Verification_Standard_5.0.0_en.txt,What is the minimum version of glibc required for the Linux kernel's special file to provide secure random bytes directly from the kernel's entropy source?,The minimum version of glibc required for the Linux kernel's special file to provide secure random bytes directly from the kernel's entropy source is glibc 2.25+.,"Based stream. Found in iOS SecRandomCopyBytes and Android Secure Random with the correct settings Linux kernel’s special file for providing random data Provides high‑quality, entropy sources from Application Security Verification Standard implementations, such as BCryptGenRandom set by OpenBSD, available in Linux glibc 2.25+ and Provides secure random bytes directly from the kernel’s entropy source with a straightforward and minimal API. It’s more modern and avoids pitfalls associated with older APIs. The underlying hash function used with HMAC‑DRBG or Hash‑DRBG must be approved for this us‑ This section provides additional information for V11.3 Encryption Algorithms. Approved cipher algorithms are listed in order of preference. Symmetric Key Algorithms Salsa 20 specification Extending the Salsa20 nonce Application Security Verification Standard Symmetric Key Algorithms Block ciphers, such as AES, can be used with different modes of operations. Many modes of oper‑ ations, such as Electronic codebook (ECB), are insecure and must not be used. The Galois/Counter Mode (GCM) and Counter with cipher block chaining message authentication code (CCM) modes of operations provide authenticated encryption and should be used in modern applications. Approved modes are listed in order of preference. • All encrypted messages must be authenticated. For ANY use of CBC mode there MUST be an associated hashing MAC algorithm to validate the message. In general, this MUST be applied in the Encrypt‑Then‑Hash method (but TLS 1.2 uses Hash‑Then‑Encrypt instead). If this cannot be guaranteed, then CBC MUST NOT be used. The only application where encryption without a MAC algorithm is allowed is disk encryption. • If CBC is used, it shall be guaranteed that the verification of the padding is performed in con‑ Application Security Verification Standard • When using CCM‑8, the MAC tag only has 64 bits of security. This does not conform to require‑ ment 6.2.9 which requires at least 128 bits of s",,,,,DUZELT,YUKSEK,glibc 2.25+ getrandom arayüzüyle ilişkili; Linux özel dosyası olarak sorulması API/dosya kavramlarını karıştırıyor.,,0.79,"special file for providing random data Provides high‑quality, entropy sources from Application Security Verification Standard implementations, such as BCryptGenRandom set by OpenBSD, available in Linux glibc 2.25+ and Provides secure random bytes directly from the kernel’s entropy source with a straightforward and minimal API. It’s more modern and avoids pitfalls associated with older APIs. The underlying hash function used with HMAC‑DRBG or Hash‑DRBG must be approved for this us‑ This section provides additional information for V11.3 Encryptio",DUZELT,YUKSEK,glibc 2.25+ getrandom arayüzüyle ilişkili; Linux özel dosyası olarak sorulması API/dosya kavramlarını karıştırıyor.,,0.79,"special file for providing random data Provides high‑quality, entropy sources from Application Security Verification Standard implementations, such as BCryptGenRandom set by OpenBSD, available in Linux glibc 2.25+ and Provides secure random bytes directly from the kernel’s entropy source with a straightforward and minimal API. It’s more modern and avoids pitfalls associated with older APIs. The underlying hash function used with HMAC‑DRBG or Hash‑DRBG must be approved for this us‑ This section provides additional information for V11.3 Encryptio"
33,batch_new500_0194,train,Cryptography,sp-800-38Dr1-2nd-prredraft-comment-received-090126.txt,Are the proposed IV constructions sufficiently flexible for wGCM?,Microsoft considers the proposed wGCM IV constructions sufficient and recommends considering single-use randomly generated wGCM keys over a random nonce construction where practical.,"2(e) Is the free field useful/necessary for wGCM random IV construction? If wGCM includes a random nonce construction, Microsoft recommends using the full 192-bit nonce as random input with no free field. 2(f) Are the proposed IV constructions sufficiently flexible? Microsoft considers the proposed wGCM IV constructions sufficient and recommends considering single-use randomly generated wGCM keys over a random nonce construction where practical. Microsoft Corporation Redmond, WA 98052-6399 2(g) Should NIST consider alternative hash constructions such as concat-GHASH? Microsoft recommends using a 256-bit polynomial for wide-GHASH and strongly recommends specifying bit endianness in a way that avoids unnecessary byte shuffling of ciphertexts. In software, concat-GHASH does not appear sufficiently more efficient than a 256-bit wGHASH polynomial to justify the added security complexity. Both wider GHASH approaches require roughly twice the raw multiplication work of existing GCM, and optimizing the modular reduction step at the cost of security complexity is not recommended. Microsoft appreciates NIST’s leadership in maintaining and strengthening cryptographic standards that support secure and interoperable implementations. We encourage NIST to continue prioritizing clarity, minimal parameter variation, and implementation guidance that enables broad, consistent adoption. We look forward to continued engagement as NIST develops the revision to SP 800-38D and the associated guidance for wGCM. Respectfully submitted, NIST Program Management Office Corporate Standards Group, Microsoft Corporation Ericsson (John Preuß Mattsson), July 10, 2026 Thanks for your continuous efforts to produce well-written open-access security documents. Please find attached our comments on the Second Pre-Draft Call for Comments on NIST SP 800-38D. John Preuß Mattsson, Expert Cryptographic Algorithms and Security Protocols Group Function Technology Comments on NIST SP 800-38D (GCM, GMAC, and wGCM)",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"sufficiently flexible? Microsoft considers the proposed wGCM IV constructions sufficient and recommends considering single-use randomly generated wGCM keys over a random nonce construction where practical. Microsoft Corporation Redmond, WA 98052-6399 2(g) Should NIST consider alternative hash constructions such as concat-GHASH? Microsoft recommends using a 256-bit polynomial for wide-GHASH and strongly recommends specifying bit endianness in a way that avoids unnecessary byte shuffling of ciphertexts. In software, concat-GHASH does not appear s",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"sufficiently flexible? Microsoft considers the proposed wGCM IV constructions sufficient and recommends considering single-use randomly generated wGCM keys over a random nonce construction where practical. Microsoft Corporation Redmond, WA 98052-6399 2(g) Should NIST consider alternative hash constructions such as concat-GHASH? Microsoft recommends using a 256-bit polynomial for wide-GHASH and strongly recommends specifying bit endianness in a way that avoids unnecessary byte shuffling of ciphertexts. In software, concat-GHASH does not appear s"
