35,extra_20261108_966,train,Cryptography,Establishing Wireless Robust Security Networks_ A Guide to IEEE 802.11i.txt,What mechanism does CCMP use to ensure that the same key can be used for both confidentiality and integrity?,"CCMP uses a single cryptographic key for both confidentiality and integrity. This key is derived from the combination of the Temporal Key (KeyID) and the 48-bit packet number (PN), allowing the key to serve dual purposes without compromising either aspect.","CCMP key space has size 2128 and uses a 48-bit packet number (PN) to construct a nonce to prevent replay attacks. The construction of the nonce allows the key to be used for both integrity and confidentiality without compromising either.44 As the long-term IEEE 802.11 WLAN solution for confidentiality and integrity, CCMP uses CCM, which was specifically designed to possess the following characteristics: A single cryptographic key for confidentiality and integrity to minimize complexity and maximize performance (minimize key scheduling time)45 Integrity protection of the packet header and packet payload, in addition to providing confidentiality of the payload Computation of some cryptographic parameters prior to the receipt of packets to enable fast comparisons when they arrive, which reduces latency Small footprint (hardware or software implementation size) to minimize costs Small security-related packet overhead (minimal data expansion due to cryptographic padding and integrity field, for instance) No encumbrance by any existing or pending patents. Sections 4.3.2.1 and 4.3.2.2 briefly describe the CCMP encapsulation and decapsulation procedures. 4.3.2.1 CCMP Encapsulation CCMP encapsulation is the process of generating the cryptographic payload (ciphertext) from the plaintext data. The plaintext data comprises user traffic and a MAC header. The primary steps of CCMP encapsulation are the following: The packet number (PN) maintained for the session is incremented. The PN and other portions of the address field are combined to form the nonce. The identifier for the Temporal Key, or KeyID, and the PN are combined to form the CCMP The frame header is used to construct the Additional Authentication Data (AAD). The AAD is a 22-byte or 28-byte parameter comprising several fields, including several addresses and the quality-of-service control field, that are used as additional input into the CCM authentication The AAD, nonce, and plaintext data are provided as inputs to CCM along with the Temporal Key to encrypt the data. The packet header, the CCM header, and the ciphertext data are concatenated to form the ciphertext (or encapsulated) packet security in the standard model. The design goal was to minimize state on constrained systems CCM is an “authenticate-and-encrypt” block cipher mode of AES. As such, it both encrypts and produces a MIC. As shown, the four inputs to the CCM processing are the following: 128-bit cryptographic key, TK 48-bit nonce (derived for a 48-bit packet number, PN) Additional Authentication Data (AAD) Variable length packet (frame body) with header. CCM uses a new Temporal Key every session—with every new STA-AP association. Unlike TKIP, the use of AES at the core of CCM obviates the need to have per-packet keys. As a result, the two-phase key the CCMP encapsulation.",,,,,DUZELT,YUKSEK,Kaynak 48-bit paket numarasının nonce oluşturduğunu söylüyor; yanıt anahtarın KeyID+PN ile türetildiğini iddia ediyor. CCMP nonce ve anahtar ayrılmalı.,,0.74,"CCMP key space has size 2128 and uses a 48-bit packet number (PN) to construct a nonce to prevent replay attacks. The construction of the nonce allows the key to be used for both integrity and confidentiality without compromising either.44 As the long-term IEEE 802.11 WLAN solution for confidentiality and integrity, CCMP uses CCM, which was specifically designed to possess the following characteristics: A single cryptographic key for confidentiality and integrity to minimize complexity and maximize performance (minimize key scheduling time)45 I",DUZELT,YUKSEK,Kaynak 48-bit paket numarasının nonce oluşturduğunu söylüyor; yanıt anahtarın KeyID+PN ile türetildiğini iddia ediyor. CCMP nonce ve anahtar ayrılmalı.,,0.74,"CCMP key space has size 2128 and uses a 48-bit packet number (PN) to construct a nonce to prevent replay attacks. The construction of the nonce allows the key to be used for both integrity and confidentiality without compromising either.44 As the long-term IEEE 802.11 WLAN solution for confidentiality and integrity, CCMP uses CCM, which was specifically designed to possess the following characteristics: A single cryptographic key for confidentiality and integrity to minimize complexity and maximize performance (minimize key scheduling time)45 I"
36,kaggle1000_0774,train,Cryptography,NIST.SP.800-175Br1.txt,What is the requirement for participating entities when using an automated method for key establishment?,"When using an automated method for key establishment, participating entities require source authentication for those that rely on an established trust infrastructure, such as a Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) or a manually distributed certificate.","Keys may be established either manually (e.g., via a trusted courier) or using an automated method. However, when an automated method is used, source authentication is required for the participating entities that relies on an established trust infrastructure, such as a Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) or a manually distributed In general, keys used for one purpose (e.g., the generation of digital signatures) must not be used for another purpose (e.g., key establishment) because the use of the same key for two different cryptographic processes may weaken the security provided by one or both of the processes. See Section 5.2 in SP 800-57, Part 13 for further information. Terms and Definitions The following terms and definitions are used in this document. In general, the definitions are drawn from FIPS and NIST Special Publications. 3 SP 800-57 Part 1, Recommendation for Key Management: General Guideline. A clearly specified mathematical process for computation; a set of rules that, if followed, will give a prescribed result. FIPS-Approved and/or NIST-recommended. An algorithm or technique that is either: 1) specified in a FIPS or NIST Recommendation or 2) specified elsewhere and adopted by reference in a FIPS or NIST Recommendation. See public-key algorithm. A process that provides assurance of the source and integrity of information that is communicated or stored or the identity of an entity interacting with a system. Note that in common practice, the term ""authentication"" is used to mean either source or identity authentication only. This document will differentiate the multiple uses of the word by the terms source authentication, identity authentication, or integrity authentication, where appropriate. An ordered sequence of 0’s and 1’s. A family of functions and their inverse functions that is parameterized by cryptographic keys; the functions map bit strings of a fixed length to bit strings of the same length. Certificate (or public A set of data that uniquely iden",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.88,"However, when an automated method is used, source authentication is required for the participating entities that relies on an established trust infrastructure, such as a Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) or a manually distributed In general, keys used for one purpose (e.g., the generation of digital signatures) must not be used for another purpose (e.g., key establishment) because the use of the same key for two different cryptographic processes may weaken the security provided by one or both of the processes. See Section 5.2 in SP 800-57, Part 1",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.88,"However, when an automated method is used, source authentication is required for the participating entities that relies on an established trust infrastructure, such as a Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) or a manually distributed In general, keys used for one purpose (e.g., the generation of digital signatures) must not be used for another purpose (e.g., key establishment) because the use of the same key for two different cryptographic processes may weaken the security provided by one or both of the processes. See Section 5.2 in SP 800-57, Part 1"
37,batch1000_0898,train,Cryptography,Heart Signal _ECG_ as Vibration Signature for Human Authenti.txt,What was the range of accuracy rates achieved by the TinyML-based DNN model for ECG biometrics across different sessions?,The TinyML-based DNN model achieved exceptional accuracy rates ranging from 91% to 98% across different sessions.,"In contrast, similar research [32] found that PPG signal performance declined as the dataset size increased, underscoring the relative advantage of ECG-based methods for scalable biometric authentication. In summary, this journal explores the substantial potential of a robust TinyML-based DNN model for utilizing ECG signals as biometric identifiers on resource-constrained devices. This study delves into the development of a TinyML model for ECG biometrics, assessing its performance across diverse ECG datasets under varying health conditions, noise levels, sampling frequencies, and situational contexts, achieving exceptional accuracy rates ranging from 91% to 98% across different sessions accuracy that could be improved with higher sampling frequencies and distortion-free ECG data. Additionally, we evaluated the efficacy of different feature sets under spectrogram and spectral features, utilizing SVM, 1D CNN, 2D CNN machine learning models in conjunction with these features. Also in this work, we introduced the concept of the identification space, which optimizes biometric authentication for real-time applications by requiring only 10 ECG segments for training and a single ECG segment for authentication. This is important in real-time applications where processing time is critical. Moreover, ECG pulses contain a richer set of parameters, providing more distinctive biometric information, which makes ECG inherently more robust for large-scale authentication compared to PPG-based spectrograms. This approach enhances efficiency, making the model highly practical for implementation on resource-constrained devices, making it highly suitable for real-time, low-latency applications such as wearables and mobile devices. Integrating biometrics with TinyML for remote biometric functionality presents a powerful combination of security, efficiency, privacy, and scalability. The application of TinyML-based ECG signal biometric analysis paves the way for numerous innovations, parti",,,,,YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,"EKG biyometrik doğrulama, kriptografi mekanizması değil.",Authentication,0.91,"TinyML-based DNN model for utilizing ECG signals as biometric identifiers on resource-constrained devices. This study delves into the development of a TinyML model for ECG biometrics, assessing its performance across diverse ECG datasets under varying health conditions, noise levels, sampling frequencies, and situational contexts, achieving exceptional accuracy rates ranging from 91% to 98% across different sessions accuracy that could be improved with higher sampling frequencies and distortion-free ECG data. Additionally, we evaluated the effi",YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,"EKG biyometrik doğrulama, kriptografi mekanizması değil.",Authentication,0.91,"TinyML-based DNN model for utilizing ECG signals as biometric identifiers on resource-constrained devices. This study delves into the development of a TinyML model for ECG biometrics, assessing its performance across diverse ECG datasets under varying health conditions, noise levels, sampling frequencies, and situational contexts, achieving exceptional accuracy rates ranging from 91% to 98% across different sessions accuracy that could be improved with higher sampling frequencies and distortion-free ECG data. Additionally, we evaluated the effi"
38,kaggle1000_0310,train,Cryptography,5th Annual PKI R&D Workshop _Making PKI Easy to Use_ Proceedings.txt,Where should signing key pairs be generated according to the guidelines provided?,"Signing key pairs should be generated on the user's computer or crypto-token, as recommended by the guidelines.","5.1.2 Key Pair Generation Key generation can occur at the: End user’s computer or cryptoDepending on the use of the key there are factors that impact where it is generated. Although losing a signing private key is inconvenient (as only its corresponding verification certificate is needed after signing data, the CA should hold a copy of this certificate), it may be disastrous if a decryption private key is lost resulting in permanent loss of corporate data. If the signing private key is known to anyone other than the end user then the requirement of non-repudiation (ie “to prove to the satisfaction of a third party that the private key could not possibly have been used by anyone other that the owner of the private key”) is compromised even if the “other” is the CA itself. The CAUDIT PKI will issue separate keys/certificates for signing/non-repudiation, which can also be used for authentication since at its core authentication with X.509 certificates relies on signing a challenge from a party and returning it to be verified, and encryption to end users. To ensure that each certificate is only used for its appropriate purpose the issuing CA should set the appropriate X.509 keyUsage At this stage we recommend generating signing key pairs on the user’s computer or crypto-token; however we also recognise this may be problematic for large scale PKI production and there will be security issues to consider. We expect the onus be on the end user to ensure their signing key is appropriately backed up. Encryption keys should be generated at either the RA or CA to enable automatic safe and secure archive. If an encryption key must be created on user’s computer or crypto-token, the user must make all reasonable attempts to supply this key to the institutions CA for archival purposes. 5.1.3 Certificate Creation and Key/Certificate Distribution After generating a key pair, the public key must be securely transferred to the CA for placement in a certificate and signing by the CA and",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.78,"CA should set the appropriate X.509 keyUsage At this stage we recommend generating signing key pairs on the user’s computer or crypto-token; however we also recognise this may be problematic for large scale PKI production and there will be security issues to consider. We expect the onus be on the end user to ensure their signing key is appropriately backed up. Encryption keys should be generated at either the RA or CA to enable automatic safe and secure archive. If an encryption key must be created on user’s computer or crypto-token, the user",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.78,"CA should set the appropriate X.509 keyUsage At this stage we recommend generating signing key pairs on the user’s computer or crypto-token; however we also recognise this may be problematic for large scale PKI production and there will be security issues to consider. We expect the onus be on the end user to ensure their signing key is appropriately backed up. Encryption keys should be generated at either the RA or CA to enable automatic safe and secure archive. If an encryption key must be created on user’s computer or crypto-token, the user"
