40,kaggle1000_0243,train,Cryptography,Blockchain Networks_ Token Design and Management Overview.txt,What is an example of a nonfungible token used in the Unlock protocol to offer membership access to specific resources?,An example of a nonfungible token used in the Unlock protocol to offer membership access to specific resources is the ability to access a website.,"Dataset Ownership and Sharing: Nonfungible tokens can be used to uniquely identify content or datasets, enabling novel monetization and data sharing frameworks. For example, the Unlock protocol [160] uses nonfungible tokens compatible with the ERC-721 standard to offer membership access to specific resources (e.g., websites) and drive web content monetization. Tokens that identify datasets can also lay the foundations for incentive structures that encourage collaboration by rewarding contributors for access to their datasets or models. For example, the Ocean Protocol [161] offers privacy-preserving and role-based access control features for data marketplaces, where users can trade dataset ownership, as well as for data commons, where users can share datasets publicly. Towards Privacy-Preserving Verifiable Data Exchange Blockchain networks, second layer protocols, and privacy-enhancing techniques have the potential to enable privacy-preserving verifiable data exchange across organizations and on the web. As these ecosystems mature and new tokens are put in circulation, it could become easier for users to build tailored, verifiable proofs based on their tokens by way of aggregating otherwise fragmented identity artifacts directly from their wallet. Building on traditional token-based authorizations, this could ease user onboarding (see Identity Documents, User Credentials, and Domain Names in Section 6.3) and enable new peer-to-peer authentication methods. Different types of proofs and bundles of proofs can be created independently of how the underlying tokens were originated and on a per-relying party or per-session basis, including: • Proofs of ownership (e.g., account balances, licenses, certificates, property passes) • Proofs of collateral or stake (e.g., security deposits) • Proofs of transfer (e.g., payment receipts) • Proofs of participation (e.g., voter stickers) • Proofs of origin or existence (e.g., endorsed and timestamped documents) The disclosure of these",,,,,ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,NFT ile web sitesi üyeliği/monetizasyonu; seçilen siber güvenlik/kriptografi odağının dışında.,,0.75,"novel monetization and data sharing frameworks. For example, the Unlock protocol [160] uses nonfungible tokens compatible with the ERC-721 standard to offer membership access to specific resources (e.g., websites) and drive web content monetization. Tokens that identify datasets can also lay the foundations for incentive structures that encourage collaboration by rewarding contributors for access to their datasets or models. For example, the Ocean Protocol [161] offers privacy-preserving and role-based access control features for data marketpla",ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,NFT ile web sitesi üyeliği/monetizasyonu; seçilen siber güvenlik/kriptografi odağının dışında.,,0.75,"novel monetization and data sharing frameworks. For example, the Unlock protocol [160] uses nonfungible tokens compatible with the ERC-721 standard to offer membership access to specific resources (e.g., websites) and drive web content monetization. Tokens that identify datasets can also lay the foundations for incentive structures that encourage collaboration by rewarding contributors for access to their datasets or models. For example, the Ocean Protocol [161] offers privacy-preserving and role-based access control features for data marketpla"
41,kaggle1000_0852,val,Cryptography,2016 NIST_ITL Cybersecurity Program Annual Report.txt,How many NIST Special Publications (SP) were issued in 2016?,43,"NIST ITL responsibilities were unchanged in the update. In 2016, the ITL Cybersecurity Program addressed its assignment through the following Forty-three NIST Special Publications (SP) (20 approved as final and 23 drafts) were issued, providing management, operational, and technical security guidelines in topic areas The 2015 annual report; cryptography (cryptographic standards used for the Federal Government, block cipher modes of operation, key management, random bit generator (RBG), Secure Hash Algorithm-3 (SHA-3) cryptography, transitioning the use of cryptographic algorithms and key lengths); mobile security (enterprise telework, remote access and bring-your-own device (BYOD), mobile device security – cloud and hybrid builds): application whitelisting; cyber threat sharing; cybersecurity event recovery; datacentric system threat modeling; de-identifying government datasets; asset management – financial services; guidelines for checklist users and developers; networks of “things”; personal identification verification (PIV); protecting Controlled Unclassified Information within nonfederal information systems and organizations; securing Apple Operating System (OS) X; security content automation protocol (SCAP); systems engineering; trustworthy email; and virtual machine (VM) protection. Thirty-one NIST Interagency/Internal Reports (NISTIR) (18 approved as final and 13 drafts) were issued on a variety of topics, including: Cryptography (post-quantum cryptography, lightweight cryptography, NIST cryptographic standards and guidelines development process); mobile security (mobile devices, infrastructure and platforms); attribute metadata; automation support for security control assessments; catalyzing the identity ecosystem; de-identification of personal information; Long-Term Evolution (LTE) architecture overview and security analysis; PIV; policy machine (access control framework); public safety mobile applications; SCAP; security of interactive and automated acce",,,,,ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,Bir yıldaki toplam NIST yayın sayısı bir kriptografi kavramı ya da mekanizması değil.,,0.00,"assignment through the following Forty-three NIST Special Publications (SP) (20 approved as final and 23 drafts) were issued, providing management, operational, and technical security guidelines in topic areas The 2015 annual report; cryptography (cryptographic standards used for the Federal Government, block cipher modes of operation, key management, random bit generator (RBG), Secure Hash Algorithm-3 (SHA-3) cryptography, transitioning the use of cryptographic algorithms and key lengths); mobile security (enterprise telework, remote access a",ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,Bir yıldaki toplam NIST yayın sayısı bir kriptografi kavramı ya da mekanizması değil.,,0.00,"assignment through the following Forty-three NIST Special Publications (SP) (20 approved as final and 23 drafts) were issued, providing management, operational, and technical security guidelines in topic areas The 2015 annual report; cryptography (cryptographic standards used for the Federal Government, block cipher modes of operation, key management, random bit generator (RBG), Secure Hash Algorithm-3 (SHA-3) cryptography, transitioning the use of cryptographic algorithms and key lengths); mobile security (enterprise telework, remote access a"
42,batch500_0303,val,Cryptography,Benchmarking Classical and Quantum Machine Learning for Intr.txt,What is the primary advantage of PegasosQSVC over QSVM in terms of accuracy versus training time?,"PegasosQSVC offers the best accuracy–runtime compromise, while QSVM yields high accuracy but large computational overhead.","PegasosQSVC stands out as the most practical quantum model. QSVM reaches strong accuracy but with exponential increases in runtime due to full kernel matrix construction, a behavior documented widely in QSVM literature. Accuracy vs. Training Time (log-scale) across aggregated dataset results. PegasosQSVC offers the best accuracy–runtime compromise, while QSVM yields high accuracy but large computational overhead. E. Impact of Scaling and Imbalance Handling Angle normalization to [0, 2π] consistently stabilizes quantum feature maps. Experiments without angle scaling showed large F1 fluctuations—confirming prior studies that quantum rotations are highly sensitive to unbounded or poorly scaled inputs. SMOTE improves minority recall without harming quantum kernel structure, provided that synthetic samples remain near decision boundaries. The experimental results indicate that quantum models can approach classical baselines when preprocessing is carefully aligned with quantum kernel structure. Across all datasets, classical models (SVM and RF) consistently achieved stable and high accuracy with low computational cost. In contrast, quantum models were more sensitive to preprocessing decisions, particularly feature selection, scaling, and dimensionality reduction. This behavior reflects the strong dependence of quantum kernels on the geometry of the encoded feature Although this study focuses on binary classification for controlled benchmarking, the evaluated datasets contain multiple attack categories. Extending quantum kernel methods to full multiclass intrusion detection using one-vs-rest or hierarchical strategies represents an important research direction and may provide further insight into class separability in quantum A central finding is the impact of the QKA MI feature selector. On NSL–KDD, QKA MI improved quantum F1scores by more than 12% compared with Random Forest selection. This improvement arises because QKA MI ranks features based on alignment with the quan",,,,,ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,"QSVM/PegasosQSVC model eğitim verimliliği, güvenlik temelli kriptografi görevi değil.",,1.00,"increases in runtime due to full kernel matrix construction, a behavior documented widely in QSVM literature. Accuracy vs. Training Time (log-scale) across aggregated dataset results. PegasosQSVC offers the best accuracy–runtime compromise, while QSVM yields high accuracy but large computational overhead. E. Impact of Scaling and Imbalance Handling Angle normalization to [0, 2π] consistently stabilizes quantum feature maps. Experiments without angle scaling showed large F1 fluctuations—confirming prior studies that quantum rotations are highly ",ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,"QSVM/PegasosQSVC model eğitim verimliliği, güvenlik temelli kriptografi görevi değil.",,1.00,"increases in runtime due to full kernel matrix construction, a behavior documented widely in QSVM literature. Accuracy vs. Training Time (log-scale) across aggregated dataset results. PegasosQSVC offers the best accuracy–runtime compromise, while QSVM yields high accuracy but large computational overhead. E. Impact of Scaling and Imbalance Handling Angle normalization to [0, 2π] consistently stabilizes quantum feature maps. Experiments without angle scaling showed large F1 fluctuations—confirming prior studies that quantum rotations are highly "
43,batch1000_0383,val,Cryptography,STAR_ Scalable TEE-based Anonymous Rate-limiter.txt,What was the default setting for the time-window length and K in the authentication process?,"The default setting for the time-window length was one minute, and K was set to 100.","Parse cred into (code, uid) 4. Check cnt < K and t HLs ←Authservice(h, HLs) Authentication success ④Proxy Request (client) To demonstrate its feasibility, we built an end-to-end proofof-concept prototype of STAR in Rust, consisting of the Core Library, Rate-Limiting Service, protected Web App Server, and Client-side Proxy (see Fig. 7 for the overview). During the implementation, we made several assumptions. We set the time-window length at one minute and K to 100 as the default setting. Additionally, we abstracted the ID-verification mechanism since it can be realized through various means (e.g., TPM-backed certificates). Challenge and Solution: How to Deliver Ciphertexts Although STAR requires users to transmit cipher′′, determining the optimal transmission method is not trivial. The most straightforward way is to encapsulate it in the HTTP header. However, this allows the server to obtain cipher′′ only after the TLS handshake, so the risk of TLS flooding To address this issue, we use a TLS extension to deliver cipher′′. This way, the service obtains cipher′′ when it receives the TLS ClientHello message, allowing it to filter user requests even before completing the TLS handshake. However, the implementation choice described above introduces an additional risk; intermediary entities between users and the service, e.g., ISPs, may intercept cipher′′, allowing them to block user requests and/or send them as their own requests. Although this is a limitation of our implementation, the impact of such attacks is limited. In the case of malicious blocking, users can detect the event (e.g., by not receiving a valid response), and resend cipher′′ via a different network route using the next counter value. Although this consumes an additional cnt value, the impact is minimal if K is sufficiently large or the epoch length is short. Moreover, sending an intercepted request does not affect the rate-limiting functionality of STAR, as it does not increase the total number of reque",,,,,YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,Zaman penceresi ve K doğrulama/rate limiting protokol ayarları.,Authentication,1.00,"Parse cred into (code, uid) 4. Check cnt < K and t HLs ←Authservice(h, HLs) Authentication success ④Proxy Request (client) To demonstrate its feasibility, we built an end-to-end proofof-concept prototype of STAR in Rust, consisting of the Core Library, Rate-Limiting Service, protected Web App Server, and Client-side Proxy (see Fig. 7 for the overview). During the implementation, we made several assumptions. We set the time-window length at one minute and K to 100 as the default setting. Additionally, we abstracted the ID-verification mechanism ",YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,Zaman penceresi ve K doğrulama/rate limiting protokol ayarları.,Authentication,1.00,"Parse cred into (code, uid) 4. Check cnt < K and t HLs ←Authservice(h, HLs) Authentication success ④Proxy Request (client) To demonstrate its feasibility, we built an end-to-end proofof-concept prototype of STAR in Rust, consisting of the Core Library, Rate-Limiting Service, protected Web App Server, and Client-side Proxy (see Fig. 7 for the overview). During the implementation, we made several assumptions. We set the time-window length at one minute and K to 100 as the default setting. Additionally, we abstracted the ID-verification mechanism "
