46,batch500_0454,train,DevSecOps,When Skills Tell Agents Not to Ask_ A Large-Scale Study of O.txt,What percentage of loosening directives are unscoped according to the study?,"According to the study, over two thirds of loosening directives are unscoped (Finding 1.3), meaning one level of autonomy is set for every context a skill runs in.","Unlike the malicious skills catalogued in prior work [18], they show no sign of intent to harm, and unlike the vulnerabilities studied by Liu et al. [20], they work as their authors meant them to. The risk is that a file designed to be shared carries these directives to agents and contexts the author did not anticipate, without a signal to check when oversight is being skipped. ⊲Implication for tool builders. A flagged loosening directive should prompt a review, not a verdict. Scope is a missing safeguard. Over two thirds of loosening directives are unscoped (Finding 1.3), setting one level of autonomy for every context a skill runs in [10, 27]. ⊲Implication for skill authors and agent vendors. Authors can keep most of the convenience with a one-line scope clause (“pass --yes only in CI”). Vendors can warn about unscoped loosening at install time, especially in skills that run shell commands. Fixes should be aimed at upstream skills, not their adopters. Copying does not amplify loosening (Finding 3.1), and 92-97% of variants leave oversight text untouched (Finding 3.3), but loosening is concentrated (Finding 3.2): 960 of 86,048 distinct loosening units (1.1%) account for half of its occurrences. Cursor rules show the same reuse [15]. ⊲Implication for maintainers of upstream skills and catalog curators. Editing the 100 families carrying the most loosening would reach almost a quarter of the loosening-carrying skills that agents load, and reviewing those 960 units by hand covers half of all loosening occurrences. When Skills Tell Agents Not to Ask: A Large-Scale Study of Oversight Directives in Agent Skills Ask scanners a different question. Existing scanners treat skills as a malware problem, in their rules and in their prompts, and loosening directives contain no malware (Findings 4.1 and 4.4). ⊲Implication for scanner developers. An oversight-aware check is a cheap extension: a polarityaware rule set that tells “never use --force” from “use --force”, or a dedicated",,,,,KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,Güvenlik ajanı/skill kapsam yetkisiyle ilgili ama oran tek bir çalışma bulgusu; bağlamda kalmalı.,,0.93,"Scope is a missing safeguard. Over two thirds of loosening directives are unscoped (Finding 1.3), setting one level of autonomy for every context a skill runs in [10, 27]. ⊲Implication for skill authors and agent vendors. Authors can keep most of the convenience with a one-line scope clause (“pass --yes only in CI”). Vendors can warn about unscoped loosening at install time, especially in skills that run shell commands. Fixes should be aimed at upstream skills, not their adopters. Copying does not amplify loosening (Finding 3.1), and 92-97% of ",KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,Güvenlik ajanı/skill kapsam yetkisiyle ilgili ama oran tek bir çalışma bulgusu; bağlamda kalmalı.,,0.93,"Scope is a missing safeguard. Over two thirds of loosening directives are unscoped (Finding 1.3), setting one level of autonomy for every context a skill runs in [10, 27]. ⊲Implication for skill authors and agent vendors. Authors can keep most of the convenience with a one-line scope clause (“pass --yes only in CI”). Vendors can warn about unscoped loosening at install time, especially in skills that run shell commands. Fixes should be aimed at upstream skills, not their adopters. Copying does not amplify loosening (Finding 3.1), and 92-97% of "
47,draft_0270,train,DevSecOps,From Billion-Scale to Million-Scale Language Models_ Operati.txt,What was the result of the Kruskal-Wallis test on the performance of Qwen2.5-Coder models on the Lua dataset?,The Kruskal-Wallis test identified significant differences among models (p < 0.001) on the Lua dataset.,"Although Qwen2.5-Coder 0.5B achieved the highest average Pass@1 score, pairwise comparisons did not reveal statistically signiﬁcant diﬀerences among Qwen2.5-Coder 0.5B, Qwen2.5-Coder 1.5B, and SmolLM2 360M (all p > 0.84). Therefore, the observed numerical diﬀerences cannot be considered statistically signiﬁcant under the evaluated experimental setting. In contrast, on Lua, the Kruskal–Wallis test identiﬁed signiﬁcant diﬀerences among models (p < 0.001). The Nemenyi posthoc analysis showed that Qwen2.5-Coder 1.5B signiﬁcantly outperformed every other evaluated model (all p < 0.001), whereas no signiﬁcant diﬀerences were observed among the remaining models, all of which achieved a Pass@1 score Hortea et al.: Preprint submitted to Elsevier Embedded Post-Exploitation with uSLMs Hyperparameters and reproducibility conﬁguration across experimental phases. Rationale / Application Global Inference Parameters Decoding Temperature Low variance to preserve structural formatting while enabling narrow exploration for Pass@k. Max Generation Tokens Suﬃcient context window to accommodate both reasoning (CoT) and Number of independent completions generated per prompt to compute the P assk estimator. Number of full pipeline executions to calculate statistical mean and standard deviation (,). 8,192, 16,384, 32,768 Fixed global seeds across all 10 macro-runs to guarantee exact experimental replicability. Phase-Speciﬁc Parameters Hard limit on self-correction iterations (Phase II) to prevent inﬁnite Iterative Phase (Phase III) maintains the AST-veriﬁed code from prior Pass@1 and Pass@5 scores for the Directory Enumeration and Backdoor tasks. Task: Directory Enumeration (Task Formulation: BASIC) Task: Backdoor (Task Formulation: BASIC) Embedded Post-Exploitation with uSLMs promising direction is deployment-oriented evaluation, including embedded SLMs operating directly in realistic malware execution environments and their ability to leverage software already installed on compromised sys",,,,,KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,"Kod üreten modellerin Lua testi, post-exploitation çalışmasında geçiyor; sadece p-değerini ezberletmek zayıf.",,0.78,"signiﬁcant under the evaluated experimental setting. In contrast, on Lua, the Kruskal–Wallis test identiﬁed signiﬁcant diﬀerences among models (p < 0.001). The Nemenyi posthoc analysis showed that Qwen2.5-Coder 1.5B signiﬁcantly outperformed every other evaluated model (all p < 0.001), whereas no signiﬁcant diﬀerences were observed among the remaining models, all of which achieved a Pass@1 score Hortea et al.: Preprint submitted to Elsevier Embedded Post-Exploitation with uSLMs Hyperparameters and reproducibility conﬁguration across experimenta",KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,"Kod üreten modellerin Lua testi, post-exploitation çalışmasında geçiyor; sadece p-değerini ezberletmek zayıf.",,0.78,"signiﬁcant under the evaluated experimental setting. In contrast, on Lua, the Kruskal–Wallis test identiﬁed signiﬁcant diﬀerences among models (p < 0.001). The Nemenyi posthoc analysis showed that Qwen2.5-Coder 1.5B signiﬁcantly outperformed every other evaluated model (all p < 0.001), whereas no signiﬁcant diﬀerences were observed among the remaining models, all of which achieved a Pass@1 score Hortea et al.: Preprint submitted to Elsevier Embedded Post-Exploitation with uSLMs Hyperparameters and reproducibility conﬁguration across experimenta"
48,draft_0102,train,DevSecOps,Peer Pressure_ Execution-Verified Co-Evolution of Attacker a.txt,What is the false-discovery rate of the 7B model inside IRIS when detecting vulnerabilities?,A 7B model inside IRIS reaches a comparable raw count of 52/120 but has a false-discovery rate of 95:4%.,"CWE-Bench-Java: CodeQL detects 27/120 (22:5%) and IRIS+GPT-4 55/120 (45:8%) (Li et al., 2025). The same study reports the precision problem directly: a 7B model inside IRIS reaches a comparable raw count (52/120) but at a 95:4% false-discovery rate and Avg F1 of 0:062, below CodeQL’s 0:076, so raw detection without precision does not make a usable detector. Shortcut and degeneracy controls Each vulnerability-introducing diff is paired with a benign diff from the same repository, matched on ﬁle extension and on hunk count within a factor of two, with comment-only and test-only edits excluded from both classes. Two degenerate strategies and two shortcut probes bound how much of the headline number they can explain. always ﬂag / always clear size, hunk and extension probe + control-ﬂow and guard features Balanced accuracy pins either degenerate strategy at exactly 50:0 by construction, whereas on balanced paired data an always-ﬂag classiﬁer attains F1=0:667 while being uninformative. We also report paired accuracy, credit only when both halves of a pair are classiﬁed correctly, which sends both degenerate strategies to zero. The fourth row is the most relevant control for Because a vulnerable scenario is produced by weakening a guard, counts of added and removed conditionals, negations, and guard-name tokens (validate, is_admin, authorize, escape) are a shortcut a diff-level model could exploit without representing security. Given those features the probe reaches 57:9%, so the shortcut is predictive. ceeds the probe by 6:9 points, and by 29:2 on paired accuracy; we do not decompose this margin into shortcut and non-shortcut parts, which would require lesioning these features from 