51,batch1000_0471,train,DevSecOps,NIST_SP800-53r5_Security_Controls.txt,What types of approaches do developers use for testing custom software applications?,"Developers use approaches such as manual code review, security architecture review, and penetration testing, as well as static analysis, dynamic analysis, binary analysis, or a hybrid of these analysis approaches.","The interconnections or changes—including upgrading or replacing applications, operating systems, and firmware—may adversely affect previously implemented controls. Ongoing assessment during development allows for additional types of testing and evaluation that developers can conduct to reduce or eliminate potential flaws. Testing custom software applications may require approaches such as manual code review, security architecture review, and penetration testing, as well as and static analysis, dynamic analysis, binary analysis, or a hybrid of the three analysis approaches. Developers can use the analysis approaches, along with security instrumentation and fuzzing, in a variety of tools and in source code reviews. The security and privacy assessment plans include the specific activities that developers plan to carry out, including the types of analyses, testing, evaluation, and reviews of software and firmware components; the degree of rigor to be applied; the frequency of the ongoing testing and evaluation; and the types of artifacts produced during those processes. The depth of testing and evaluation refers to the rigor and level of detail associated with the assessment process. The coverage of testing and evaluation refers to the scope (i.e., number and type) of the artifacts included in the assessment process. Contracts specify the acceptance criteria for security and privacy assessment plans, flaw remediation processes, and the evidence that the plans and processes have been diligently applied. Methods for reviewing and protecting assessment plans, evidence, and documentation are commensurate with the security category or classification level of the system. Contracts may specify protection requirements for documentation. Related Controls: CA-2, CA-7, CM-4, SA-3, SA-4, SA-5, SA-8, SA-15, SA-17, SI-2, SR-5, SR-6, SR-7. Control Enhancements: _________________________________________________________________________________________________ Require the developer of t",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"flaws. Testing custom software applications may require approaches such as manual code review, security architecture review, and penetration testing, as well as and static analysis, dynamic analysis, binary analysis, or a hybrid of the three analysis approaches. Developers can use the analysis approaches, along with security instrumentation and fuzzing, in a variety of tools and in source code reviews. The security and privacy assessment plans include the specific activities that developers plan to carry out, including the types of analyses, te",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"flaws. Testing custom software applications may require approaches such as manual code review, security architecture review, and penetration testing, as well as and static analysis, dynamic analysis, binary analysis, or a hybrid of the three analysis approaches. Developers can use the analysis approaches, along with security instrumentation and fuzzing, in a variety of tools and in source code reviews. The security and privacy assessment plans include the specific activities that developers plan to carry out, including the types of analyses, te"
52,batch1000_0657,train,DevSecOps,Sign Here_ Attributing a Source for a More Trustworthy Softw.txt,How many authentic malicious packages were listed in Table IV?,36 authentic malicious packages were listed in Table IV.,"These errors were expected due to our best-guess approach to determining the specific version of the repository to use to build the sdist. . 4,228 packages met our authentic source criteria, 2,918 of which matched their requested ref. There were 36 authentic malicious packages, less than one percent of all malicious packages, listed in Table IV. cchubber, malcontent and confirmed with manual review. Notably, safe-chain-test clearly disclosed itself as a proof-ofconcept for testing Aikido Safe Chain’s malware detection celery-flower had cases of hard coded HTTP header lengths, but as comments not in the code. Some versions markdown-agent created dummy files with Microsoft extensions, but solely temporary for testing file identification. @zecho@baileys-mod spoofed request headers which is a well known way to get around website restrictions [30], but did not appear to be inherently malicious. Lastly, discord.js-hakai is likely a fork of discord.js [11], a popular npm library for working with Discord, since the repo is nearly identical to discord.js and has only two commits as of analysis. discord.js is commonly used for exfiltration, however this repository appears mostly unchanged from the fork and likely is not malicious. The only package that could potentially be considered malicious is grandslam. The package used a hardcoded url for a custom Anisette server, an authentication service used by Apple [8]. This is likely an artifact of the author’s testing since there are multiple commented out urls, the project is archived, and in a simplified gist [26] the author stresses that this is a toy example. The Backstabber’s Knife Collection does not provide any additional reasoning for including discord.js-hakai or grandslam, only that grandslam was originally sourced from the Malregistry. All versions of grandslam, safe-chain-test were reported malicious by DataDog according to their latest respective manifests as of writing. MalOSS and Malregistry do not include additiona",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"There were 36 authentic malicious packages, less than one percent of all malicious packages, listed in Table IV. cchubber, malcontent and confirmed with manual review. Notably, safe-chain-test clearly disclosed itself as a proof-ofconcept for testing Aikido Safe Chain’s malware detection celery-flower had cases of hard coded HTTP header lengths, but as comments not in the code. Some versions markdown-agent created dummy files with Microsoft extensions, but solely temporary for testing file identification. @zecho@baileys-mod spoofed request head",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"There were 36 authentic malicious packages, less than one percent of all malicious packages, listed in Table IV. cchubber, malcontent and confirmed with manual review. Notably, safe-chain-test clearly disclosed itself as a proof-ofconcept for testing Aikido Safe Chain’s malware detection celery-flower had cases of hard coded HTTP header lengths, but as comments not in the code. Some versions markdown-agent created dummy files with Microsoft extensions, but solely temporary for testing file identification. @zecho@baileys-mod spoofed request head"
53,batch500_0244,train,DevSecOps,How Deterministic SAST and AI Security Agents Divide the Wor.txt,How many reference vulnerabilities are included in the corpus?,108 reference vulnerabilities,"3. To our knowledge, the first benchmark evaluation on curated, endpoint-annotated ground truth of Claude Code /security-review, Codex Security and DeepSec as security scanners, including the offset between a harness’s reported line and the curated sink, and a recall ceiling set by a harness before its model runs. Concurrent work evaluates Codex Security on real vulnerability-introducing code changes [25]. 4. A sensitivity analysis of how line tolerance and matching policy change localization scores, and a prompt-swap control, with outcome thresholds fixed before its per-class analysis, that tests the harness-policy reading. Section 2 describes the benchmark and method, Sections 3–6 report the findings, and Section 7 discusses threats to validity. Related work, future work and data availability follow. Benchmark and method The corpus consists of 20 open-source web applications and services. Sixteen are third-party projects whose upstream repositories are recorded in the fixture metadata; four were written by Snyk employees for earlier work unrelated to this benchmark and later reused here: two Java applications, coffeeshop and vinyl-marketplace, and two intentionally vulnerable demonstration applications by the first author, keystonebank and sassyreg. It contains 108 reference vulnerabilities, between 2 and 10 per application (median 5). For 62 of them the curated flow from source to sink spans more than one file; the other 46 stay within a single file. The applications cover four language families (16), command injection (13) and SQL injection (13). Each vulnerability is annotated with its full code flow: across the corpus there are 447 authored flow locations, 131 marked as sources, 118 as sinks and 198 as unlabeled intermediate steps, about 4.1 per vulnerability. Appendix A 