60,extra_20261108_6871,test,DevSecOps,Safety Graph_ When Safety Responses Become Attack Surfaces i.txt,"How does Safety Graph (SG) unify cyber attacks, faults, and safety measures into a single formal framework?","Safety Graph (SG) unifies cyber attacks as Attack Graphs (AG), faults transitions as Fault Trees (FT), and safety measures as Event Trees (ET) over a shared propositional state space. This integration allows for the full bidirectional cascade between IT and OT layers in cyber-physical systems, capturing the interactions between these different types of elements.","Reasoning over unbounded cascade cycles is left as future work. In this work, we have presented Safety Graph (SG), a formal framework that unifies cyber attacks as Attack Graphs (AG), faults transitions as Fault Trees (FT), and safety measures as Event Trees (ET) over a shared propositional state space to capture the full bidirectional cascade between IT and OT layers in cyber-physical systems. We defined a sound and complete compilation of SG to classical AI planning, reducing crosslayer cascade path discovery to a PSPACE-complete planning problem solvable automatically by a classical planner. The compilation preserves the full semantics of AG attack steps, FT fault transitions, and ET barrier evaluations as planning actions over a unified fluent space. On a simulated Tennessee Eastman Process test-bench, and the planner is able to discover a critical cascade path traversing IT ↔OT. SG produces hardening recommendations for cascade and verifies them by re-running the planner after applying recommended security control. We have also evaluated how operator’s choices influence the outcome. Scalability analysis confirms tractability across configurations substantially larger than realistic ICS deployments. Automated semantic alignment of propositions across AG, FT, and ET layers, currently requiring domain expert reconciliation, is a natural target for future task. Incremental re-analysis as the vulnerability surface evolves and multi-domain validation in other critical infrastructures are also directions for future work. This work is partially supported by the U.S. Office of Naval Research (ONR) under award N000142612041. The opinions, results, conclusions, and suggestions presented in this work are those of the authors and do not necessarily represent those of the ONR or federal organizations and agencies. [1] C. Tang, A. Alshtein, M. Hardison, E. Holt, C. Sames, H. Tran et al., “Transit Cybersecurity Framework Community Profile,” National Institute of Standards and Technology, Tech. Rep., 2026. [2] M. Baezner and P. Robin, “Stuxnet,” ETH Zurich, Tech. Rep., 2017. [3] D. U. Case, “Analysis of the Cyber Attack on the Ukrainian Power Grid,” Electricity information sharing and analysis center (E-ISAC), vol. 388, no. 1-29, p. 3, 2016. [4] J. Beerman, D. Berent, Z. Falter, and S. Bhunia, “A Review of Colonial Pipeline Ransomware Attack,” in 2023 IEEE/ACM 23rd international symposium on cluster, cloud and internet computing workshops (CCIEEE, 2023, pp. 8–15. [5] M. Barr`ere, C. Hankin, and D. O’Reilly, “Cyber-Physical Attack Graphs (CPAGs): Composable and Scalable Attack Graphs for Cyber-Physical Systems,” Computers & security, vol. 132, p. 103348, 2023. [6] P. J. Hawrylak, M. Haney, M. Papa, and J. Hale, “Using Hybrid Attack Graphs to Model Cyber-Physical Attacks in the Smart Grid,” in 2012 5th International symposium on resilient control systems. [7] M. Ibrahim, Q. Al-Hindawi, R. Elhafiz, A. Alsheikh, and O.",,,,,YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,IT/OT güvenlik-safety birleşik hata/saldırı grafiği.,System Security,0.77,"Safety Graph (SG), a formal framework that unifies cyber attacks as Attack Graphs (AG), faults transitions as Fault Trees (FT), and safety measures as Event Trees (ET) over a shared propositional state space to capture the full bidirectional cascade between IT and OT layers in cyber-physical systems. We defined a sound and complete compilation of SG to classical AI planning, reducing crosslayer cascade path discovery to a PSPACE-complete planning problem solvable automatically by a classical planner. The compilation preserves the full semantics",YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,IT/OT güvenlik-safety birleşik hata/saldırı grafiği.,System Security,0.77,"Safety Graph (SG), a formal framework that unifies cyber attacks as Attack Graphs (AG), faults transitions as Fault Trees (FT), and safety measures as Event Trees (ET) over a shared propositional state space to capture the full bidirectional cascade between IT and OT layers in cyber-physical systems. We defined a sound and complete compilation of SG to classical AI planning, reducing crosslayer cascade path discovery to a PSPACE-complete planning problem solvable automatically by a classical planner. The compilation preserves the full semantics"
61,batch500_0374,train,Digital Forensics,What Must Be Done Here_ Using a Bounded Application-Security.txt,What happens when a change is confined to one domain in the ontology?,"When a change is confined to one domain, it results in a single closed working set.","For space, the full expansion into practice, mechanisms, and artifacts is shown for one representative objective (ACO-IAT-006); the pattern is identical for the others in the slice. A trade-off follows from the boundedness itself. Because every relation stays within its slice, a change confined to one domain yields a single closed working set — but a change that spans domains (authentication logic that also alters the audit trail, say) must query each governing slice in turn, and the ontology models no interaction between them. The slice partition that makes each answer closed is also what leaves cross-domain composition to the engineer; surfacing inter-slice dependencies is out of scope here. The same burden appears on the normative side: an obligation that emerges only from the joint reading of several provisions — common in NIS2 — is surfaced claim by claim, so composing interdependent provisions into one engineering requirement likewise remains with the reader. The forward use queries the deposited ontology directly; the inverse use additionally consumes the source→entity substrate, whose construction, similarity calibration, and cross-validation are the cycle paper’s contribution [6], referenced and not re-derived here — and the inverse mapping is coverage grounding, not a validated per-article legal reading. The ontology’s formalization, FAIR assessment, and change-governance protocol are likewise reported in the artefact paper [5]. Both artefacts and their per-identifier provenance are deposited under open licences (identifiers in [5], [6]), so the working sets shown here are reproducible and open to scrutiny. Existing cybersecurity ontologies model the threat and the risk; an engineer — or an AI assistant — at a point of work also needs to know what must be done here, and an organization under a new regulation needs to know what must be done to comply. We have shown that a bounded, functionally typed application-security ontology answers both by returning th",,,,,YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,"Ontoloji değişiminin kapalı çalışma kümesine etkisi, adli bilişimden ziyade erişim/kanıt modelleme konusu.",System Security,0.89,"within its slice, a change confined to one domain yields a single closed working set — but a change that spans domains (authentication logic that also alters the audit trail, say) must query each governing slice in turn, and the ontology models no interaction between them. The slice partition that makes each answer closed is also what leaves cross-domain composition to the engineer; surfacing inter-slice dependencies is out of scope here. The same burden appears on the normative side: an obligation that emerges only from the joint reading of se",YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,"Ontoloji değişiminin kapalı çalışma kümesine etkisi, adli bilişimden ziyade erişim/kanıt modelleme konusu.",System Security,0.89,"within its slice, a change confined to one domain yields a single closed working set — but a change that spans domains (authentication logic that also alters the audit trail, say) must query each governing slice in turn, and the ontology models no interaction between them. The slice partition that makes each answer closed is also what leaves cross-domain composition to the engineer; surfacing inter-slice dependencies is out of scope here. The same burden appears on the normative side: an obligation that emerges only from the joint reading of se"
62,batch500_0173,train,Digital Forensics,Revision-Bound Evidence Cuts_ Authenticated Queries and Cont.txt,What is the total number of records returned by the declared postcranial query?,Eight cohort/stratum groups covering 225 source records.,"Even multiplying a numerator and denominator by the same factor fails the exact-group check. If two storage recipes restore identical source bytes, the source and query roots, bag and qualified result are unchanged. A different transport payload alone therefore does not stale this query contract. A changed source root or parameter contract invalidates the materialized-view binding; these are different revision domains. The argument composes deterministic relational evaluation with SCENE’s existing source-binding result, under its stated runtime/key/root assumptions. It does not claim a new SQLite index or a succinct proof that a physical plan executed. Actual relational workload. The artifact imports all 16,824 records and all 59 original field lexemes into a fresh SQLite database, with an explicit source-column dictionary, URI uniqueness and count-alias consistency constraints. Complete base-value verification recomputes every parsed record hash against the original source, including fields unused by the current aggregate. The declared postcranial query fixes Protoliterate, both context labels, the five exact taxon labels and four anatomical strata. It returns eight cohort/stratum groups covering 225 source records, 5/61 versus 2/164, and the same exact contrast 349/5002 through the scope_contrasts.certify and verify_certificate interface. Three measured physical strategies use a table scan, a covering index, and a covered materialized bag. Their EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN outputs are retained as diagnostics, never parsed into an authentication decision; SQLite warns that this format can change between versions [76]. The experiment separately reports import/index/materialization cost, database bytes, aggregate SQL time, full source-value checking and full-source answer verification. In one SQLite 3.53.3 run, aggregate SQL took 4.215, 0.127 and 0.079ms, respectively; database files occupied 24,399,872, 27,901,952 and 27,975,680 bytes. Import and complete base-value verifica",,,,,ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,Postcranial arkeolojik/anatomik sorgu sayımı siber güvenlik veri seti için alan dışı.,,1.00,"base-value verification recomputes every parsed record hash against the original source, including fields unused by the current aggregate. The declared postcranial query fixes Protoliterate, both context labels, the five exact taxon labels and four anatomical strata. It returns eight cohort/stratum groups covering 225 source records, 5/61 versus 2/164, and the same exact contrast 349/5002 through the scope_contrasts.certify and verify_certificate interface. Three measured physical strategies use a table scan, a covering index, and a covered mat",ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,Postcranial arkeolojik/anatomik sorgu sayımı siber güvenlik veri seti için alan dışı.,,1.00,"base-value verification recomputes every parsed record hash against the original source, including fields unused by the current aggregate. The declared postcranial query fixes Protoliterate, both context labels, the five exact taxon labels and four anatomical strata. It returns eight cohort/stratum groups covering 225 source records, 5/61 versus 2/164, and the same exact contrast 349/5002 through the scope_contrasts.certify and verify_certificate interface. Three measured physical strategies use a table scan, a covering index, and a covered mat"
63,batch500_0113,train,Digital Forensics,AWARE-RG_ Abstraction Aware Explanation of Resiliency Graphs.txt,Which two predicates share the same tactic assignment and are therefore part of the same knowledge unit?,"vul-CVE-2019-0575 and vul-CVE-2019-0584 share the same tactic assignment ""Execution"" and are therefore part of the same knowledge unit K3.","Predicates sharing a tactic assignment are grouped into a single knowledge unit. Rather than consuming a single LLM pass, the domain expert samples multiple outputs and selects the assignment that best reflects each predicate’s causal role in the attack-fault chain rather than its syntactic similarity to a tactic name, preventing a hallucinated assignment from placing a knowledge unit at the wrong propagation stage. Table I shows the mappings for the running example. Formal Concept Analysis (FCA) groups the mapped predicates into coherent knowledge units. For AWARE-RG, FCA operates over a formal context κ = (P, T, φ), where the predicate set P ⊆F RG is the set of objects, the tactic set T the set of attributes, and the incidence relation φ ⊆P × T is defined by (p, t) ∈φ iff t ∈τ(p). Predicates sharing the same tactic assignment form a single knowledge unit and are revealed or removed together as the abstraction structure transitions between levels. This reflects how analyst knowledge is organized in practice: an IT analyst’s boundary is better represented by whether they understand the Lateral Movement stage than by whether they know each CVE predicate within it, so knowledge units capture analyst knowledge at the level of attack propagation concepts rather than isolated predicates. {K1, . . . , Km} of F RG: each block Ki is the set of predicates sharing a common tactic assignment, and all admissible projections in the AWARE-RG abstraction structure remove unions of these blocks. Hence no abstraction node can expose a semantically incoherent predicate set, such as a state in which one CVE’s exploitability is visible while another CVE serving the same role in attack propagation is removed. In Table I, vul-CVE-2019-0575 and vul-CVE-2019-0584 share Execution as their tactic, forming knowledge unit K3, and are revealed or removed together at every abstraction. ICS Cyber Kill Chain Mapper organizes FCA derived knowledge units according to the progression of attacks from ",,,,,YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,"MITRE tactic ve saldırı yayılımı, adli delil analizi değil.",Incident Response,0.82,"Km} of F RG: each block Ki is the set of predicates sharing a common tactic assignment, and all admissible projections in the AWARE-RG abstraction structure remove unions of these blocks. Hence no abstraction node can expose a semantically incoherent predicate set, such as a state in which one CVE’s exploitability is visible while another CVE serving the same role in attack propagation is removed. In Table I, vul-CVE-2019-0575 and vul-CVE-2019-0584 share Execution as their tactic, forming knowledge unit K3, and are revealed or removed together ",YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,"MITRE tactic ve saldırı yayılımı, adli delil analizi değil.",Incident Response,0.82,"Km} of F RG: each block Ki is the set of predicates sharing a common tactic assignment, and all admissible projections in the AWARE-RG abstraction structure remove unions of these blocks. Hence no abstraction node can expose a semantically incoherent predicate set, such as a state in which one CVE’s exploitability is visible while another CVE serving the same role in attack propagation is removed. In Table I, vul-CVE-2019-0575 and vul-CVE-2019-0584 share Execution as their tactic, forming knowledge unit K3, and are revealed or removed together "
