65,batch1000_0660,train,Digital Forensics,When Benign Is Unverified_ Label-Signal Mismatch and Abstent.txt,What percentage of snapshots are sent to analyst review when all gates are disabled?,"When all gates are disabled, 95.3% of snapshots are sent to analyst review.","When “Benign” Is Unverified: Label–Signal Mismatch and Abstention-First Triage in Graph-Based Memory Forensics Viraj Rajeshbhai Chudasamaa, Eduardo Palmierib, Dipo Dunsina, Aisha Junejob, Mohamed Chahine Ghanemb,c,∗ aDepartment of Applied Computing, IICL, University of Wales Trinity Saint David, London, E14 4HA, UK bSchool of Computer Science and Mathematics, Keele University, Newcastle-under-Lyme, ST5 5BG, UK cSchool of Computer Science and Informatics, University of Liverpool, Liverpool, L69 3BX, UK Machine-learning triage for memory forensics is usually validated on corpora whose benign class is asserted by provenance and never verified. We report a validation study of a graph-based triage pipeline for Windows memory images: Volatility 3 output is compiled into typed heterogeneous graphs and scored by a temperature-scaled GINE classifier and two one-class encoders, fused with rule risk under an abstention gate. On MalVol-25 a transparent rule layer contradicts the benign label on every control (15/15 and 18/18 flagged; at least 11 of 15 paired controls rated HIGH or CRITICAL), and mean injection and command-and-control counts run higher on controls than on infected captures. We call this rule–label discordance label–signal mismatch; it shows the benign class is unverified, not compromised. Leave-one-family-out AUROC over matched pairs is provably three-valued, so a reported mean and standard deviation identify the fold composition. The learned model (0.786 ± 0.311; 9/4/1, scored in-sample by a frozen checkpoint) equals a logistic model over rule-derived attributes (0.786 ± 0.410; 11/0/3) in mean only, and its significance rests on dropping four saturated ties. The fusion path sends 97.7% of snapshots to analyst review, 95.3% with every gate disabled. Two public feature-level corpora show the same defect: on CIC-MalMem-2022 every benign row carries injected regions and one host-population feature reaches AUROC 0.997 under leave-one-family-out validation; on the Si",,,,,YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,Analist alarm/snapshot değerlendirme oranı.,Incident Response,0.67,"dropping four saturated ties. The fusion path sends 97.7% of snapshots to analyst review, 95.3% with every gate disabled. Two public feature-level corpora show the same defect: on CIC-MalMem-2022 every benign row carries injected regions and one host-population feature reaches AUROC 0.997 under leave-one-family-out validation; on the Si",YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,Analist alarm/snapshot değerlendirme oranı.,Incident Response,0.67,"dropping four saturated ties. The fusion path sends 97.7% of snapshots to analyst review, 95.3% with every gate disabled. Two public feature-level corpora show the same defect: on CIC-MalMem-2022 every benign row carries injected regions and one host-population feature reaches AUROC 0.997 under leave-one-family-out validation; on the Si"
66,kaggle1000_0567,train,Digital Forensics,Linux_system_programming_talking_directly_to_the_kernel_and_C_library.txt,What is the practical limit for the total size of all keys and values associated with a single file in an ext3 filesystem?,"The practical limit for the total size of all keys and values associated with a single file in an ext3 filesystem is about 1 KB to 8 KB, depending on the size of the filesystem's blocks.","When reading an attribute, the kernel provides the size; when writing an attribute, you must provide the size. Linux does not enforce any limits on the number of keys, the length of a key, the size of a value, or the total space that can be consumed by all of the keys and values associated with a file. Filesystems, however, have practical limits. These limits are usually mani‐ fested as constraints on the total size of all of the keys and values associated with a given With ext3, for example, all extended attributes for a given file must fit within the slack space in the file’s inode and up to one additional filesystem block. (Older versions of ext3 were limited to the one filesystem block, without the in-inode storage.) This equates to a practical limit of about 1 KB to 8 KB per file, depending on the size of the filesystem’s blocks. XFS, in contrast, has no practical limits. Even with ext3, these limits are usually not an issue, as most keys and values are short text strings. Nonetheless, keep them in mind—think twice before storing the entire revision control history of a project in a file’s extended attributes! Extended attribute namespaces The namespaces associated with extended attributes are more than just organizational tools. The kernel enforces different access policies depending on the namespace. Linux currently defines four extended attribute namespaces and may define more in the future. The current four are as follows: The system namespace is used to implement kernel features that utilize extended attributes, such as access control lists (ACLs). An example of an extended attribute in this namespace is system.posix_acl_access. Whether users can read from or write to these attributes depends on the security module in place. Assume at worst that no user (including root) can even read these attributes. The security namespace is used to implement security modules, such as SELinux. Whether user-space applications can access these attributes depends, again, on",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.92,"have practical limits. These limits are usually mani‐ fested as constraints on the total size of all of the keys and values associated with a given With ext3, for example, all extended attributes for a given file must fit within the slack space in the file’s inode and up to one additional filesystem block. (Older versions of ext3 were limited to the one filesystem block, without the in-inode storage.) This equates to a practical limit of about 1 KB to 8 KB per file, depending on the size of the filesystem’s blocks. XFS,",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.92,"have practical limits. These limits are usually mani‐ fested as constraints on the total size of all of the keys and values associated with a given With ext3, for example, all extended attributes for a given file must fit within the slack space in the file’s inode and up to one additional filesystem block. (Older versions of ext3 were limited to the one filesystem block, without the in-inode storage.) This equates to a practical limit of about 1 KB to 8 KB per file, depending on the size of the filesystem’s blocks. XFS,"
67,kaggle1000_0420,train,Digital Forensics,NIST.SP.800-171r2.txt,What are the recordings of evidence of activities performed or results achieved called?,"The recordings of evidence of activities performed or results achieved are called ""evidence records.""","The recordings (automated and/or manual) of evidence of activities performed or results achieved (e.g., forms, reports, test results), which serve as a basis for verifying that the organization and the system are performing as intended. Also used to refer to units of related data fields (i.e., groups of data fields that can be accessed by a program and that contain the complete set of information on particular items). Access to an organizational system by a user (or a process acting on behalf of a user) communicating through an external network (e.g., the Internet). _________________________________________________________________________________________________ Maintenance activities conducted by individuals communicating through an external network (e.g., the Internet). Protection against the capture of transmitted authentication or access control information and its subsequent retransmission with the intent of producing an unauthorized effect or gaining unauthorized access. A measure of the extent to which an entity is threatened by a potential circumstance or event, and typically is a function of: (i) the adverse impact, or magnitude of harm, that would arise if the circumstance or event occurs; and (ii) the likelihood of The process of identifying risks to organizational operations (including mission, functions, image, reputation), organizational assets, individuals, other organizations, and the Nation, resulting from the operation of a system. Actions taken to render data written on media unrecoverable by both ordinary and, for some forms of sanitization, extraordinary Process to remove information from media such that data recovery is not possible. It includes removing all classified labels, markings, and activity logs. A condition that results from the establishment and maintenance of protective measures that enable an organization to perform its mission or critical functions despite risks posed by threats to its use of systems. Protective measures may invol",,,,,KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,"""Evidence records"" ifadesi genel dokümantasyon/standart tanımı olabilir; adli delil zinciriyle bağ zayıf.",,0.75,"The recordings (automated and/or manual) of evidence of activities performed or results achieved (e.g., forms, reports, test results), which serve as a basis for verifying that the organization and the system are performing as intended. Also used to refer to units of related data fields (i.e., groups of data fields that can be accessed by a program and that contain the complete set of information on particular items). Access to an organizational system by a user (or a process acting on behalf of a user) communicating through",KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,"""Evidence records"" ifadesi genel dokümantasyon/standart tanımı olabilir; adli delil zinciriyle bağ zayıf.",,0.75,"The recordings (automated and/or manual) of evidence of activities performed or results achieved (e.g., forms, reports, test results), which serve as a basis for verifying that the organization and the system are performing as intended. Also used to refer to units of related data fields (i.e., groups of data fields that can be accessed by a program and that contain the complete set of information on particular items). Access to an organizational system by a user (or a process acting on behalf of a user) communicating through"
68,batch1000_0798,train,Digital Forensics,Cyberjutsu.txt,What is the purpose of performing a forensic capture before reimaging or rebuilding systems to preserve evidence of threats?,"The purpose of performing a forensic capture before reimaging or rebuilding systems is to preserve evidence of threats. This practice is recommended when implementing non-persistence as a countermeasure against threats, such as by regularly reimaging or rebuilding all systems to destroy any unauthorized access.","If you decide to implement non-persistence as a countermeasure against threats—such as by regularly reimaging or rebuilding all your systems to destroy any unauthorized access—consider performing a forensic capture before reimaging or teardown to preserve evidence of threats. [AU-11: Audit Record Retention | (1) Long-Term Retrieval Capability; MP-6: Media Sanitization | (8) Remote Purging or Wiping of Information; SI-14: NonPersistence; SI-18: Information Disposal] 4. Implement, document, and enforce baseline system configura­ tions in your organization so forensic analysts can more eas­ ily determine what information could have been altered by a threat. [CM-2: Baseline Configuration | (7) Configure Systems and Components for High-Risk Areas; SC-34: Non-Modifiable Executable Programs] 5. Provide training and simulated exercises for your forensic staff to facilitate effective responses in the event of a security incident. [IR-2: Incident Response Training | (1) Simulated Events] 6. Establish a forensic analysis team with the capability and authori­ zation to conduct real-time forensic collection and investigation. [IR-10: Integrated Information Security Analysis Team] Use safeguards to validate that forensic systems, software, and hardware have not been tampered with. [SA-12: Supply Chain Risk Management | (10) Validate as Genuine and Not Altered | (14) Identity and Traceability] In this chapter, we reviewed the shinobi techniques of capturing and interrogating enemy shinobi, as well as tactics used to evade capture. We touched on how collecting more forensic evidence gives the threat actor more opportunities to feed investigators false data points—and why it can be better to interact with live threats. We discussed best practices around forensic capabilities to gain visibility into threats, along with advanced techniques, such as ambushes and traps, for confronting threats. In the next chapter, we will discuss the most destructive mode of attack in the shinobi’s ars",,,,,KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,Kaynakta adli görüntü alma öncesinde yeniden kurmanın kanıtı yok etme riski açık; saklanabilir.,,0.81,"If you decide to implement non-persistence as a countermeasure against threats—such as by regularly reimaging or rebuilding all your systems to destroy any unauthorized access—consider performing a forensic capture before reimaging or teardown to preserve evidence of threats. [AU-11: Audit Record Retention | (1) Long-Term Retrieval Capability; MP-6: Media Sanitization | (8) Remote Purging or Wiping of Information; SI-14: NonPersistence; SI-18: Information Disposal] 4. Implement, document, and enforce baseline system configura­ tions in your org",KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,Kaynakta adli görüntü alma öncesinde yeniden kurmanın kanıtı yok etme riski açık; saklanabilir.,,0.81,"If you decide to implement non-persistence as a countermeasure against threats—such as by regularly reimaging or rebuilding all your systems to destroy any unauthorized access—consider performing a forensic capture before reimaging or teardown to preserve evidence of threats. [AU-11: Audit Record Retention | (1) Long-Term Retrieval Capability; MP-6: Media Sanitization | (8) Remote Purging or Wiping of Information; SI-14: NonPersistence; SI-18: Information Disposal] 4. Implement, document, and enforce baseline system configura­ tions in your org"
69,batch500_0089,train,Digital Forensics,Forensic Analysis of the HackRF One with PortaPack Mayhem Fi.txt,What is the format in which forensically relevant data is written to the microSD card of a device running Mayhem v2.0.1?,"Forensically relevant data is written to the microSD card of a device running Mayhem v2.0.1 in a specific format that depends on the real-time clock (RTC) battery state, traces of on-device trimming, and text editing. The exact format and its dependencies are established at the source-code level for forensic analysis.","Forensic Analysis of the HackRF One with PortaPack Mayhem Department of Digital Forensics Engineering, Fırat University, Elazığ, Türkiye Software-defined radios (SDRs) have become an inexpensive, portable platform for replay attacks, location spoofing and jamming. The HackRF One can operate stand-alone when fitted with a PortaPack interface board running the open-source Mayhem firmware. No peer-reviewed study has addressed the forensic analysis of this device family. This work establishes, at source-code level, where forensically relevant data reside on the microSD card of a device running Mayhem v2.0.1, in which format, when they are written and how they should be interpreted. The methodology is applied to an SD card image that the author acquired from their own device after four months of real use, and every inference is drawn from the image alone. The workflow covers timestamp rules that depend on the real-time clock (RTC) battery state, traces of on-device trimming and text editing, cluster-level recovery of deleted in-phase/quadrature (IQ) captures, and decoding of recorded signals cross-checked with an independent decoder. In the example image, the final part of a deleted IQ capture and a trimmed-away capture segment are recovered, and six captures are attributed to a single rolling-code on-off keying (OOK) transmitter sharing one 32-bit identifier. The study provides practitioners with an artifact catalogue, interpretation rules and acquisition Keywords: digital forensics; embedded device forensics; FAT32; deleted data recovery; OOK Software-defined radios (SDRs) are devices that use software in place of most of the circuitry that processes radio signals. As a result, a single piece of hardware can work as both a receiver and a transmitter over a wide frequency range. The HackRF One is an open-hardware, lowcost SDR that operates from 1 MHz to 6 GHz in half-duplex mode [1]. In this mode, it either receives or transmits at any one time. When the PortaPack inter",,,,,DUZELT,YUKSEK,"Soru veri biçimini soruyor; yanıt belirli dosya biçimini vermiyor, RTC/trimleme koşullarından söz ediyor.",,0.83,"of this device family. This work establishes, at source-code level, where forensically relevant data reside on the microSD card of a device running Mayhem v2.0.1, in which format, when they are written and how they should be interpreted. The methodology is applied to an SD card image that the author acquired from their own device after four months of real use, and every inference is drawn from the image alone. The workflow covers timestamp rules that depend on the real-time clock (RTC) battery state, traces of on-device trimming and text editin",DUZELT,YUKSEK,"Soru veri biçimini soruyor; yanıt belirli dosya biçimini vermiyor, RTC/trimleme koşullarından söz ediyor.",,0.83,"of this device family. This work establishes, at source-code level, where forensically relevant data reside on the microSD card of a device running Mayhem v2.0.1, in which format, when they are written and how they should be interpreted. The methodology is applied to an SD card image that the author acquired from their own device after four months of real use, and every inference is drawn from the image alone. The workflow covers timestamp rules that depend on the real-time clock (RTC) battery state, traces of on-device trimming and text editin"
70,batch_new500_0056,train,Digital Forensics,CyberSecurity.txt,What are the three main components of the CIA Triad in cybersecurity?,"The three main components of the CIA Triad in cybersecurity are Confidentiality, Integrity, and Availability.","(Autonomous Institution – UGC, Govt. of Recognized under 2(f) and 12 (B) of UGC ACT 1956 (Affiliated to JNTUH, Hyderabad, Approved by AICTE - Accredited by NBA & NAAC – ‘A’ Grade - ISO 9001:2015 Maisammaguda, Dhulapally (Post Via. Hakimpet), Secunderabad – 500100, Telangana State, India (Professional Elective 2) • To understand various types of cyber-attacks and cyber-crimes • To learn threats and risks within context of the cyber security • To have an overview of the cyber laws & concepts of cyber forensics • To study the defensive techniques against these attacks Introduction to Cyber Security: Basic Cyber Security Concepts, layers of security, Vulnerability, threat, Harmful acts, Internet Governance – Challenges and Constraints, Computer Criminals, CIA Triad, Assets and Threat, motive of attackers, active attacks, passive attacks, Software attacks, hardware attacks, Cyber Threats-Cyber Warfare, Cyber Crime, Cyber terrorism, Cyber Espionage, etc., Comprehensive Cyber Security Policy. Cyberspace and the Law & Cyber Forensics: Introduction, Cyber Security Regulations, Roles of International Law. The INDIAN Cyberspace, National Cyber Security Policy. Introduction, Historical background of Cyber forensics, Digital Forensics Science, The Need for Computer Forensics, Cyber Forensics and Digital evidence, Forensics Analysis of Email, Digital Forensics Lifecycle, Forensics Investigation, Challenges in Computer Cybercrime: Mobile and Wireless Devices: Introduction, Proliferation of Mobile and Wireless Devices, Trends in Mobility, Credit card Frauds in Mobile and Wireless Computing Era, Security Challenges Posed by Mobile Devices, Registry Settings for Mobile Devices, Authentication service Security, Attacks on Mobile/Cell Phones, Organizational security Policies and Measures in Mobile Computing Era, Laptops. Cyber Security: Organizational Implications: Introduction, cost of cybercrimes and IPR issues, web threats for organizations, security and privacy implications, social",,,,,YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,CIA üçlüsü genel güvenlik kavramı; kaynak pasajı da üç öğeyi açıkça sıralamıyor.,System Security,0.22,"Vulnerability, threat, Harmful acts, Internet Governance – Challenges and Constraints, Computer Criminals, CIA Triad, Assets and Threat, motive of attackers, active attacks, passive attacks, Software attacks, hardware attacks, Cyber Threats-Cyber Warfare, Cyber Crime, Cyber terrorism, Cyber Espionage, etc., Comprehensive Cyber Security Policy. Cyberspace and the Law & Cyber Forensics: Introduction, Cyber Security Regulations, Roles of International Law. The INDIAN Cyberspace, National Cyber Security Policy. Introduction, Historical background o",YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,CIA üçlüsü genel güvenlik kavramı; kaynak pasajı da üç öğeyi açıkça sıralamıyor.,System Security,0.22,"Vulnerability, threat, Harmful acts, Internet Governance – Challenges and Constraints, Computer Criminals, CIA Triad, Assets and Threat, motive of attackers, active attacks, passive attacks, Software attacks, hardware attacks, Cyber Threats-Cyber Warfare, Cyber Crime, Cyber terrorism, Cyber Espionage, etc., Comprehensive Cyber Security Policy. Cyberspace and the Law & Cyber Forensics: Introduction, Cyber Security Regulations, Roles of International Law. The INDIAN Cyberspace, National Cyber Security Policy. Introduction, Historical background o"
71,batch_new500_0271,val,Digital Forensics,sec_2508.01054v1_Autonomous_Penetration_Testing__Sol.txt,What was the performance rate of GPT-4o in the CTF challenges?,GPT-4o's success rate in the CTF challenges was 80%.,"This caused extreme difficulty for GPT-4o, which did not attempt the correct solution and entered navigation commands in a loop. However, the Python program also struggled to confer information related to this specific challenge to the LLM through its prompts due to its uniqueness, which likely caused less information about the challenge to be sent to GPTC. Performance Review GPT-4o’s success rate, 80%, is significant, especially knowing that the successful completions largely involved advanced Linux filesystem navigation, decrypting data, and utilizing common command-line programs (see Table II). Additionally, when the model did succeed in these challenges, it did so very quickly. The LLM generally excelled at general-scale task recognition, though it occasionally faltered when given specifics it struggled to navigate. Reading file contents Listing files/directories Locating files with specific attributes Filtering output text Separating individual lines in files Identifying file type Output file in specific order Locate unique line in file Extracting printable text from binaries Decoding encoded data Accessing next bandit level in sequence Accessing remote daemons Accessing encrypted connection Sending information over connections Decrypting data via transformation Finding differences in files The results clearly show that GPT-4o’s strongest capability for penetration testing is navigation of the Linux filesystem using bash commands, a strength that rarely faltered while completing the CTF challenges. In addition, it admirably performed data decryption and bash scripting tasks, and recognized how to use simple bespoke utilities created for the Bandit CTF. Further, it excelled in any tasks that required only a single command to obtain the flag (Table III). In contrast, GPT-4o struggled in remembering the directory in which it should be working, as well as with more advanced networking tasks. It also struggled when it needed to create files in the remote Bandit CTF ",,,,,YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,CTF çözme performansı ve test ortamı.,Penetration Testing,1.00,"its prompts due to its uniqueness, which likely caused less information about the challenge to be sent to GPTC. Performance Review GPT-4o’s success rate, 80%, is significant, especially knowing that the successful completions largely involved advanced Linux filesystem navigation, decrypting data, and utilizing common command-line programs (see Table II). Additionally, when the model did succeed in these challenges, it did so very quickly. The LLM generally excelled at general-scale task recognition, though it occasionally faltered when given sp",YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,CTF çözme performansı ve test ortamı.,Penetration Testing,1.00,"its prompts due to its uniqueness, which likely caused less information about the challenge to be sent to GPTC. Performance Review GPT-4o’s success rate, 80%, is significant, especially knowing that the successful completions largely involved advanced Linux filesystem navigation, decrypting data, and utilizing common command-line programs (see Table II). Additionally, when the model did succeed in these challenges, it did so very quickly. The LLM generally excelled at general-scale task recognition, though it occasionally faltered when given sp"
72,batch500_0161,val,Digital Forensics,Provenance and Authorization in Autobiographical Language Mo.txt,What does a request for a private discussion indicate about the sensitivity of the topic?,A request for a private discussion records a preference and may support a disclosure restriction if separately authorized by the owner. It does not supply blanket permission to expose the sensitive topic.,"Similarly, the report that C knew a travel plan leaves the source of that knowledge unspecified and does not establish a surveillance mechanism. The report that P sat nearby can coexist with U’s feeling of reduced distance without establishing that P recognized or yielded to The same distinction applies to assurances and disclosure. Statements attributed to L remain quotations rather than verified guarantees of security or confidentiality. A request for private discussion records a preference and may support a disclosure restriction if separately authorized by the owner. It does not supply blanket permission to expose the sensitive topic. These are boundaries on inference from the records, not findings about the participants. Derivation does not create an independent source Records form a directed acyclic graph through parent identifiers. For a record r, define its primitive lineage recursively: Copying a claim through five summaries therefore need not create five primitive sources. This is an accounting invariant, not a probabilistic independence test: distinct roots can remain correlated, and absent parent links can conceal dependence. Parent completeness is a trust assumption whenever this lineage is used for enforcement. Attesting that an assistant produced a sentence may be new evidence about the assistant’s output, but is not new independent evidence for the sentence’s account of another person’s intentions. Threat Model and Authenticated Authorization Principals, adversary, and trusted components The owner U controls a policy channel. An archival attester A labels and records incoming material. A memory service stores records and derived summaries. A decision engine reads only an admitted view. The model, retrieval service, external writers, and generated summaries can be faulty or adversarial. In particular, an adversary may submit arbitrary narrative payloads, replay old material, remove attribution in a proposed summary, or suggest that a quotation should ",,,,,ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,"Özel görüşme tercihi ve ifşa yetkisi, dosya adli analizinin teknik sorusu değil.",,1.00,"than verified guarantees of security or confidentiality. A request for private discussion records a preference and may support a disclosure restriction if separately authorized by the owner. It does not supply blanket permission to expose the sensitive topic. These are boundaries on inference from the records, not findings about the participants. Derivation does not create an independent source Records form a directed acyclic graph through parent identifiers. For a record r, define its primitive lineage recursively: Copying a claim through five",ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,"Özel görüşme tercihi ve ifşa yetkisi, dosya adli analizinin teknik sorusu değil.",,1.00,"than verified guarantees of security or confidentiality. A request for private discussion records a preference and may support a disclosure restriction if separately authorized by the owner. It does not supply blanket permission to expose the sensitive topic. These are boundaries on inference from the records, not findings about the participants. Derivation does not create an independent source Records form a directed acyclic graph through parent identifiers. For a record r, define its primitive lineage recursively: Copying a claim through five"
73,batch1000_0579,val,Digital Forensics,NIST_SP800_61r2_Olay_Mudahale.txt,What specific types of identifying information should be included in a detailed log for evidence collection?,"Specific types of identifying information that should be included in a detailed log for evidence collection include the location, serial number, model number, hostname, media access control (MAC) addresses, and IP addresses of a computer.","A detailed log should be kept for all evidence, including the following:  Identifying information (e.g., the location, serial number, model number, hostname, media access control (MAC) addresses, and IP addresses of a computer)  Name, title, and phone number of each individual who collected or handled the evidence during the  Time and date (including time zone) of each occurrence of evidence handling  Locations where the evidence was stored. Collecting evidence from computing resources presents some challenges. It is generally desirable to acquire evidence from a system of interest as soon as one suspects that an incident may have occurred. Many incidents cause a dynamic chain of events to occur; an initial system snapshot may do more good in identifying the problem and its source than most other actions that can be taken at this stage. From an evidentiary standpoint, it is much better to get a snapshot of the system as-is rather than doing so after incident handlers, system administrators, and others have inadvertently altered the state of the machine during the investigation. Users and system administrators should be made aware of the steps that they should take to preserve evidence. See NIST SP 800-86, Guide to Integrating Forensic Techniques into Incident Response, for additional information on preserving evidence. NIST SP 800-86, Guide to Integrating Forensic Techniques into Incident Response, provides detailed information on establishing a forensic capability. It focuses on forensic techniques for PCs, but much of the material is applicable to other Evidence gathering and handling is not typically performed for every incident that occurs—for example, most malware incidents do not merit evidence acquisition. In many organizations, digital forensics is not needed for most incidents. Searching and Seizing Computers and Obtaining Electronic Evidence in Criminal Investigations, from the Computer Crime and Intellectual Property Section (CCIPS) of the Department ",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.76,"A detailed log should be kept for all evidence, including the following:  Identifying information (e.g., the location, serial number, model number, hostname, media access control (MAC) addresses, and IP addresses of a computer)  Name, title, and phone number of each individual who collected or handled the evidence during the  Time and date (including time zone) of each occurrence of evidence handling  Locations where the evidence was stored. Collecting evidence from computing resources presents some challenges. It is generally desirable to ",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.76,"A detailed log should be kept for all evidence, including the following:  Identifying information (e.g., the location, serial number, model number, hostname, media access control (MAC) addresses, and IP addresses of a computer)  Name, title, and phone number of each individual who collected or handled the evidence during the  Time and date (including time zone) of each occurrence of evidence handling  Locations where the evidence was stored. Collecting evidence from computing resources presents some challenges. It is generally desirable to "
