75,kaggle1000_0931,test,Digital Forensics,"Hacking For Dummies, 3rd Edition.txt",What text strings should be looked for when using a basic text search tool like FileLocator Pro to detect sensitive information?,"When using a basic text search tool like FileLocator Pro, look for the following text strings: DOB (for dates of birth), SSN (for Social Security numbers), License (for driver’s license information), and Credit (for credit card numbers).","This will give you a better view of regular users who have unauthorized access to sensitive files and shares that you thought were otherwise secure. When using a basic text search tool, such as FileLocator Pro, look for the following text strings: ✓ DOB (for dates of birth) ✓ SSN (for Social Security numbers) ✓ License (for driver’s license information) ✓ Credit (for credit card numbers) Don’t forget about your mobile devices when seeking sensitive, unprotected information. Everything from laptops to USB drives to external hard drives is fair game to the bad guys. A costly data breach is as close as a misplaced or The possibilities for information exposure are endless; just start with the basics and only peek into nonbinary files that you know will have text in them. Limiting your search to these text-based files will save you a ton of Note the files found in different locations on the server. Part V: Hacking Applications To speed the process, you can use Identity Finder, a tool designed for the very purpose of scanning storage devices for sensitive, personally identifiChapter 15: Databases and Storage Systems Identity Finder has an Enterprise edition that can be used to search network systems and even databases for sensitive information. For a second round of testing, you could perform your searches logged in as administrator. You’re likely to find a lot of sensitive information scattered about. It seems worthless at first; however, this can highlight sensitive information stored in places it shouldn’t be or that the network administrator shouldn’t have access to. Testing is highly dependent on timing, searching for the right keywords, and looking at the right systems on the network. You likely won’t root out every single bit of sensitive information, but this will show you where certain problems are to help justify the need for stronger access controls and better IT and security management processes. Best Practices for Minimizing Storage Security Risks Like databa",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"files and shares that you thought were otherwise secure. When using a basic text search tool, such as FileLocator Pro, look for the following text strings: ✓ DOB (for dates of birth) ✓ SSN (for Social Security numbers) ✓ License (for driver’s license information) ✓ Credit (for credit card numbers) Don’t forget about your mobile devices when seeking sensitive, unprotected information. Everything from laptops to USB drives to external hard drives is fair game to the bad guys. A costly data breach is as close as a misplaced or The possibilities fo",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"files and shares that you thought were otherwise secure. When using a basic text search tool, such as FileLocator Pro, look for the following text strings: ✓ DOB (for dates of birth) ✓ SSN (for Social Security numbers) ✓ License (for driver’s license information) ✓ Credit (for credit card numbers) Don’t forget about your mobile devices when seeking sensitive, unprotected information. Everything from laptops to USB drives to external hard drives is fair game to the bad guys. A costly data breach is as close as a misplaced or The possibilities fo"
76,batch500_0364,train,Incident Response,Bridging the Closed-World Gap_ Interpretable Anomaly Detecti.txt,What are the 9 discriminative features extracted from the BETH dataset?,"The 9 discriminative features extracted from the BETH dataset are:
- Process Features: processId, parentProcessId
- User Context: userId, mountNamespace
- System Call Context: eventId, argsNum, returnValue
- Categorical Features: processName, hostName","We propose a three-stage hybrid fuzzy ensemble architecture that combines supervised detection of known suspicious behaviors with unsupervised identification of out-of-distribution (OoD) malicious events. The system consists of a gradient-optimized Takagi-Sugeno-Kang (TSK) fuzzy classifier for supervised detection and a Fuzzy C-Means (FCM) clustering module for unsupervised OoD detection. This dual-module approach addresses the fundamental challenge in botnet activation detection: identifying novel attack patterns that were not present The BETH dataset [2] consists of kernel log traces captured from 23 honeypot networks during normal operation and botnet infection periods. Each system call event is represented by 14 raw features, including process identifiers, user contexts, and system call parameters. Following the BETH benchmark methodology, we extract 9 discriminative features for our detection system: – Process Features: processId, parentProcessId – User Context: userId, mountNamespace – System Call Context: eventId, argsNum, returnValue – Categorical Features: processName, hostName To handle the heterogeneous nature of system call features, we apply domainspecific transformations: 1. Binary Encoding: Following security best practices, process and user identifiers are binarized based on privilege levels: processId′ = 1[processId ∈{0, 1, 2}] userId′ = 1[userId < 1000] where system-critical processes (PIDs 0-2) and system users (UID < 1000) 2. Categorical Encoding: Process and host names are encoded using label encoding to preserve ordinal relationships discovered during training. 3. Return Value Mapping: System call return values are mapped to three semantic categories: if data returned ( > 0) 4. Standardization: All features are normalized using z-score standardization using the training data and applied consistently to validation and test data. Fuzzy Ensemble Anomaly Detection Stage 1: Supervised TSK Fuzzy Classifier – The first detection module is a gradient-o",,,,,KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,"BETH özellikleri özel veri kümesi ayrıntısı, olay müdahalesi modeliyle bağlamlı tutulmalı.",,0.95,"novel attack patterns that were not present The BETH dataset [2] consists of kernel log traces captured from 23 honeypot networks during normal operation and botnet infection periods. Each system call event is represented by 14 raw features, including process identifiers, user contexts, and system call parameters. Following the BETH benchmark methodology, we extract 9 discriminative features for our detection system: – Process Features: processId, parentProcessId – User Context: userId, mountNamespace – System Call Context: eventId, argsNum, re",KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,"BETH özellikleri özel veri kümesi ayrıntısı, olay müdahalesi modeliyle bağlamlı tutulmalı.",,0.95,"novel attack patterns that were not present The BETH dataset [2] consists of kernel log traces captured from 23 honeypot networks during normal operation and botnet infection periods. Each system call event is represented by 14 raw features, including process identifiers, user contexts, and system call parameters. Following the BETH benchmark methodology, we extract 9 discriminative features for our detection system: – Process Features: processId, parentProcessId – User Context: userId, mountNamespace – System Call Context: eventId, argsNum, re"
77,kaggle1000_0788,train,Incident Response,NIST.SP.800-209.txt,What is the consequence of not having solid audit logs in place?,"Without solid audit logs, an attack may go unnoticed indefinitely, and the actual damages done may be irreversible.","Even if the victims know that their systems have been compromised, without protected and complete logging records, they are blind to the details of the attack and to subsequent actions taken by the attackers. Without solid audit logs, an attack may go unnoticed indefinitely, and the actual damages done may be irreversible. Sometimes, logging records are the only evidence of a successful attack. Many organizations keep audit records for compliance purposes, but attackers rely on the fact that such organizations rarely look at the audit logs, and they do not know that their systems have been compromised. Because of poor or nonexistent log analysis processes, attackers sometimes control victim machines for months or years without anyone in the target organization knowing, even though evidence of the attack can be obtained in unexamined log files. Based on the criticality of event log data for attack detection and forensic investigation, the following are the security recommendations for implementing audit logging capabilities. AL-SS-R1 – Audit logging should be enabled on all storage infrastructure components using reliable delivery and secure protocols. AL-SS-R2 – Reliable, external time synchronization: A Network Time Protocol (NTP) service is critical for time synchronization. If the NTP service is disabled, dependent systems may suffer from inaccurate timestamps on messages, events and alerts, inconsistent time across different devices, and subsequent failure to perform log analysis, correlation, anomaly detection or forensics. Establishing and using a common, accurate time source across the environment helps ensure that event records from different sources can be correlated. The following are recommendations regarding the deployment and integration of NTP with storage-related devices: (a) The NTP service should be enabled on all devices (including log servers and storage (b) All devices should be configured to synchronize time with a time source server, such as (c",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"records, they are blind to the details of the attack and to subsequent actions taken by the attackers. Without solid audit logs, an attack may go unnoticed indefinitely, and the actual damages done may be irreversible. Sometimes, logging records are the only evidence of a successful attack. Many organizations keep audit records for compliance purposes, but attackers rely on the fact that such organizations rarely look at the audit logs, and they do not know that their systems have been compromised. Because of poor or nonexistent log analysis pr",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"records, they are blind to the details of the attack and to subsequent actions taken by the attackers. Without solid audit logs, an attack may go unnoticed indefinitely, and the actual damages done may be irreversible. Sometimes, logging records are the only evidence of a successful attack. Many organizations keep audit records for compliance purposes, but attackers rely on the fact that such organizations rarely look at the audit logs, and they do not know that their systems have been compromised. Because of poor or nonexistent log analysis pr"
