80,extra_20261108_4849,train,Incident Response,Do Flow-Generation Tools Matter_ Unsupervised Characterizati.txt,"What is the Silhouette score for determining the quality of clusters, and how does it differ from the Adjusted Rand Index (ARI) in terms of their calculation and interpretation?","The Silhouette score is calculated using the formula provided, which involves comparing the mean intra-cluster distance \(a\) and the mean nearest-cluster distance \(b\) for each sample. The Silhouette score ranges from -1 to 1, where a higher score indicates more coherent clusters. It is an internal evaluation metric that does not require class labels. In contrast, the Adjusted Rand Index (ARI) compares the similarity of the clusters generated by the algorithm to the original labeled classes of the dataset. ARI's value ranges from -1 to 1, with a higher value indicating more similarity between the clusters. A value of 1 indicates perfect agreement, while negative values may occur when the agreement is worse than expected by chance.","In our PCA-based settings, the feature space was reduced to 10, 20, or 30 principal components, depending on the number of valid features available after preprocessing. This allowed us to evaluate whether clustering performance improves when the algorithms operate on a compact representation that retains the main information while reducing noise, redundancy, and dimensionality-related effects. When a dataset did not contain enough valid features to support a given PCA configuration, that configuration was omitted. 4.6. Evaluation Metrics We use 2 metrics to evaluate the performance of our algorithms, namely the Silhouette score and the Adjusted Rand Index (ARI). First, using the Silhouette score helps determine which setting of a given algorithm generates the most coherent clusters. This metric provides an internal evaluation of cluster quality without requiring class labels. A Silhouette score can fall in the range between -1 and 1; the higher the score, the more coherent the cluster. The lower bound of -1 indicates that the sample is in the wrong cluster, while the upper bound of 1 means that it is in the correct one. The Silhouette score is the mean of all the Silhouette coefficients in the dataset. For each sample, the coefficient is Rashed et al.: Preprint submitted to Elsevier Do Flow-Generation Tools Matter? calculated by considering, both the mean intra-cluster distance a and the mean nearest-cluster distance b. The following equation is used [20]: On the other hand, we use ARI to compare the similarity of the clusters generated by the algorithm – with a given set of parameters – to the original labeled classes of the dataset. This method is well established in the literature and has been used previously in the context of malware classification [53]. ARI’s value falls between between -1 and 1. A higher value of ARI means more similarity between the clusters. A value of 1 indicates perfect agreement, values close to 0 indicate agreement similar to random labeling, and negative values may occur when the agreement is worse than expected by chance. The following equation represents ARI [54]: Index −Expected Value Maximum Value of Index −Expected Value , where Index is what is actually observed, Expected Value what would be obtained purely by chance and Maximum Value of Index is the best possible score. In this section, we highlight the results of our clustering experiments on each of the datasets of the generated flows by our 3 tools. Tables 3, 5 and 4 show the results of our experiments. To avoid any random initialization in our results, for each K-value (2-16), we ran our script 10 times and averaged the output. We observe that most of the top Silhouette and ARI values were obtained with Zeek with the PCA10 setting. The exceptions in which Zeek PCA 10 was not the top performer for the Silhouette score were: a) NTLFlowLyzer for the GothX (also with PCA10) and b) Zeek with PCA20 for the MQTTset.",,,,,YANL