87,kaggle1000_0935,val,Incident Response,An Agentic ENF Framework for Forensic Video Authentication A.txt,What is the number of parameters in the unified ENFDetector model?,"≈ 157,600","4. Synthetic-injection training. Grid ENF is fitted with an OrnsteinUhlenbeck model and injected into hum-free carriers to train one dual-channel Six classes cover the main ways an ENF can be present, absent or inconsistent: a genuine drifting line; an absent line; a flat, non-drifting line; a phase discontinuity; a cross-regional line at the wrong nominal frequency; and a splice of two segments. Classes are sampled uniformly at training time, so every example is freshly synthesised and the stream is balanced by construction. The genuine class is split evenly between real anchor windows from held-in recordings and synthetic carriers with an injected genuine drift; the governing rule is that real recordings appear only as the genuine class, so every fake is synthetic and separation is never learned from Fig. 5. The genuine ENF signature and the five synthetic manipulation classes used for injection training. Both channels use the same network. Two convolutional branches read the ENF spectrogram, a fully connected branch reads summary statistics of the instantaneous-frequency trace (peak prominence, drift magnitude and variability), and the branches are fused for a per-class decision. Adaptive pooling makes the network invariant to input size, so the audio and optical features, which differ in length, feed the same architecture unchanged. The model is deliberately small, ≈ 157,600 parameters, which suits the limited training signal and keeps inference cheap enough to run inside the orchestrator Fig. 6. The unified ENFDetector. One shared second-harmonic extractor feeds a 1-D convolutional branch and a trace-statistics branch, fused into a six-class head, and serves both the audio and optical channels. D. Reference-matching authentication Presence of an ENF is weak evidence, because generated video carries an ENF-band line too; consistency with an independent grid reference is strong evidence, because a generator does not measure a live grid. The authentication channel",,,,,YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,ENFDetector ses/video kayıt doğrulama modeli; olay müdahalesinden çok dijital adli bilişimle ilgili.,Digital Forensics,0.00,"the audio and optical features, which differ in length, feed the same architecture unchanged. The model is deliberately small, ≈ 157,600 parameters, which suits the limited training signal and keeps inference cheap enough to run inside the orchestrator Fig. 6. The unified ENFDetector. One shared second-harmonic extractor feeds a 1-D convolutional branch and a trace-statistics branch, fused into a six-class head, and serves both the audio and optical channels. D. Reference-matching authentication Presence of an ENF is weak evidence, because gene",YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,ENFDetector ses/video kayıt doğrulama modeli; olay müdahalesinden çok dijital adli bilişimle ilgili.,Digital Forensics,0.00,"the audio and optical features, which differ in length, feed the same architecture unchanged. The model is deliberately small, ≈ 157,600 parameters, which suits the limited training signal and keeps inference cheap enough to run inside the orchestrator Fig. 6. The unified ENFDetector. One shared second-harmonic extractor feeds a 1-D convolutional branch and a trace-statistics branch, fused into a six-class head, and serves both the audio and optical channels. D. Reference-matching authentication Presence of an ENF is weak evidence, because gene"
88,batch_new500_0019,val,Incident Response,Domain Name System-Based Electronic Mail Security.txt,What does the project support in the Access Control (PR.AC) subcategory regarding identities and credentials?,"The project supports identities and credentials being managed for authorized devices and users, specifically focusing on managing the credentials associated with the addresses from which email purportedly originates and the integrity of the user identities associated with the email.","They provide a set of results that, while not exhaustive, help support achievement of the outcomes in each Category. Examples of Subcategories include “External information systems are catalogued,” “Data-at-rest is protected,” and “Notifications from detection systems are investigated.” Informative References are specific sections of standards, guidelines, and practices common among critical infrastructure sectors that illustrate a method to achieve the outcomes associated with each Subcategory. The Informative References presented in the Framework Core are illustrative and not exhaustive. They are based upon cross-sector guidance most frequently referenced during the Framework development process. This project supported the Cybersecurity Framework’s Protect, Detect, and Respond Functions. Applicability to specific Functions, Categories, and Subcategories is described in the following The Protect Function develops and implements the appropriate safeguards needed to ensure delivery of critical infrastructure services. This Function supports the ability to limit or contain the impact of a potential cybersecurity event. Examples of outcome Categories within this Function addressed by the project include: Access Control, Data Security, and Protective Technology. 1. Access Control (PR.AC) NIST SP 1800-6B: Domain Name System-Based Electronic Mail Security The PR.AC-1 subcategory under Access Control supports identities and credentials being managed for authorized devices and users. The security platform resulting from the project supports effective management of the credentials associated with the addresses from which email purportedly originates and the integrity of the user identities associated with the email. The original design of the DNS did not include security; instead, it was designed to be a scalable distributed system. DNSSEC and DANE attempt to add security, while maintaining backward compatibility with the existing DNS. DNSSEC was designed to protect applicat",,,,,YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,PR.AC kimlik ve kimlik bilgisi yönetimi; olay müdahalesinden çok Authentication.,Authentication,0.83,"(PR.AC) NIST SP 1800-6B: Domain Name System-Based Electronic Mail Security The PR.AC-1 subcategory under Access Control supports identities and credentials being managed for authorized devices and users. The security platform resulting from the project supports effective management of the credentials associated with the addresses from which email purportedly originates and the integrity of the user identities associated with the email. The original design of the DNS did not include security; instead, it was designed to be a scalable distributed",YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,PR.AC kimlik ve kimlik bilgisi yönetimi; olay müdahalesinden çok Authentication.,Authentication,0.83,"(PR.AC) NIST SP 1800-6B: Domain Name System-Based Electronic Mail Security The PR.AC-1 subcategory under Access Control supports identities and credentials being managed for authorized devices and users. The security platform resulting from the project supports effective management of the credentials associated with the addresses from which email purportedly originates and the integrity of the user identities associated with the email. The original design of the DNS did not include security; instead, it was designed to be a scalable distributed"
89,batch500_0375,test,Incident Response,StateFence_ Task-State Protection Against Evidence-Chain Con.txt,What is the improvement in Task Success (TS) percentage when using StateFence compared to public baselines?,"StateFence achieves an 89.06% end-to-end Task Success (TS) percentage, which is an improvement of 31.65 percentage points over the best public baseline.","Evidence rewriting provides additional benefit only when coupled with this state-aware policy. End-to-End Evidence-Chain Evaluation after IOC extraction, severity assessment, and response recommendation are linked into one workflow. Each task invocation returns structured severity, indicator, action, citation, and memory-write fields in one completion; actions are recommenStructured extraction TS (%) ↑AS (%) ↓Rec. (%) ↑PD (%) ↓ Fence on public SOC tasks. dations from a fixed tool vocabulary. StateFence reaches 89.06% end-to-end TS with 96.88% IOC recall, compared with 57.81% TS and 78.13% recall without defense. Best public and structured extraction improve on raw execution, but their 70.31% and 65.63% TS show that generic filtering or structuring does not reliably preserve all three protected states. full_firewall is worst at 46.88% TS and 62.50% recall: this is consistent with the indicatorloss pattern in the IOC ablation. The joint scores support task-matched protection across the linked workflow, but do not isolate the causal contribution of IOC loss to later errors. Public-SOC Validation idence. StateFence achieves the strongest overall performance, with 91.41% task success and 8.59% attack success. It also keeps PD below 5%, whereas all public baselines remain above 12% PD. DF obtains the highest IOC recall at 94.5%, but StateFence remains close at 94% recall. Appendix D reports a cross-source transfer experiment that remaps ASB, RedCode, SafeArena, and AgentHarm attacks into SOC evidence channels to test whether StateFence routing remains effective Propagation and Failure-Mode Analysis We analyse the 264 trace-labelled task failures observed across 1,024 attacked task–model evaluations in the formal benchmark, spanning four tasks, four models, and 64 cases per task–model setting. Triage contributes the largest share of failures, with 128 cases (48.48%), compared with 58 (21.97%) for IOC extraction, 42 (15.91%) for summarisation, and 36 (13.64%) for response. ",,,,,YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,StateFence SOC kanıt zinciri / gösterge çıkarımı ve yanıt iş akışı.,Incident Response,0.64,"recall without defense. Best public and structured extraction improve on raw execution, but their 70.31% and 65.63% TS show that generic filtering or structuring does not reliably preserve all three protected states. full_firewall is worst at 46.88% TS and 62.50% recall: this is consistent with the indicatorloss pattern in the IOC ablation. The joint scores support task-matched protection across the linked workflow, but do not isolate the causal contribution of IOC loss to later errors. Public-SOC Validation idence. StateFence achieves the stro",YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,StateFence SOC kanıt zinciri / gösterge çıkarımı ve yanıt iş akışı.,Incident Response,0.64,"recall without defense. Best public and structured extraction improve on raw execution, but their 70.31% and 65.63% TS show that generic filtering or structuring does not reliably preserve all three protected states. full_firewall is worst at 46.88% TS and 62.50% recall: this is consistent with the indicatorloss pattern in the IOC ablation. The joint scores support task-matched protection across the linked workflow, but do not isolate the causal contribution of IOC loss to later errors. Public-SOC Validation idence. StateFence achieves the stro"
90,batch_new500_0185,test,Incident Response,From Trust Scores to Driver Decisions_ An Abstaining Evident.txt,What is the maximum change in the decision margin when a single report is received according to the analytical properties derived in Section 5?,A single report changes the decision margin by at most its weight.,"CREST requires only a scalar trust value per reporter and is instantiated here on the evidential trust model IPEK [12], whose consensus and revocation steps are updated to support the decision layer. The contributions of this paper are as follows. • An abstaining evidential decision framework for event validation on the vehicle, with explicit policies for vehicles that remain unsure, so that the trade-off between missed hazards and unnecessary detours can be chosen by the application (Section 4). • Analytical properties that link the trust layer to the robustness of the decision: a single report changes the decision margin by at most its weight, so that reversing a correct decision requires a coalition whose trust-bounded weights reach twice the abstention threshold; the conditions under which an honest vehicle abstains; and the breakdown point of the credibility-weighted consensus (Section 5). • Two updates of the trust substrate motivated by the decision layer: a credibility-weighted median consensus that keeps the attackers’ trust low under local attacker majorities, and a time-based revocation rule whose delay does not depend on the update tempo (Section 4.2). • An evaluation of complete systems against HTEMD [15] and TCEMD [14], and of decision rules including AR-NDA [17] on a common trust substrate, under fourteen attacks, among them position spoofing and trust-gated camouflage, at two traffic densities and four decision geometries. The protocol was registered before the runs, all unspecified parameters of all systems were tuned with the same procedure, and the results are reported at the trust, decision and application levels (Sections 6 and 7). The remainder of the paper is organised as follows. Section 2 reviews trust management and event decision schemes. Section 3 defines the system, the decision geometry and the threat model. Section 4 presents CREST, and Section 5 derives its analytical properties. Section 6 describes the evaluation methodology, and Sec",,,,,KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,Karar marjı ve tek rapor ağırlığı özel araştırma modeline ait; genel olay müdahalesi kuralı değil.,,1.00,"so that the trade-off between missed hazards and unnecessary detours can be chosen by the application (Section 4). • Analytical properties that link the trust layer to the robustness of the decision: a single report changes the decision margin by at most its weight, so that reversing a correct decision requires a coalition whose trust-bounded weights reach twice the abstention threshold; the conditions under which an honest vehicle abstains; and the breakdown point of the credibility-weighted consensus (Section 5). • Two updates of the trust su",KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,Karar marjı ve tek rapor ağırlığı özel araştırma modeline ait; genel olay müdahalesi kuralı değil.,,1.00,"so that the trade-off between missed hazards and unnecessary detours can be chosen by the application (Section 4). • Analytical properties that link the trust layer to the robustness of the decision: a single report changes the decision margin by at most its weight, so that reversing a correct decision requires a coalition whose trust-bounded weights reach twice the abstention threshold; the conditions under which an honest vehicle abstains; and the breakdown point of the credibility-weighted consensus (Section 5). • Two updates of the trust su"
91,extra_20261108_2392,train,Malware,PhishGen_ Understanding LLM Capabilities in Automated Phishi.txt,What metric does the study use to quantify how often a model produces any output?,"The safeguard bypass rate is used to quantify how often a model produces any output. This metric evaluates whether some LLMs refuse to generate output outright, such as ""I'm sorry, but I can't assist with that request.""","Screenshot-based Characterization. Given the diversity of LLM-generated pages, no single metric captures their resemblance to legitimate counterparts. We render each page with Playwright and evaluate it along seven complementary axes: The (1) safeguard bypass rate quantifies how often a model produces any output, as some LLMs refuse outright (e.g., “I’m sorry, but I can’t assist with that request.”). Among returned we use FastDup [26] clustering (threshold: 0.9), and report the fraction of non-failed outputs as the (2) valid website rate. Since visual quality ultimately rests on human perception, we measure (3) UWS (User-perceived Website Similarity) via a crowdsourced user study refining the Website Appearance Scale (WAS) of Roy et al. [8]. Participants rate each evaluated page against its legitimate counterpart shown side-by-side, following [28], without knowing whether it is legitimate, phishing, or LLM-generated. Because we assess perceived visual similarity rather than phishing detection, we use paired comparisons to directly measure visual fidelity. We recruit 61 Prolific participants under an IRB-approved protocol. A pilot of the original WAS rubric reveals that lay participants disregard its complex descriptions and fall back on intuitive judgments, so we replace those anchors with concise descriptors (1: “Not similar at all” through 5: “Nearly identical”) while preserving the 5-point Likert structure. We use GPT 5 to pre-categorize a large sample pool into similarity tiers and then uniformly draw 2-5 images per (model, brand) combination for balanced coverage across quality tiers. Beyond the Likert rating, we collect per-trial confidence, rationale tags, and an embedded attention check; filter out responses with confidence ≤2; and report inter-rater reliability (IRR) via Krippendorff’s α. Each sample receives at least two independent ratings, yielding 10-11 per (model, brand) cell, and UWS is the mean (full protocol and findings in Appendix B-B and Appendix A-D). We measure (4) homogeneity as the fraction of screenshots in the largest FastDup cluster (threshold: 0.96); higher values indicate lower diversity. For brand mimicry, (5) brand text matching extracts text via PaddleOCR [29] and fuzzy-matches it against the target brand string (≥0.9), (6) brand logo matching uses the Google Cloud Vision API [30] (confidence ≥0.7); both thresholds are empirically selected based on clear performance separation, and (7) Phishpedia detection rate measures the fraction of generated pages detected by Phishpedia [31] from screenshots (threshold: 0.83). We choose Phishpedia because it exposes the pure brand-matching signal from screenshots, capturing the brand-impersonation metric. HTML-based Characterization. Screenshots capture what users see, but HTML governs what is actually rendered and how the page behaves. We therefore complement the screenshotbased analysis with an HTML-centric one along four axes.",,,,,YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,Phishing web sayfaları üretiminde güvenlik önlemi aşma ölçütü; malware yerine web/phishing güvenliği.,Web Security,0.84,"Screenshot-based Characterization. Given the diversity of LLM-generated pages, no single metric captures their resemblance to legitimate counterparts. We render each page with Playwright and evaluate it along seven complementary axes: The (1) safeguard bypass rate quantifies how often a model produces any output, as some LLMs refuse outright (e.g., “I’m sorry, but I can’t assist with that request.”). Among returned we use FastDup [26] clustering (threshold: 0.9), and report the fraction of non-failed outputs as the (2) valid website rate. Since",YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,Phishing web sayfaları üretiminde güvenlik önlemi aşma ölçütü; malware yerine web/phishing güvenliği.,Web Security,0.84,"Screenshot-based Characterization. Given the diversity of LLM-generated pages, no single metric captures their resemblance to legitimate counterparts. We render each page with Playwright and evaluate it along seven complementary axes: The (1) safeguard bypass rate quantifies how often a model produces any output, as some LLMs refuse outright (e.g., “I’m sorry, but I can’t assist with that request.”). Among returned we use FastDup [26] clustering (threshold: 0.9), and report the fraction of non-failed outputs as the (2) valid website rate. Since"
92,batch1000_0884,train,Malware,siber_guvenlik_lise.txt,How long is recommended to perform the cleanup and restore the system to a secure state after detecting malicious software?,The recommendation is 15 minutes.,"Kötü amaçlı yazılım tespit edildikten sonra gereken temizleme ve sistemi tekrar güvenli hale getirme işlemleri yapılmalıdır. Bölüm 5: Kötü Amaçlı Yazılımlar Önerilen süre: 15 dakika Bu bölümde eğitmen tarafından öğrencilere bir antivirüs yazılımı (örn. Microsoft Defender Antivirus) tanıtılarak bazı temel işlemler gösterilecektir. Eğitmenin ekran paylaşımı yoluyla öğrencilere aşağıdaki işlemlerin yapılışını gösterebilir: o Özel tarama (dosya veya konum seçerek)  Korumayı güncelleme  Virüs ve tehdit koruması ayarlarını değiştirme  Denetimli klasör erişimini yönetme  Karantina yönetimi Önerilen süre: 40 dakika Bu etkinlikte öğrenciler hash değeri karşılaştırması ve metin içinde arama yoluyla temel statik analiz yapmayı öğreneceklerdir. Etkinlikte izlenecek adımlar için Ek 1’de sunulan doküman takip edilebilir. Linux için antivirüs yazılımları mevcut olsa da kullanımı pek yaygın değildir. Bu nedenle bu bölümde gösterimin Windows üzerinden yapılması önerilmektedir. Windows sistemlerinde yer alan Microsoft Defender Antivirus bu etkinlik için Bölüm 5: Kötü Amaçlı Yazılımlar Önerilen süre: 20 dakika Eğitmen öğrencilerinin oltalama (phishing) hakkında tartışmalarını sağlar. Bunun için öğrencilerine çeşitli sorular sorarak onların cevabını alır ve ardından oltalama ile ilgili detaylı açıklamalarını örnekler kullanarak yapar.  Size güvenilir gelmeyen e-postalar veya mesajlar alıyor musunuz?  Sizce bir e-postanın veya mesajın şüpheli olması ne demektir? Nasıl mesajlardan  Oltalama saldırısı diye bir şey duydunuz mu? Örnek verebilir misiniz?  Sosyal mühendislik kavramı size ne ifade ediyor? Eğitmen bu sorulara ek olarak başka sorular da sorabilir. Gelen cevaplarla birlikte öğrencilerden de yeni sorular ortaya çıkabilir. Bunlara benzer sorular çerçevesinde tartışma yönetilerek öğrencilerin fikirlerini beyan etmesi sağlanır. Tartışmadan sonra aşağıdaki temel bilgiler ve örnekler öğrencilere açıklanır. Sosyal mühendislik, insanları kandırarak saldırganın istediği yönde hare",,,,,ELE_ADAYI,KRITIK,"Kaynakta ""Önerilen süre: 15 dakika"" öğretim bölümünün ders süresidir; kötü amaçlı yazılım temizliği için 15 dakikalık operasyonel öneri değildir.",,0.00,"ek olarak başka sorular da sorabilir. Gelen cevaplarla birlikte öğrencilerden de yeni sorular ortaya çıkabilir. Bunlara benzer sorular çerçevesinde tartışma yönetilerek öğrencilerin fikirlerini beyan etmesi sağlanır. Tartışmadan sonra aşağıdaki temel bilgiler ve örnekler öğrencilere açıklanır. Sosyal mühendislik, insanları kandırarak saldırganın istediği yönde hare",ELE_ADAYI,KRITIK,"Kaynakta ""Önerilen süre: 15 dakika"" öğretim bölümünün ders süresidir; kötü amaçlı yazılım temizliği için 15 dakikalık operasyonel öneri değildir.",,0.00,"ek olarak başka sorular da sorabilir. Gelen cevaplarla birlikte öğrencilerden de yeni sorular ortaya çıkabilir. Bunlara benzer sorular çerçevesinde tartışma yönetilerek öğrencilerin fikirlerini beyan etmesi sağlanır. Tartışmadan sonra aşağıdaki temel bilgiler ve örnekler öğrencilere açıklanır. Sosyal mühendislik, insanları kandırarak saldırganın istediği yönde hare"
93,batch1000_0073,train,Malware,Measuring Duplication_ Leakage_ and Rendering Dependence in.txt,What percentage of AndroDex samples include obfuscated variants of their originals?,34.4% of AndroDex samples include obfuscated variants of their originals.,"Deep visual representation learning therefore does not appear necessary for most of the reported accuracies. group to one image removes 31.6% of Malimg, 58% of MaleVis, 39.4% of Virus-MNIST and 34.4% of AndroDex includes obfuscated variants of its originals, which an obfuscator has rewritten so that their code differs while their behavior is unchanged. Training on the originals and testing on the obfuscated variants, with zero exact-duplicate leakage between the two, reduces accuracy by 13.6 points (92.76 →79.17), but accuracy remains far above chance, so the byteplot contains some signal that is invariant to obfuscation. The accuracy that remains is inconsistent with the model relying only on recognizing a fixed, repeated byte pattern such as a packer stub or a shared header. Section 4 does not establish whether the image encoding is itself responsible for the inflation, because the benchmarks differ in corpus, labeling and rendering at once, so we separate the effect of the rendering from the other two in two ways. Comparing Representations on The experiment is a paired comparison of two representations of one corpus: we take a fixed set of samples, represent each sample both as a static feature vector and as a byteplot image, train a model on each representation over identical folds, and compare top-1 accuracy. Because the samples, the folds and the seeds are shared, any difference between the two is attributable to the representation and not to the corpus, the labels or the split. et al., 2021) is the one corpus on which this comparison is possible, since it provides raw binaries and EMBER-v2 (Anderson and Roth, 2018) feature vectors for the same samples, the standard static-feature baseline for PE malware. MOTIF’s family labels are also derived from published threat reports rather than from antivirus votes, and its samples are deduplicated by construction. We render exactly those fold CV over seeds 0–2 with identical folds in every row; n/m is not measured. Log",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.83,"to one image removes 31.6% of Malimg, 58% of MaleVis, 39.4% of Virus-MNIST and 34.4% of AndroDex includes obfuscated variants of its originals, which an obfuscator has rewritten so that their code differs while their behavior is unchanged. Training on the originals and testing on the obfuscated variants, with zero exact-duplicate leakage between the two, reduces accuracy by 13.6 points (92.76 →79.17), but accuracy remains far above chance, so the byteplot contains some signal that is invariant to obfuscation. The accuracy that remains is incons",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.83,"to one image removes 31.6% of Malimg, 58% of MaleVis, 39.4% of Virus-MNIST and 34.4% of AndroDex includes obfuscated variants of its originals, which an obfuscator has rewritten so that their code differs while their behavior is unchanged. Training on the originals and testing on the obfuscated variants, with zero exact-duplicate leakage between the two, reduces accuracy by 13.6 points (92.76 →79.17), but accuracy remains far above chance, so the byteplot contains some signal that is invariant to obfuscation. The accuracy that remains is incons"
94,batch1000_0150,train,Malware,malwaredatascience.txt,What does the violin plot in Listing 9-14 reveal about the distribution of file sizes for different malware types?,"The violin plot in Listing 9-14 reveals the distribution of file sizes for different malware types, with the x-axis representing the malware type and the y-axis showing the file size in bytes on a logarithmic scale. This visualization helps identify patterns such as ransomware being associated with larger file sizes compared to other malware types like bitcoin, trojan, and keylogger, which are often detected by fewer scanners.","Knowing these patterns could potentially help us to classify unknown files better (a larger file being more likely to be ransomware and less likely to be a worm), or teach us what file sizes we should focus on in a defensive tool targeted at a specific type of malware. Visualizing Malware Trends 173 Making the violin plot takes one plotting call, as shown in Listing 9-14. from matplotlib import pyplot malware = pandas.read_csv(""malware_data.csv"") u axis = seaborn.violinplot(x=malware['type'], y=malware['size']) v axis.set(xlabel=""Malware type"", ylabel=""File size in bytes (log base-10)"", title=""File Sizes by Malware Type"", yscale=""log"") Listing 9-14: Creating a violin plot In Listing 9-14, first we create the violin plot u. Next we tell seaborn to set the axis labels and title and to set the y-axis to log-scale v. Finally, we make the plot appear w. We can also make an analogous plot showing the Number of Detections by Malware Type Number of vendor detections of looking at file size on the y-axis, we’re looking at the number of positives each file received. The results show some interesting trends. For example, ransomware is almost always detected by more than 30 scanners. The bitcoin, trojan, and keylogger malware types, in contrast, are detected by less than 30 scanners a substantial portion of the time, meaning more of these types are slipping past the security industry’s defenses (folks who don’t have the scanners that detect these files installed are likely getting infected by these samples). Listing 9-15 shows how to create the plot shown in Figure 9-10. from matplotlib import pyplot malware = pandas.read_csv(""malware_data.csv"") axis = seaborn.violinplot(x=malware['type'], y=malware['positives']) axis.set(xlabel=""Malware type"", ylabel=""Number of vendor detections"", title=""Number of Detections by Malware Type"") Listing 9-15: Visualizing antivirus detections per malware type The only differences in this code and the previous are that we pass the violinplot functi",,,,,DUZELT,YUKSEK,Violin grafiği dosya boyutlarını gösterirken yanıt farklı tespit sayısı grafiğinin ransomware karşılaştırmasını dosya boyutuna yüklüyor.,,0.57,"create the violin plot u. Next we tell seaborn to set the axis labels and title and to set the y-axis to log-scale v. Finally, we make the plot appear w. We can also make an analogous plot showing the Number of Detections by Malware Type Number of vendor detections of looking at file size on the y-axis, we’re looking at the number of positives each file received. The results show some interesting trends. For example, ransomware is almost always detected by more than 30 scanners. The bitcoin, trojan, and keylogger malware types,",DUZELT,YUKSEK,Violin grafiği dosya boyutlarını gösterirken yanıt farklı tespit sayısı grafiğinin ransomware karşılaştırmasını dosya boyutuna yüklüyor.,,0.57,"create the violin plot u. Next we tell seaborn to set the axis labels and title and to set the y-axis to log-scale v. Finally, we make the plot appear w. We can also make an analogous plot showing the Number of Detections by Malware Type Number of vendor detections of looking at file size on the y-axis, we’re looking at the number of positives each file received. The results show some interesting trends. For example, ransomware is almost always detected by more than 30 scanners. The bitcoin, trojan, and keylogger malware types,"
95,batch1000_0441,train,Malware,ENISA%20Threat%20Landscape%202022.txt,What specific software supply chain attacks were mentioned in the report as having a high impact and disrupting critical services?,"The report mentions several software supply chain attacks with high impact and potential to disrupt critical services, including the exploitation of the Log4j Java logging library vulnerability, malicious Python library manager PyPi packages, and malicious RubyGems packages.","While supply chain attacks are primarily associated with state-backed actors139, cybercriminals became more interested and proficient in the supply chain as an attack vector to conduct their operations during the reporting period. During the reporting period, supply chain attacks have become increasingly interconnected with ransomware campaigns140 141 142, allowing the threat actors to increase the scale of their operations with a single initial compromise150. Such supply chain attacks usually lead to ransomware deployment, coin mining, stealing cryptocurrency, or stealing credentials that will enable cybercriminals to facilitate their malicious activities further. Public reports say supply chain attacks are related to poisoned developer libraries and software platform compromises143. Software supply chain attacks (e.g. via widely deployed software) can have a high impact and 143disrupt critical services or even services not directly affected. Indicative examples of such attacks include the Node Package Manager (NPM) package compromises144 145 143 exploitation of the Log4j Java logging library vulnerability144, malicious python library manager PyPi packages 146 147, and malicious RubyGems packages148. Major 134 Mandiant - UNC1151 Assessed with High Confidence to have Links to Belarus, Ghostwriter Campaign Aligned with Belarusian Government 136 U.S. Department of Justice - Two Iranian Nationals Charged for Cyber-Enabled Disinformation and Threat Campaign Designed to Influence the 137 Bleeping Computer - Moses Staff hackers wreak havoc on Israeli orgs with ransomless encryptions - shadow-keeps-targeting-israeli-victims/ intelligence/cyber-year-in-retrospect.html organised-crime-threat-assessment-iocta-2021 Intelligence-Report-Vol-2.pdf discovered-popular-npm-package-ua-parser-js traffic-in-novel-exfiltration-technique/ credit-cards-injecting-code/#products 148 Bleeping Computer - Malicious RubyGems packages used in cryptocurrency supply chain attack - Please use footn",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.80,"platform compromises143. Software supply chain attacks (e.g. via widely deployed software) can have a high impact and 143disrupt critical services or even services not directly affected. Indicative examples of such attacks include the Node Package Manager (NPM) package compromises144 145 143 exploitation of the Log4j Java logging library vulnerability144, malicious python library manager PyPi packages 146 147, and malicious RubyGems packages148. Major 134 Mandiant - UNC1151 Assessed with High Confidence to have Links to Belarus, Ghostwriter Cam",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.80,"platform compromises143. Software supply chain attacks (e.g. via widely deployed software) can have a high impact and 143disrupt critical services or even services not directly affected. Indicative examples of such attacks include the Node Package Manager (NPM) package compromises144 145 143 exploitation of the Log4j Java logging library vulnerability144, malicious python library manager PyPi packages 146 147, and malicious RubyGems packages148. Major 134 Mandiant - UNC1151 Assessed with High Confidence to have Links to Belarus, Ghostwriter Cam"
96,batch_new500_0265,train,Malware,Bytes to Threats_ Byte-Sequence Transformer For Malware Dete.txt,What is the false positive rate (FPR) observed in the 7-8 entropy bin?,The false positive rate (FPR) observed in the 7-8 entropy bin is 11.63%.,"Published in 2026 IEEE ICAD. DOI: 10.1109/ICAD69378.2026.11608884 Notably, overall accuracy in this bin remains high (99.06%), illustrating how aggregate accuracy can mask a severe classasymmetric failure that would be operationally significant in deployment. The same asymmetry is observed when stratifying by full-file entropy (FPR: 11.63%, FNR: 0.67% in the 7– 8 bin), confirming that this behaviour reflects a genuine bias in the learned representations rather than an artifact of input truncation. These findings suggest that entropy-aware training strategies or a dedicated high-entropy classifier could meaningfully reduce false positives in this regime. Comparison with CNN-based malware detectors highlights the advantages of transformer-based architectures for byte sequence modeling. CNNs use fixed receptive fields and local convolutional operations, which limit their ability to capture long-range dependencies. In contrast, MalwareFormer combines token reduction with windowed self-attention, enabling aggregation of both local and global byte-level structures. This architectural expressiveness likely contributes to its superior performance across all threshold-dependent metrics, even under truncated input constraints. In this work, we presented MalwareFormer, a transformerbased, byte-level malware detection framework that operates directly on raw executable bytes without requiring handcrafted features or domain expertise. Leveraging transformer selfattention, the model captures long-range byte dependencies and consistently outperforms CNN-based baselines across all threshold-dependent metrics, achieving 98.35% accuracy, a macro-F1 of 98.32%, and a false positive rate of 1.94% on We further analyzed the impact of input sequence length and found that while larger byte sequences can provide modest gains, the framework shows stable performance even with truncated inputs. An entropy-stratified analysis reveals a nuanced failure mode: while error rates remain balanced acro",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"illustrating how aggregate accuracy can mask a severe classasymmetric failure that would be operationally significant in deployment. The same asymmetry is observed when stratifying by full-file entropy (FPR: 11.63%, FNR: 0.67% in the 7– 8 bin), confirming that this behaviour reflects a genuine bias in the learned representations rather than an artifact of input truncation. These findings suggest that entropy-aware training strategies or a dedicated high-entropy classifier could meaningfully reduce false positives in this regime. Comparison with",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"illustrating how aggregate accuracy can mask a severe classasymmetric failure that would be operationally significant in deployment. The same asymmetry is observed when stratifying by full-file entropy (FPR: 11.63%, FNR: 0.67% in the 7– 8 bin), confirming that this behaviour reflects a genuine bias in the learned representations rather than an artifact of input truncation. These findings suggest that entropy-aware training strategies or a dedicated high-entropy classifier could meaningfully reduce false positives in this regime. Comparison with"
97,batch500_0416,train,Malware,VMSpec_ A Decompiler-based Approach against Virtual Machine.txt,How many programs were used in the evaluation of VMSpec's prototype?,147 programs were used in the evaluation of VMSpec's prototype.,"VMSpec: A Decompiler-based Approach against Virtual Machine Obfuscation Technical University of Munich Malicious actors frequently obfuscate their code to hinder investigative reverse engineering efforts. Among the most formidable techniques is virtual machine (VM) obfuscation: programs are translated into bytecode for a custom VM, often with a randomized virtual instruction set and distributed alongside a corresponding interpreter. Manual analysis and deobfuscation scale poorly due to the complexity of VM obfuscation and the diversification techniques that render each VM and interpreter unique. Existing methods primarily focus on simplifying execution traces and identifying virtual execution boundaries. However, comparatively little attention has been given to recovering higher-level program semantics from VM-obfuscated code. To address this gap, we propose VMSpec, a novel decompiler-based approach that automates the construction of VM processor specifications, enabling existing, well-known decompilers to analyze the VM-obfuscated code. We evaluate our prototype of VMSpec on 147 programs from an established obfuscation benchmark, comprising hash functions, standard algorithms, and randomized functions, obfuscated with different configurations of the Tigress and Code Virtualizer obfuscators. To demonstrate real-world applicability, we additionally include six domain generation algorithms stemming from real-world malware. VMSpec completely strips VM-obfuscation from nearly all obfuscated samples, producing output comparable to Ghidra’s decompilation of the original code. Furthermore, it is the first method capable of reconstructing the complete program behavior of VMobfuscated programs with infinite, input-dependent paths and Program authors obfuscate their software to thwart reverse engineering and other forms of program analysis. While obfuscation can serve legitimate purposes – such as protecting intellectual property or reducing fraud – it is especially attractiv",,,,,KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,"VMSpec 147 programı bir deney düzeneğine ait, malware kapsamı sınırlı.",,0.75,"specifications, enabling existing, well-known decompilers to analyze the VM-obfuscated code. We evaluate our prototype of VMSpec on 147 programs from an established obfuscation benchmark, comprising hash functions, standard algorithms, and randomized functions, obfuscated with different configurations of the Tigress and Code Virtualizer obfuscators. To demonstrate real-world applicability, we additionally include six domain generation algorithms stemming from real-world malware. VMSpec completely strips VM-obfuscation from nearly all obfuscated",KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,"VMSpec 147 programı bir deney düzeneğine ait, malware kapsamı sınırlı.",,0.75,"specifications, enabling existing, well-known decompilers to analyze the VM-obfuscated code. We evaluate our prototype of VMSpec on 147 programs from an established obfuscation benchmark, comprising hash functions, standard algorithms, and randomized functions, obfuscated with different configurations of the Tigress and Code Virtualizer obfuscators. To demonstrate real-world applicability, we additionally include six domain generation algorithms stemming from real-world malware. VMSpec completely strips VM-obfuscation from nearly all obfuscated"
98,draft_0151,train,Malware,sec_2403.01871v1_Penetration_Testing_of_5G_Core_Netw.txt,"What specific folder was discovered as vulnerable in the Free5gc implementation, leading to the leakage of critical information?","The specific folder discovered as vulnerable in the Free5gc implementation, leading to the leakage of critical information, was /etc/passwd.","This isolated scenario turns out to be vulnerable to DDoS attack, exploited using the Hping3 tool in flooding mode, randomizing the source IPs of each request. Although the web server did not crush, this offense may be deemed as effective also considering a large-scale uptake. Directory traversal. For this particular attack, only Free5gc was vulnerable, indeed, the examined folder discovered without knowing its specific path was /etc/passwd enabling in this circumstance the leakage of a large amount of critical information that can be exploited by malicious actors. Clickjacking. About OpenAirInterface, here the attack is not feasible, due to the absence of a web interface. Clickjacking seemed to be a feasible attack, taking into account that either Open5gs and Free5gc enable IFRAME. This was not sufficient, indeed, we did not reach the expected result, mainly due to the presence of the Cross-Origin Resource Sharing policy (CORS) which forestalls to access web resources through different sources than the ones employed by the website. Open5gs turned out to be vulnerable to this attack because it uses a known passphrase. Therefore, once obtained the key, it is possible to change the content of the JWT’s payload and then recreate the proper signature through the previously obtained key, making it valid. Regarding Free5gc, it does not rely on cookies, or hard-coding the credentials, thus it does not save the logged user’s session. Whilst, for the OpenAirInterface, this category of offense is not even applicable, because of the absence of a web interface. Table II presents a summary of the attacks tested against the different 5G core implementations, together with the effectiveness outcome. We notice that not all cores are vulnerable to the same attacks, and for some of them, attacks were not implementable at all. Implementable attacks Database permission leakage Json Web Token robustness TABLE II: Comparison Table between tested attacks over the three mentioned 5G Core. ",,,,,DUZELT,YUKSEK,"/etc/passwd bir dosyadır, klasör değil. Kaynak da yanlış ""folder"" diyor; soruyu dosya okuma/directory traversal bulgusu olarak düzelt.",,0.83,"as effective also considering a large-scale uptake. Directory traversal. For this particular attack, only Free5gc was vulnerable, indeed, the examined folder discovered without knowing its specific path was /etc/passwd enabling in this circumstance the leakage of a large amount of critical information that can be exploited by malicious actors. Clickjacking. About OpenAirInterface, here the attack is not feasible, due to the absence of a web interface. Clickjacking seemed to be a feasible attack, taking into account that either Open5gs and Free5",DUZELT,YUKSEK,"/etc/passwd bir dosyadır, klasör değil. Kaynak da yanlış ""folder"" diyor; soruyu dosya okuma/directory traversal bulgusu olarak düzelt.",,0.83,"as effective also considering a large-scale uptake. Directory traversal. For this particular attack, only Free5gc was vulnerable, indeed, the examined folder discovered without knowing its specific path was /etc/passwd enabling in this circumstance the leakage of a large amount of critical information that can be exploited by malicious actors. Clickjacking. About OpenAirInterface, here the attack is not feasible, due to the absence of a web interface. Clickjacking seemed to be a feasible attack, taking into account that either Open5gs and Free5"
99,kaggle1000_0275,train,Malware,ENISA%20Threat%20Landscape%202021.txt,"When did the original Buer malware evolve into RustyBuer, and what language was RustyBuer written in?","The original Buer malware was shown to have been rewritten in Rust, named RustyBuer, in May 2021.","An example of this trend was reported in May 2021, when the original Buer malware was shown to have been rewritten in Rust, named RustyBuer. Their similarity is based on nearly identical communication structures between the commandand-control servers and the infected host.326 Malware written in these emerging programming languages only represents a fraction of all the malware being developed today. Still, it is an important aspect for the future development of malware detection and code analysis techniques. Migrating to other languages could mean that the scanners and defenders of today lose effectiveness and ability to detect the malware of tomorrow. 4.1.5 Malware disruption Emotet is a strain of computer malware. Initially, the malware acted as a banking trojan. Over the years, the malware evolved and gained more ground. The functionality shifted from a simple information stealer to malware used to create an advanced botnet. The malware featured a well-developed command and control (C2) infrastructure, frequent updates and was very effective in evading detection. In 2020, the malware used Trickbot and QBot to steal credentials and spread inside networks.327 Throughout 2020, Emotet had been the most prevalent malware. The victims, part of the botnet, were often sold to other threat actors for targeted cyber-crime, such as ransomware or information stealing. In January 2021, Interpol announced a crackdown on the botnet. Law enforcement and judicial authorities worldwide cooperated and took control of the botnet’s infrastructure in a coordinated international action.328 Through the C2 infrastructure, they delivered ‘updates’ to the malware, which would cause it to uninstall itself in April 2021. The Ukrainian government arrested two individuals in relation to this investigation.329 The fight against cyber-crime is an ongoing and uneven battle. However, this case shows that through international cooperation, law enforcement can have an impact. 4.1.6 High-Risk Vulnerab",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"An example of this trend was reported in May 2021, when the original Buer malware was shown to have been rewritten in Rust, named RustyBuer. Their similarity is based on nearly identical communication structures between the commandand-control servers and the infected host.326 Malware written in these emerging programming languages only represents a fraction of all the malware being developed today. Still, it is an important aspect for the future development of malware detection and code analysis techniques. Migrating to other languages could me",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"An example of this trend was reported in May 2021, when the original Buer malware was shown to have been rewritten in Rust, named RustyBuer. Their similarity is based on nearly identical communication structures between the commandand-control servers and the infected host.326 Malware written in these emerging programming languages only represents a fraction of all the malware being developed today. Still, it is an important aspect for the future development of malware detection and code analysis techniques. Migrating to other languages could me"
100,batch1000_0163,train,Malware,the-underground-hacker-s-handbook.txt,What is the purpose of creating a Trojan file in the given example of ProRat?,"The purpose of creating the Trojan file in the given example of ProRat is to enable the program to connect to the server through the specified server port, ask for a password when the victim is infected, and identify the victim by a chosen name.","Trojan Horse – A trojan horse is a malicious program that can be used to do silly things to a system like changing its desktop, mess with the user interface, and take control of your mouse. It can also be used for some serious things like accessing your data, erasing your files, stealing your passwords, and capturing your keystrokes. 4. Logic Bombs – Logic bombs are usually pieces of code that are programmed into a program that lie dormant until a certain time or until a user does a certain action which causes it to be executed. When it is triggered it performs a certain function that the program wasn’t 5. Bacteria – Bacteria make many copies of themselves and eventually end up taking up all of the computers recourses such as all of its processor power, memory and disk space. This results in the legitimate user losing access to those resources. 6. Blended Threats – Blended threats combine all of the characteristics of the above and use them along with system vulnerabilities to spread and To show you an example of a malicious program, I will use a well known Windows Trojan, ProRat. 1. Download ProRat. Once it is downloaded right click on the folder and choose to extract it. A password prompt will come up. The password will be 2. Open up the program. You should see the following: 3. Next we will create the actual Trojan file. Click on Create and choose Create 4. Next put in your IP address so the server could connect to you. If you don’t know your IP address click on the little arrow to have it filled in for you automatically. Next put in your e-mail so that when and if a victim gets infected it will send you a message. We will not be using the rest of the 5. Click on the General Settings button to continue. Here we will choose the server port the program will connect through, the password you will be asked to enter when the victim is infected and you wish to connect with them, and the victim name. As you can see ProRat has the ability to disable the windows firewall ",,,,,YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,Trojan yapılandırma örneği kötü amaçlı yazılım alanıyla ilişkili.,Malware,0.63,"with system vulnerabilities to spread and To show you an example of a malicious program, I will use a well known Windows Trojan, ProRat. 1. Download ProRat. Once it is downloaded right click on the folder and choose to extract it. A password prompt will come up. The password will be 2. Open up the program. You should see the following: 3. Next we will create the actual Trojan file. Click on Create and choose Create 4. Next put in your IP address so the server could connect to you. If you don’t",YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,Trojan yapılandırma örneği kötü amaçlı yazılım alanıyla ilişkili.,Malware,0.63,"with system vulnerabilities to spread and To show you an example of a malicious program, I will use a well known Windows Trojan, ProRat. 1. Download ProRat. Once it is downloaded right click on the folder and choose to extract it. A password prompt will come up. The password will be 2. Open up the program. You should see the following: 3. Next we will create the actual Trojan file. Click on Create and choose Create 4. Next put in your IP address so the server could connect to you. If you don’t"
101,batch_new500_0432,val,Malware,NIST.SP.800-83r1.txt,"What type of mobile code is restricted by the Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformance (DMARC) system?","Unsigned ActiveX is an example of mobile code that is restricted by the Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformance (DMARC) system.","In addition to the standard spam filtering technologies, there are also emerging solutions that aim to reduce spam through email authentication. An example is the Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformance (DMARC) restricting which types of mobile code (such as unsigned ActiveX) may or may not be used from various sources (e.g., internal servers, external servers). Organizations should also block undesired web browser popup windows, as a form of content filtering. Some popup windows are crafted to look like legitimate system message boxes or websites, and can trick users into going to phony websites, including sites used for phishing, or authorizing changes to their hosts, among other malicious actions. Most web browsers can block popup windows, and third-party popup blockers are also available. Both email and web content filtering should use real-time blacklists, reputation services, and other similar mechanisms whenever feasible to avoid accepting content from known or likely malicious hosts and domains. These mechanisms use a variety of techniques to identify certain IP addresses, domain names, or URIs as being probably malicious or probably benign. Real-time blacklists tend to be based on observed malware activity, while reputation services may be based on user opinions or on automated analysis of websites, emails, etc. without necessarily detecting malware. Because the fidelity and accuracy of these mechanisms varies widely from one implementation to another, organizations should carefully evaluate any real-time blacklists, reputation services, or other similar mechanisms before deploying them into production environments to minimize disruption to operations. 3.4.5 Application Whitelisting Application whitelisting technologies, also known as application control programs, are used to specify which applications are authorized for use on a host. Most application whitelisting technologies can be run in two modes: audit and enforcement. In enforcem",,,,,ELE_ADAYI,KRITIK,DMARC e-posta alanı doğrulamasıdır; unsigned ActiveX mobil kodunu kısıtlamaz. PDF metninde farklı iki satır/sütun birleşmiş olabilir.,,0.92,"reduce spam through email authentication. An example is the Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformance (DMARC) restricting which types of mobile code (such as unsigned ActiveX) may or may not be used from various sources (e.g., internal servers, external servers). Organizations should also block undesired web browser popup windows, as a form of content filtering. Some popup windows are crafted to look like legitimate system message boxes or websites, and can trick users into going to phony websites, including sites used for ph",ELE_ADAYI,KRITIK,DMARC e-posta alanı doğrulamasıdır; unsigned ActiveX mobil kodunu kısıtlamaz. PDF metninde farklı iki satır/sütun birleşmiş olabilir.,,0.92,"reduce spam through email authentication. An example is the Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformance (DMARC) restricting which types of mobile code (such as unsigned ActiveX) may or may not be used from various sources (e.g., internal servers, external servers). Organizations should also block undesired web browser popup windows, as a form of content filtering. Some popup windows are crafted to look like legitimate system message boxes or websites, and can trick users into going to phony websites, including sites used for ph"
102,kaggle1000_0231,val,Malware,Same Attack_ Different Judge_ A First Controlled Adversarial.txt,What was the cost difference in terms of time and money between running a full search against JEV and GPT-6 Astra?,"The full search against JEV took about 200 seconds and $0.05; the identical search against GPT-6 Astra took about 1,600 seconds.","Success falls from 5 to 3 of 50 on JEV and from 11 to 10 on GPT-6 Astra; neither change is larger than chance. One limit applies to both searches: the phrase pool comes mostly from attacks that already work on LLM judges, so it suits them better, and the comparison favors JEV (§9). What the search does show clearly is cost. The full search against JEV took about 200 seconds and $0.05; the identical search against GPT-6 Astra took about 1,600 seconds (Appendix G). Answer to RQ4. The cost is false alarms (27% against 9– 17% for the LLM judges); we found no cost in robustness. JEV’s speed also makes an adaptive search against it about eight times faster to run. We first explain where JEV’s interface is a weakness and why a purpose statement fools Llama Guard 3 most, then discuss deployment and defenses, and end with the limitations of our JEV’s interface is a weakness only on phishing detection. On input moderation, no forgery gets a request through JEV, and interference between its questions stays at 0.01–0.04. On phishing detection the same forgeries reach 0.19–0.29, on a task where JEV already trails the GLM judges without attack (§7.2). The interface is therefore a weakness on the judgment-based task, not on the rule-based ones. JEV’s high false-positive rate (§5.1) suggests that the same strictness that keeps A5a out also makes it flag borderline benign requests. A purpose statement fools Llama Guard 3 most. moderation, a teaching purpose alone does nothing to the LLM judges; A5a works through its request for a general explanation (Appendix E). On phishing detection, a similar purpose statement (A2) makes Llama Guard 3 miss 62% of the phishing it had caught, against 0.00–0.28 for the LLM judges and 0.00 for JEV. Being purpose-built is not the cause: the same sentence makes Prompt Guard 2 flag more injections, from 66% to 100%, because a model trained on injections treats a sentence about the input as a sign of an attack. How a classifier reacts depends on what it ",,,,,ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,İki model için arama süre/maliyet karşılaştırması malware mekanizmasını öğretmiyor.,,1.00,"is cost. The full search against JEV took about 200 seconds and $0.05; the identical search against GPT-6 Astra took about 1,600 seconds (Appendix G). Answer to RQ4. The cost is false alarms (27% against 9– 17% for the LLM judges); we found no cost in robustness. JEV’s speed also makes an adaptive search against it about eight times faster to run. We first explain where JEV’s interface is a weakness and why a purpose statement fools Llama Guard 3 most, then discuss deployment and defenses, and end with the limitations of our",ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,İki model için arama süre/maliyet karşılaştırması malware mekanizmasını öğretmiyor.,,1.00,"is cost. The full search against JEV took about 200 seconds and $0.05; the identical search against GPT-6 Astra took about 1,600 seconds (Appendix G). Answer to RQ4. The cost is false alarms (27% against 9– 17% for the LLM judges); we found no cost in robustness. JEV’s speed also makes an adaptive search against it about eight times faster to run. We first explain where JEV’s interface is a weakness and why a purpose statement fools Llama Guard 3 most, then discuss deployment and defenses, and end with the limitations of our"
103,batch500_0019,val,Malware,NIST.SP.800-150.txt,How does the sharing of malware samples between companies benefit the cybersecurity community in terms of detecting and protecting against similar attacks?,"The sharing of malware samples between companies benefits the cybersecurity community by allowing each company to share detailed information about observed threats, such as the sender addresses of phishing emails, samples of malware collected from the attacks, analysis of exploit code used by actors, the IPs and URLs associated with the actor's command and control servers, and other infrastructure involved with attacks. This information is posted on a central forum where it can be accessed and analyzed by other members of the community. For example, if one company (A) identifies a new attack and shares a malware sample via the forum, another company (B) with advanced malware analysis capabilities can analyze the sample and provide insights into the actor","Systems that are successfully compromised by the malware are then reconfigured by the malware to contact command and control servers and other infrastructure operated by the actor to receive additional instructions, download other malware, and perform data exfiltration. Many companies within this industry sector are members of a formal threat information sharing organization in which a central forum is used to post information about observed threats. The posts describe details relevant to detecting and defending against the threat, such as the sender addresses of phishing emails, samples of malware collected from the attacks, analysis of exploit code used by actors, the IPs and URLs associated with the actor’s command and control servers, and other infrastructure involved with attacks. As soon as one company’s security team identifies a new attack, the information is shared with its peers within the forum. One of the companies (A) that participates in the forum has advanced malware analysis capabilities and is able to further characterize the actor and its command and control infrastructure using a malware sample shared via the forum by another company (B). Company A then shares back the information gained through its analysis of the malware. Through B’s sharing of the malware sample, the community benefits from the malware analysis capabilities of company A, and is able to quickly and efficiently detect and protect against similar attacks. In this scenario, an attack faced by one company contributes to another’s defense. Scenario 2: Campaign Analysis Cybersecurity analysts from companies in a business sector have been sharing indicators and malware samples in an online forum over the past few years. Each company performs independent analysis of the attacks and observes consistent patterns over time, with groups of events often having some commonalities, such as the type of malware used, the DNS domains of command and control channels, and other technical indicators",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.81,"Many companies within this industry sector are members of a formal threat information sharing organization in which a central forum is used to post information about observed threats. The posts describe details relevant to detecting and defending against the threat, such as the sender addresses of phishing emails, samples of malware collected from the attacks, analysis of exploit code used by actors, the IPs and URLs associated with the actor’s command and control servers, and other infrastructure involved with attacks. As soon as one company’s",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.81,"Many companies within this industry sector are members of a formal threat information sharing organization in which a central forum is used to post information about observed threats. The posts describe details relevant to detecting and defending against the threat, such as the sender addresses of phishing emails, samples of malware collected from the attacks, analysis of exploit code used by actors, the IPs and URLs associated with the actor’s command and control servers, and other infrastructure involved with attacks. As soon as one company’s"
104,batch_new500_0050,test,Malware,Mal-AAEGAN_ Adversarial Autoencoder with Double-Critic WGAN-.txt,What is the range of the input dimensionality (M) for the malware datasets used in the evaluation of Mal-AAEGAN?,"The range of the input dimensionality (M) for the malware datasets used in the evaluation of Mal-AAEGAN is {139, 215}.","Mal-AAEGAN: Adversarial Autoencoder with Double-Critic WGAN-GP for Malware Nam Hoang Thanh, Trung Pham Duy, and Lam Bui Thu⋆ Academy of Cryptography Techniques, Ha Noi, Viet Nam Abstract. Machine-learning (ML) malware detectors are known to be vulnerable to adversarial samples. Generative adversarial networks (GANs) such as MalGAN, Mal-LSGAN, and our previous Mal-D2GAN can craft adversarial malware to probe and harden these detectors, yet they operate directly on a high-dimensional, discrete binary feature space, which leads to unstable training, vanishing gradients, and adversarial samples of limited diversity. To address these problems we propose Mal-AAEGAN. First, an adversarial autoencoder (AAE) learns a low-dimensional, regularized and continuous latent representation of the M-dimensional binary API feature vector, and the generator performs an additive perturbation in this latent space rather than in the raw binary space. Second, training follows the Wasserstein GAN with gradient penalty (WGAN-GP) scheme using two critics: a mimic-black-box critic that inherits the role of the substitute detector and a naive critic that inherits the role of the additional detector. A decode-binarize-OR pipeline guarantees that the original API calls of the malware are preserved. The model is evaluated on two malware datasets of different input dimensionality (M ∈{139, 215}) against eight common ML detectors and compared, under an identical protocol, with MalGAN, Mal-LSGAN and Mal-D2GAN. Mal-AAEGAN trains stably in the continuous latent space and attains an evasion rate on par with or higher than these binary-space baselines across both datasets, while its generator stays responsive to the noise input rather than reducing to a single fixed perturbation per malware. Keywords: malware detection · adversarial malware samples · generative adversarial network · adversarial autoencoder · Wasserstein GAN · Malware refers to programs intentionally designed to harm computers, servers, o",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.71,"dimensionality (M ∈{139, 215}) against eight common ML detectors and compared, under an identical protocol, with MalGAN, Mal-LSGAN and Mal-D2GAN. Mal-AAEGAN trains stably in the continuous latent space and attains an evasion rate on par with or higher than these binary-space baselines across both datasets, while its generator stays responsive to the noise input rather than reducing to a single fixed perturbation per malware. Keywords: malware detection · adversarial malware samples · generative adversarial network · adversarial autoencoder · Wa",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.71,"dimensionality (M ∈{139, 215}) against eight common ML detectors and compared, under an identical protocol, with MalGAN, Mal-LSGAN and Mal-D2GAN. Mal-AAEGAN trains stably in the continuous latent space and attains an evasion rate on par with or higher than these binary-space baselines across both datasets, while its generator stays responsive to the noise input rather than reducing to a single fixed perturbation per malware. Keywords: malware detection · adversarial malware samples · generative adversarial network · adversarial autoencoder · Wa"
105,draft_0294,test,Malware,Phantomware_ a Data Augmentation Framework for the Detection.txt,What are the limitations of generating adversarial samples for malware detection?,"The limitations of generating adversarial samples for malware detection include:
(i) The process is time-consuming due to the need for functional correctness checks;
(ii) When only a limited number of malware samples is available, the generation procedure may fail to produce a sufficiently large and/or diverse number of adversarial samples for effective finetuning;
(iii) It assumes the availability of a well-trained detector, which is unlikely in scarce-data scenarios, making the adversarial component practically ineffective when dealing with early-stage malware.","Moreover, these approaches suffer from three critical limitations: (i) the process is time-consuming due to the need for functional correctness checks; (ii) when only a limited number of malware samples is available, the generation procedure may fail to produce a sufficiently large and/or diverse number of adversarial samples for effective finetuning; and (iii) it assumes the availability of a well-trained detector, which is unlikely in scarce-data scenarios, making the adversarial component practically ineffective when dealing with early-stage malware An alternative approach is to generate representations of malware variants, that is, synthetic malware embeddings within a learned latent domain rather than constructing functional executables. These generated embeddings do not correspond to real malware samples; instead, they serve as constructive artifacts that approximate the potential distribution of small malware families. By enriching the embedding space in this way, detectors can be trained on a more diverse set of representations and can expand their decision boundaries toward regions that real future variants may plausibly occupy, eliminating the need for costly functionality validation. However, existing approaches rely on an adversarial generation objective, which provides only limited control over the location of the generated samples, as their primary goal is to induce misclassification. Consequently, the generated samples typically concentrate around the detector’s decision boundary, providing limited coverage of other potentially challenging regions of the latent space. Moreover, these approaches generally assume access to tens or hundreds of real samples [29, 52], and their effectiveness in early-stage malware campaigns, where only a handful of samples are available, has not been demonstrated. To fill these gaps, we propose Phantomware, a geometry-guided embedding generation approach which synthesizes malware-like latent representations by perturbing r",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.93,"Moreover, these approaches suffer from three critical limitations: (i) the process is time-consuming due to the need for functional correctness checks; (ii) when only a limited number of malware samples is available, the generation procedure may fail to produce a sufficiently large and/or diverse number of adversarial samples for effective finetuning; and (iii) it assumes the availability of a well-trained detector, which is unlikely in scarce-data scenarios, making the adversarial component practically ineffective when dealing with early-stage",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.93,"Moreover, these approaches suffer from three critical limitations: (i) the process is time-consuming due to the need for functional correctness checks; (ii) when only a limited number of malware samples is available, the generation procedure may fail to produce a sufficiently large and/or diverse number of adversarial samples for effective finetuning; and (iii) it assumes the availability of a well-trained detector, which is unlikely in scarce-data scenarios, making the adversarial component practically ineffective when dealing with early-stage"
106,extra_20261108_5613,train,Mobile Security,Mathematical model of printing-imaging channel for blind det.txt,What type of metric is commonly used to measure the information loss during the printing and scanning process of digital CDP blueprints?,"The commonly used metric to measure the information loss during the printing and scanning process of digital CDP blueprints is the Hamming distance between the template \( t \) and an estimation \( \tilde{t} \) of the template, based on the probe \( y \).","The Defender protects his brand by using a family of digital CDP blueprints {tn}N n=1 stored in the form of a binary matrix tn, which is then printed on the object package on and sent to the public domain. The Attacker has access to the printed version of the CDP and may use it to create a counterfeit cn, through the process of scanning, post-processing and reprinting (see [7]–[10] for investigations of attacking techniques). At the authentication stage, the Veriﬁer receives an unindentiﬁed package (either on or cn) from which a digital image yn is acquired, using any device such as a scanner or a mobile phone. We denote xn the code acquired from on and f n the code acquired from cn. An authentication is then performed based on the probe yn, which might be either xn or f n, and on the reference template tn. B. Authentication techniques The algorithms used for authentication evolved a lot in the last few years. At ﬁrst, CDP were designed with an idea to be resistant to simple scanning & reprinting attacks [4]. Due to the dot gain effect of printers, a portion of the information stored in the template blueprint t is lost in the probe y through the process of printing and scanning. Various ways to measure the information loss have been proposed which can be formalized with different types of metrics: 1) ℓ1- or ℓ2-distance between the probe y and the template 2) Pearson correlation between t and y; 3) Hamming distance between the template t and an estimation ˜t of the template, based on the probe y. A very common way to perform the estimation ˜t is to use Otsu’s TABLE I: Mathematical notations used in the paper. Mathematical notation binary digital template digital original printed from t digital fake version of t probe representing either x or f digital template estimated from y binary random matrix for t binary random matrix for ˜t probability of black symbol in T set of all neighbourhoods positive probability at ω probability of bit-ﬂipping at ω codebook of probabilities index within the dataset coordinates of pixels in t integer deﬁning the size of ω magniﬁcation factor from t to x binarization algorithm and then a majority voting for each symbol. Fig. 2 on the next page illustrates this technique. Nowadays, new techniques emerge with the use of machine learning, allowing one to train deep classiﬁers [6], [11] and deep binarization techniques [7]–[10]. Although showing very promising results, these new algorithms act as black boxes and thus lack interpretability, which is paramount when working on reliability questions and security-critical applications such as the protection of pharmaceutical products. C. Stochastic model of Printing-Imaging channel The PI channel can be described mathematically as a Markov Chain T →X →˜T, where: • T is a random binary matrix of size L×L sampled from i.i.d.",,,,,ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,Basım-tarama CDP taslaklarında Hamming uzaklığı; mobil cihaz güvenliği bağı görünmüyor.,,0.82,"the information stored in the template blueprint t is lost in the probe y through the process of printing and scanning. Various ways to measure the information loss have been proposed which can be formalized with different types of metrics: 1) ℓ1- or ℓ2-distance between the probe y and the template 2) Pearson correlation between t and y; 3) Hamming distance between the template t and an estimation ˜t of the template, based on the probe y. A very common way to perform the estimation ˜t is to use Otsu’s TABLE I: Mathematical",ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,Basım-tarama CDP taslaklarında Hamming uzaklığı; mobil cihaz güvenliği bağı görünmüyor.,,0.82,"the information stored in the template blueprint t is lost in the probe y through the process of printing and scanning. Various ways to measure the information loss have been proposed which can be formalized with different types of metrics: 1) ℓ1- or ℓ2-distance between the probe y and the template 2) Pearson correlation between t and y; 3) Hamming distance between the template t and an estimation ˜t of the template, based on the probe y. A very common way to perform the estimation ˜t is to use Otsu’s TABLE I: Mathematical"
107,kaggle1000_0727,train,Mobile Security,A Systematic Study of Security Enforcement at Android Inter-.txt,What is the target SDK version requirement for the ServiceStateProvider to perform permission checks?,"The ServiceStateProvider requires a target SDK version to perform permission checks, with a target SDK version gating on permission checks.","MmsSmsProvider: Write permission declaration (readPermission=READ_SMS but no writePermission; zero-perm app can delete all SMS/MMS) com.android.providers.telephony GoogleSettingsProvider: Read permission declaration (writePermission set but no readPermission; leaks 18 partner settings including privacy prefs) ServiceStateProvider: targetSdkVersion gating on permission checks (targetSdk < 31 bypasses both READ_ GServicesProvider: Permission protection level adequacy (READ_GSERVICES is normal, auto-granted; 1404 config entries including 3 API keys, 86 URLs, 10 certs) com.google.android.gsf BluetoothOppProvider: SQL injection defense (setStrict(true) + path-permission) com.google.android.bluetooth IccProvider, SimPhonebookProvider: Permission ContactsProvider2 zero-perm variant: URI grant scope (ACTION_PICK contact picker grants temporary URI but SQLi from that URI accesses ALL internal tables) com.android.providers.contacts B3: BroadcastReceivers (11 evaluations, all gaps) GmsExternalReceiver (FRP): Per-action caller validation (FrpUpdateIntentOperation writes FRP secret without com.google.android.gms GmsExternalReceiver (CryptAuth): Per-action caller validation (AuthZenEventHandler triggers ForceRegistration of 6 security keys) com.google.android.gms GmsExternalReceiver (systemic): Architectural pattern: single receiver, 31 actions, no android:permission, no per-action validation com.google.android.gms TestingToolsBroadcastReceiver: Build-time stripping of debug code (receiver exported in production; gated by runtime Phenotype flag, not build-time removal) 11 Google apps (Gmail, Dialer, CalendarDebugReceiver: Export control on debug receiver (accepts SECRET_CODE broadcast) com.android.providers.calendar TestReceiver: Export control on test receiver (no permission, no build-type guard; forwards SIMULATE_ STATE to privileged StateService) com.google.android.apps.scone MediaOutputDialogReceiver: Protected broadcast NOT declared as protected; sibling LAUNCH_SYSTEM_ packa",,,,,DUZELT,YUKSEK,Yanıt SDK eşik değerini vermiyor; kaynak hedef SDK <31 için izin denetimi baypasından söz ediyor.,,0.44,"Read permission declaration (writePermission set but no readPermission; leaks 18 partner settings including privacy prefs) ServiceStateProvider: targetSdkVersion gating on permission checks (targetSdk < 31 bypasses both READ_ GServicesProvider: Permission protection level adequacy (READ_GSERVICES is normal, auto-granted; 1404 config entries including 3 API keys, 86 URLs, 10 certs) com.google.android.gsf BluetoothOppProvider: SQL injection defense (setStrict(true) + path-permission) com.google.android.bluetooth IccProvider, SimPhonebookProvider:",DUZELT,YUKSEK,Yanıt SDK eşik değerini vermiyor; kaynak hedef SDK <31 için izin denetimi baypasından söz ediyor.,,0.44,"Read permission declaration (writePermission set but no readPermission; leaks 18 partner settings including privacy prefs) ServiceStateProvider: targetSdkVersion gating on permission checks (targetSdk < 31 bypasses both READ_ GServicesProvider: Permission protection level adequacy (READ_GSERVICES is normal, auto-granted; 1404 config entries including 3 API keys, 86 URLs, 10 certs) com.google.android.gsf BluetoothOppProvider: SQL injection defense (setStrict(true) + path-permission) com.google.android.bluetooth IccProvider, SimPhonebookProvider:"
108,extra_20261108_6215,train,Mobile Security,Hacking.Android.txt,What mechanism can a malicious user exploit to gain higher-level permissions in an Android app?,"A malicious user can exploit improper implementation of sensitive activity invocation in Android apps. Specifically, if the app invokes sensitive activities by using IPC or web service calls and the sensitive parameters are tampered with by the attacker, the app may grant higher level permissions to the attacker. This can occur due to weak implementation of such functionality, where developers fail to properly validate or sanitize inputs.","Typically, this malicious code is provided in the form of data that the threat agent inputs to the mobile app through a number of different means. The following are some of the examples of Client-Side Injection attacks in Injection in WebViews Traditonal SQL Injection in raw SQL statements used with SQLite databases SQL Injection in content providers Path traversal in content providers M8: Security Decisions via Untrusted Inputs Developers should always assume that malformed inputs can be given by unauthorized parties to abuse the sensitive functions of an application. Specifically in Android, an attacker can intercept the calls (IPC or web service calls) and tamper with such sensitive parameters. Weak implementation of such functionality leads to improper behavior of an app and can even grant higher level permissions to an attacker. One example would be to invoke sensitive activities by using Overview of Attacking Android Apps M9: Improper Session Handling Mobile apps use protocols like SOAP or REST to connect to services. These protocols are stateless. When a mobile client application is used with these protocols, clients get a token from the server after authentication. The token generated by the server will now be used during the user's session. OWASP's Improper Session Handling talks about attacking and securing these sessions. One common problem that is often seen in mobile apps is invalidating the token at the client side and not at the server side. Usually, the token received by the application will be placed on the client's file system using shared preferences or SQLite databases. A malicious user who gains access to this token, can use it at any time if the token is not properly invalidated at the server side. Other possible scenarios are session timeouts, weak token creation, and an expired token. M10: Lack of Binary Protections Reverse Engineering is one of the most common problems seen in the majority of Android apps. One of the first steps attackers perform when they get an app binary is to decompile or disassemble the application. This allows them to view the hardcoded secrets, find vulnerabilities, and even modify the functionality of the application by repacking the disassembled application. Though obfuscating the source code is not a hard thing to do, the majority of the apps do not appear to do it. When the code is not obfuscated, all an attacker needs is a nice tool such as apktool or dex2jar to get the work done. Some applications check for rooted devices. It is also possible to bypass such checks by reversing the app or by manipulating the application flow by hooking into it. Security_Project_-_Top_Ten_Mobile_Risks This book is focused more on concepts rather than tools. However, automated tools often save us some time during penetration tests. The following are some of the most common automated tools that are available for automated assessments of Android applications.",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.71,"content providers M8: Security Decisions via Untrusted Inputs Developers should always assume that malformed inputs can be given by unauthorized parties to abuse the sensitive functions of an application. Specifically in Android, an attacker can intercept the calls (IPC or web service calls) and tamper with such sensitive parameters. Weak implementation of such functionality leads to improper behavior of an app and can even grant higher level permissions to an attacker. One example would be to invoke sensitive activities by using Overview of At",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.71,"content providers M8: Security Decisions via Untrusted Inputs Developers should always assume that malformed inputs can be given by unauthorized parties to abuse the sensitive functions of an application. Specifically in Android, an attacker can intercept the calls (IPC or web service calls) and tamper with such sensitive parameters. Weak implementation of such functionality leads to improper behavior of an app and can even grant higher level permissions to an attacker. One example would be to invoke sensitive activities by using Overview of At"
109,batch1000_0410,train,Mobile Security,Derived Personal Identity Verification (PIV) Credentials.txt,What are the steps required before installing the Intercede Desktop Client?,"Before installing the Intercede Desktop Client, have available the host name and the distinguished name (DN) of the issuer of the Transport Layer Security (TLS) certificate used to communicate with the MyID application server.","Choose Authentication Certificate in the Container m. Select the roles that receive, issue, and validate DPCs. All was chosen in this example. o. Select PIV_CON in the Select Card Layout tab. q. Enter text into the Comments and click Next, then Finish. 2.2.2 Intercede MyID Identity Agent The MyID Identity Agent runs as an application and interfaces with the MyID CMS and supports a wide range of mobile devices and credential stores, including the device native keystore, software keystore, and microSD. The MyID Identity Agent mobile application is required to issue and manage DPCs. No special configuration is necessary after installing the application; scanning the QR code during the initial enrollment directs the Identity Agent to your instance of MyID CMS. MyID Identity Agent is supported for both iOS and Android platforms. 2.2.2.1 Installation MyID Identity Agent is available on the Google Play Store and the Apple App Store. Detailed installation procedures are found on the Google Play Store and Apple App Store support sites. 2.2.3 Intercede Desktop Client The Intercede Desktop component of this example solution serves as the main point of administration of the MyID CMS. It was installed on a Dell Latitude E6540 laptop running Windows 7. The procedures below are adapted from the Installation and Configuration Guide Version 10.8, Section 7.4. 2.2.3.1 Installation Before installation, have available the host name and the distinguished name (DN) of the issuer of the Transport Layer Security (TLS) certificate used to communicate with the MyID application server. 1. Run the provided .msi file as an administrator. 2. Select the destination location, then click Next. ______________________________________________________________________________________________________ NIST SP 1800-12C: Derived Personal Identity Verification (PIV) Credentials 3. Select the desired shortcuts to be installed. 5. In the MyID Desktop InstallShield Wizard: a. In the Server URL field, enter the ",,,,,ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,Intercede masaüstü istemcisi yükleme ön koşulları mobil cihaz güvenliği sorusu değil.,,1.00,"Intercede Desktop component of this example solution serves as the main point of administration of the MyID CMS. It was installed on a Dell Latitude E6540 laptop running Windows 7. The procedures below are adapted from the Installation and Configuration Guide Version 10.8, Section 7.4. 2.2.3.1 Installation Before installation, have available the host name and the distinguished name (DN) of the issuer of the Transport Layer Security (TLS) certificate used to communicate with the MyID application server. 1. Run the provided .msi file as an admini",ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,Intercede masaüstü istemcisi yükleme ön koşulları mobil cihaz güvenliği sorusu değil.,,1.00,"Intercede Desktop component of this example solution serves as the main point of administration of the MyID CMS. It was installed on a Dell Latitude E6540 laptop running Windows 7. The procedures below are adapted from the Installation and Configuration Guide Version 10.8, Section 7.4. 2.2.3.1 Installation Before installation, have available the host name and the distinguished name (DN) of the issuer of the Transport Layer Security (TLS) certificate used to communicate with the MyID application server. 1. Run the provided .msi file as an admini"
110,batch1000_0514,train,Mobile Security,I-HITL_ Inalienable Human-in-the-Loop.txt,What is the total number of lines of code (LOC) for the backend service in the Countersign implementation?,The total number of lines of code (LOC) for the backend service in the Countersign implementation is 800 LOC.,"Deﬁnition 2 (I-HITL). An I-HITL protocol for a message space M and a topology is a tuple  = (Enroll; Approve;  Enroll and Approve(m) are interactive protocols among over its channels, prescribing a program for each of P; S; T and a program in C for H. At the Ledger apps currently provide. Our construction relies only on the attestation and key management capabilities available to any Android or iOS application, so it may be deployed without requiring users to acquire new devices. In this section, we present Countersign, a mobile phonebased strong I-HITL implementation. Mobile phones are a natural candidate for T because they have the necessary interfaces and attestation capabilities, and are readily accessible to users. Yet, as shown by Section 6, many mobile phone applications providing closely related functionality (e.g., authentication and proof of personhood) fail to realize strong I-HITL. Countersign demonstrates that the necessary capabilities for I-HITL exist on mobile phones (under reasonable assumptions), and many similar applications are insecure simply because their workﬂow is designed for a subtly different threat model. Countersign’s core IHITL logic consists of 800 LOC for the backend service, and 150 LOC each for the Android/iOS application (excluding generic scaffolding such as conﬁguration, data models, and user interfaces), illustrating the feasibility of achieving I-HITL in practice. Our code is open-source at mentation details are deferred to Appendix F. 7.1. Mobile Phone Attestation Capabilities In our instantiation, the trusted entity T is a mobile application running on a commodity phone, so we rely on the phone to execute the application faithfully and to manage its keys securely. Our design leverages the attestation mechanisms provided by the Android and iOS ecosystems to allow S to verify the security properties of T . S must establish two properties. The ﬁrst is code integrity: the application handling requests and controlling the devi",,,,,ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,Bir uygulamanın backend satır sayısı mobil güvenlik yetkinliği değil.,,0.67,"designed for a subtly different threat model. Countersign’s core IHITL logic consists of 800 LOC for the backend service, and 150 LOC each for the Android/iOS application (excluding generic scaffolding such as conﬁguration, data models, and user interfaces), illustrating the feasibility of achieving I-HITL in practice. Our code is open-source at mentation details are deferred to Appendix F. 7.1. Mobile Phone Attestation Capabilities In our instantiation, the trusted entity T is a mobile application running on a commodity phone, so we rely on ",ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,Bir uygulamanın backend satır sayısı mobil güvenlik yetkinliği değil.,,0.67,"designed for a subtly different threat model. Countersign’s core IHITL logic consists of 800 LOC for the backend service, and 150 LOC each for the Android/iOS application (excluding generic scaffolding such as conﬁguration, data models, and user interfaces), illustrating the feasibility of achieving I-HITL in practice. Our code is open-source at mentation details are deferred to Appendix F. 7.1. Mobile Phone Attestation Capabilities In our instantiation, the trusted entity T is a mobile application running on a commodity phone, so we rely on "
111,batch1000_0147,train,Mobile Security,LABBA_ LLM-Assisted Behavioral Biometric Authentication.txt,What is the duration of the blowing gesture required for the phone-blowing interaction?,The blowing gesture required for the phone-blowing interaction lasts within a 5-second duration.,"In the proposed system, users perform blowing gestures toward the smartphone microphone screen, producing acoustic patterns that contain userspecific temporal and spectral characteristics suitable for behavioral biometric authentication. The phone-blowing interaction is performed within a 5-second duration, during which users may exhibit personalized blowing behaviors, including variations in the number of blows, duration of each blow, blowing intensity, blowing patterns, and airflow dynamics. Blow-acoustic biometric signals provide several advantages, including strong resistance against spoofing attacks, implicit liveness detection, continuous and unobtrusive authentication capability, non-invasiveness, high usability, and low-cost deployment through existing smartLABBA: LLM-Assisted Behavioral Biometric Authentication phone microphones. However, as a soft biometric modality, blow-acoustic signals are highly sensitive to contextual variability, including ambient acoustic noise, smartphone orientation, user posture (e.g., standing and sitting), variations in blowing intensity, environmental dynamics, as well as user fatigue and behavioral drift. These challenges motivate the integration of LLM-based contextual understanding and logical reasoning into the authentication process to improve robustness under diverse real-world conditions. The proposed framework extracts discriminative behavioral features from blow-acoustic signals to characterize user-specific airflow interaction patterns. The extracted features include two main categories: spectral and temporal. Spectral Feature Extraction Spectral features are extracted from the blow-acoustic recordings using a GFCC-based processing pipeline to capture discriminative frequency characteristics and short-term temporal variations. We adopt the GFCC configuration of BlowLive [9], the audio signal is segmented into overlapping frames of 50 ms with a 20 ms hop size. For each frame, a 24-channel gammatone filterbank is appli",,,,,KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,Telefona üfleme etkileşimi süresi tek başına güvenlik kavramı değil; kaynak bağlamı doğrulanmalı.,,0.67,"that contain userspecific temporal and spectral characteristics suitable for behavioral biometric authentication. The phone-blowing interaction is performed within a 5-second duration, during which users may exhibit personalized blowing behaviors, including variations in the number of blows, duration of each blow, blowing intensity, blowing patterns, and airflow dynamics. Blow-acoustic biometric signals provide several advantages, including strong resistance against spoofing attacks, implicit liveness detection, continuous and unobtrusive authe",KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,Telefona üfleme etkileşimi süresi tek başına güvenlik kavramı değil; kaynak bağlamı doğrulanmalı.,,0.67,"that contain userspecific temporal and spectral characteristics suitable for behavioral biometric authentication. The phone-blowing interaction is performed within a 5-second duration, during which users may exhibit personalized blowing behaviors, including variations in the number of blows, duration of each blow, blowing intensity, blowing patterns, and airflow dynamics. Blow-acoustic biometric signals provide several advantages, including strong resistance against spoofing attacks, implicit liveness detection, continuous and unobtrusive authe"
112,extra_20261108_755,train,Mobile Security,Android Hacker's Handbook.txt,What mechanism does Android use to check if a caller has the necessary permission to access the Surface Flinger graphics service's Binder IPC interface?,"Android checks if a caller has the required permission to access the Surface Flinger graphics service's Binder IPC interface by first obtaining the calling process ID (`pid`) and user ID (`uid`). It then verifies if `uid` is equal to `AID_GRAPHICS`. If not, it calls `PermissionCache::checkPermission` with the required permission `sReadFramebuffer` and logs an error message if the permission is denied.","This permission is typically granted only to the graphics system user, and allows access to the Binder IPC interface of the Surface Flinger graphics service. Furthermore, the caller’s group membership—and subsequent bearing of the required permission—is checked through a series of calls to the aforementioned functions, as illustrated by the following code snippet: const int pid = ipc->getCallingPid(); const int uid = ipc->getCallingUid(); if ((uid != AID_GRAPHICS) && !PermissionCache::checkPermission(sReadFramebuffer, ALOGE(""Permission Denial: "" ""can't read framebuffer pid=%d, uid=%d"", pid, uid); At a higher level, exposed IPC methods, such as those provided by bound Services, are typically distilled into an abstract interface via Android Interface Defi nition Language (AIDL). AIDL allows for two applications to use “agreed-upon” or standard interfaces for sending and receiving data, keeping the interface separate from the implementation. AIDL is akin to other Interface Defi nition Language fi les or, in a way, C/C++ header fi les. Consider the following sample AIDL snippet: Chapter 2 ■ Android Security Design and Architecture // IRemoteService.aidl package com.example.android; // Declare any non-default types here with import statements /** Example service interface */ interface IRemoteService { /** Request the process ID of this service, to do evil things with it. */ /** Demonstrates some basic types that you can use as parameters * and return values in AIDL. void basicTypes(int anInt, long aLong, boolean aBoolean, double aDouble, String aString); This AIDL example defi nes a simple interface, IRemoteService, along with two methods: getPid and basicTypes. An application that binds to the service exposing this interface would subsequently be able to call the aforementioned methods—facilitated by Binder. Anonymous Shared Memory, or ashmem for short, was another addition to the Android Linux kernel fork. The ashmem driver basically provides a fi le-based, reference-counted shared memory interface. Its use is prevalent across much of Android’s core components, such as Surface Flinger, Audio Flinger, System Server, and the DalvikVM. Because ashmem is designed to automatically shrink memory caches and reclaim memory regions when available system-wide memory is low, it is well suited for low-memory environments. At a low level, using ashmem is as simple as calling ashmem_create_region, and using mmap on the returned fi le descriptor: int fd = ashmem_create_region(""SomeAshmem"", size); At a higher level, the Android Framework provides the MemoryFile class, which serves as a wrapper around the ashmem driver. Furthermore, processes can use the Binder facility to later share these memory objects, leveraging the security features of Binder to restrict access. Incidentally, ashmem proved to be the source of a pretty serious fl aw in early 2011, allowing for a privilege escalation via Android properties. This is covered in greater detail in Chapter 3.",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.71,"This permission is typically granted only to the graphics system user, and allows access to the Binder IPC interface of the Surface Flinger graphics service. Furthermore, the caller’s group membership—and subsequent bearing of the required permission—is checked through a series of calls to the aforementioned functions, as illustrated by the following code snippet: const int pid = ipc->getCallingPid(); const int uid = ipc->getCallingUid(); if ((uid != AID_GRAPHICS) && !PermissionCache::checkPermission(sReadFramebuffer, ALOGE(""Permission Denial: ",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.71,"This permission is typically granted only to the graphics system user, and allows access to the Binder IPC interface of the Surface Flinger graphics service. Furthermore, the caller’s group membership—and subsequent bearing of the required permission—is checked through a series of calls to the aforementioned functions, as illustrated by the following code snippet: const int pid = ipc->getCallingPid(); const int uid = ipc->getCallingUid(); if ((uid != AID_GRAPHICS) && !PermissionCache::checkPermission(sReadFramebuffer, ALOGE(""Permission Denial: "
113,batch500_0044,train,Mobile Security,From Observations to Authorized Actions_ Measuring Source In.txt,What is the main limitation found when using the WebView accessibility fields for checking the source of content?,"The WebView accessibility fields expose the host application but do not provide information about the document origins available inside the browser, making it impossible for an agent reading through this interface to check which document supplied an object.","From Observations to Authorized Actions: Measuring Source Information in Android Agents Kangwon National University Chuncheon, Republic of Korea Kangwon National University Chuncheon, Republic of Korea Android agents read content from applications and web pages and act on it with the user’s authority, yet they cannot reliably tell where that content came from—its source, the application or document that supplied it. The WebView accessibility fields we inspected expose the host application but omit the document origins available inside the browser, so an agent reading through this interface cannot check which document supplied an object; a limited reproduction on an unmodified WebView yields the same finding. We build and separately evaluate two prototypes to examine how source information supports authorization. Our application-source monitor withholds side-effecting calls whose signed source record contains an untrusted application. With the source set derived from the OS accessibility log, it blocked every selected attack write in the tested runs, but it also withheld benign sends issued after reading untrusted content, flagged benign tasks that use external content, and carried source records into a later clean task. Our WebView source-reference gate uses records from a modified browser to check object origins and reference lifetime through direct calls; in a separate 24-trial evaluation, the live agent selected no disallowed object. Supporting benchmark and planner measurements characterize textual argument matches and attacker-target selection, without establishing causal dataflow or a controlled model ranking. The prototypes are not an integrated defense: the WebView gate still lacks authenticated browser delivery and atomic capture of selected bytes and origin. Together, the results show which source information the tested policies need and why it must stay linked to the object used in execution. • Security and privacy →Mobile platform security; Browser LLM a",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.86,"with the user’s authority, yet they cannot reliably tell where that content came from—its source, the application or document that supplied it. The WebView accessibility fields we inspected expose the host application but omit the document origins available inside the browser, so an agent reading through this interface cannot check which document supplied an object; a limited reproduction on an unmodified WebView yields the same finding. We build and separately evaluate two prototypes to examine how source information supports authorization. Ou",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.86,"with the user’s authority, yet they cannot reliably tell where that content came from—its source, the application or document that supplied it. The WebView accessibility fields we inspected expose the host application but omit the document origins available inside the browser, so an agent reading through this interface cannot check which document supplied an object; a limited reproduction on an unmodified WebView yields the same finding. We build and separately evaluate two prototypes to examine how source information supports authorization. Ou"
114,batch_new500_0286,train,Mobile Security,NIST.SP.800-124r2.txt,What type of attacks could potentially compromise a mobile device's lock screen if it is poorly protected with a simple password?,Brute-force attacks could potentially compromise a mobile device's lock screen if it is poorly protected with a simple password.,"Mobile Malware Mobile devices are designed to make it easy for users to find, acquire, and install third-party apps offered by app stores. Some users may sideload applications onto their device by allowing installation from untrusted or unknown sources. This accessibility poses significant security risks, especially for mobile device platforms and app stores that do not place security restrictions or other limitations on third-party app publishing. Organizations should base their mobile device security policy on the assumption that all unknown third-party apps downloaded by its employees to enterprise-accessible mobile devices are untrusted. Any application installed onto a mobile device can act as a portal for the developer to compromise the device and access sensitive enterprise information. Mobile devices have many sensors, like microphones and cameras, to improve user functionality, such as making phone calls and taking advanced pictures. Most mobile devices have two cameras (front and back) and have at least one microphone for audio input. Mobile malware on a mobile device can use these sensors to unknowingly collect information about the user or the administrator. Microphones and cameras should only be exposed when in use and protected at all NIST Mobile Threat Catalogue Reference: APP-16, APP-26, APP-43, CEL-33, STA-15 3.1.10. Information Loss Due to Insecure Lock Screen Configuration The lock screen is the first barrier that an unauthorized user must pass to gain access to information stored on a mobile device. The lock screen can be configured with an authentication factor to restrict access to the device. If poorly protected with a simple password, the lock screen may be breached through a brute-force attack. An unauthorized user who has compromised the authentication factor of a mobile device can access all sensitive information, modify the information, and pretend to be the device’s owner to gain further access to enterprise data. The lock screen can als",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.77,"be configured with an authentication factor to restrict access to the device. If poorly protected with a simple password, the lock screen may be breached through a brute-force attack. An unauthorized user who has compromised the authentication factor of a mobile device can access all sensitive information, modify the information, and pretend to be the device’s owner to gain further access to enterprise data. The lock screen can als",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.77,"be configured with an authentication factor to restrict access to the device. If poorly protected with a simple password, the lock screen may be breached through a brute-force attack. An unauthorized user who has compromised the authentication factor of a mobile device can access all sensitive information, modify the information, and pretend to be the device’s owner to gain further access to enterprise data. The lock screen can als"
115,batch500_0068,train,Mobile Security,Threat Modeling Intimate Partner Abuse in Agentic Systems.txt,What type of risks do abusers face when accessing artifacts of victim-survivors' behavior in agentic systems?,"Abusers face risks when accessing artifacts of victim-survivors' behavior in agentic systems, such as permission requests, logs, or information persistent within these systems. These artifacts may include permission requests for calendar management, where an abuser could request a victim-survivor's calendar for the coming month. The agentic system might treat this request as low-risk, reflecting typical norms of intimate-partner information sharing learned from its training data. However, in an abusive context, this low-risk assessment exposes the victim-survivor to real danger, as the system does not account for the specific abusive situation.","In agentic systems: Agentic systems might also be used to automate ways in which victim-survivors use technology, including help-seeking and evidence gathering. While this may be beneficial, it also creates risk if abusers can access any artifacts of this behavior, which may take the form of permission requests, logs, or information persistent in the Given their conversational capabilities and depending on their underlying model’s behavior, agentic systems may share the issues with conversational AI systems identified above as well as exacerbate them. For example, systems may not identify the need for and suggest help-seeking actions for all users in need based on stereotyped interpretations of Known TFA vector(s): Many commercial technologies and security mechanisms are designed under norms of benign households, leading to vulnerabilities such as abusers leveraging shared phone plans for surveillance (Tseng et al., 2020) and UI-bound adversaries being able to exploit passkey authentication (Daffalla et al., 2025). In agentic systems: Abusers may be able to exploit aspects of agentic systems that assume benign household norms. Consider a victim-survivor whose agent manages their calendar, if the abuser requests their calendar for the coming month, the system might treat this as low-risk, reflecting typical norms of intimate-partner information sharing learned from its training data. In an abusive context, however, treating the request as low-risk creates real exposure for the victim-survivor. This exploits approaches to permissioning that aim to make context-dependent decisions, often using a model to assess the risk of an action and acting accordingly. This failure mode is non-trivial to correct, since the underlying norms are learned during training rather than set by any configurable policy. The same norms may also shape how agentic systems treat Threat Modeling Intimate Partner Abuse in Agentic Systems instructions from an abuser. While explicitly malicious prom",,,,,YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,"Agentic sistemlerde istismar ve mahremiyet, doğrudan mobil güvenlik sorusu değil.",System Security,0.72,"al., 2025). In agentic systems: Abusers may be able to exploit aspects of agentic systems that assume benign household norms. Consider a victim-survivor whose agent manages their calendar, if the abuser requests their calendar for the coming month, the system might treat this as low-risk, reflecting typical norms of intimate-partner information sharing learned from its training data. In an abusive context, however, treating the request as low-risk creates real exposure for the victim-survivor. This exploits approaches to permissioning that aim ",YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,"Agentic sistemlerde istismar ve mahremiyet, doğrudan mobil güvenlik sorusu değil.",System Security,0.72,"al., 2025). In agentic systems: Abusers may be able to exploit aspects of agentic systems that assume benign household norms. Consider a victim-survivor whose agent manages their calendar, if the abuser requests their calendar for the coming month, the system might treat this as low-risk, reflecting typical norms of intimate-partner information sharing learned from its training data. In an abusive context, however, treating the request as low-risk creates real exposure for the victim-survivor. This exploits approaches to permissioning that aim "
116,batch500_0260,val,Mobile Security,Penetrating the Reality Wall_ Long-Range and Through-Wall AR.txt,What is the maximum distance the attacker can be from the victim's VR device to still be considered within the threat model?,The maximum distance the attacker can be from the victim's VR device to still be considered within the threat model is 8.0 meters.,"Attacker’s Capabilities. Unlike previous AR/VR attacks, we do not assume that the attacker can compromise the VR headset by installing malware to obtain motion sensor data or monitor changes in system workload [4, 23, 24, 50, 64, 68, 70–72]. Next, the attacker has no Line-of-Sight (LoS) view of VR users and cannot place cameras or hand trackers to record videos of the victim’s head movements and hand gestures directly [10, 15, 30, 35], nor can a suspicious device be placed in close proximity (e.g., less than 2.0 m) to record button-clicking sounds or emitted infrared signals from VR controllers [25, 37]. Moreover, we do not assume that the victim would join unauthorized shared virtual spaces [28, 62] or multi-user VR apps [55] to share avatar data publicly (e.g., hand movements, gaze information). Furthermore, the victim can use the VR device while (a) Before interference removal. (b) After interference removal ence caused by other non-target connected devices. sitting on the stationary boundary or moving in the playing zone, without the physical restrictions of a fixed position required in other side-channel attacks [1, 27]. Note that we only assume that the attacker can place the wireless power sniffer in the adjacent room to capture the Wi-Fi signals from the target router and use the measurable wireless power patterns to infer multi-level and fine-grained privacy from the AR/VR users. Following the research line of prior wireless side-channel studies [1, 27, 38], our threat model inherently requires the attacker to be physically present in a space adjacent to the victim within a limited yet practical proximity (e.g., 5.0 m–8.0 m). This scenario is naturally satisfied in apartments, hotels, and shared offices without requiring same-room device placement or malware installation, although the attack’s effectiveness depends on the propagation characteristics of the wireless medium and is influenced by intervening obstacles and co-channel devices in the environment. ",,,,,DUZELT,YUKSEK,"Kaynak 5,0–8,0 m gibi örnek yakınlık aralığı veriyor; kesin maksimum mesafe ilan etmiyor.",,0.55,"27, 38], our threat model inherently requires the attacker to be physically present in a space adjacent to the victim within a limited yet practical proximity (e.g., 5.0 m–8.0 m). This scenario is naturally satisfied in apartments, hotels, and shared offices without requiring same-room device placement or malware installation, although the attack’s effectiveness depends on the propagation characteristics of the wireless medium and is influenced by intervening obstacles and co-channel devices in the environment.",DUZELT,YUKSEK,"Kaynak 5,0–8,0 m gibi örnek yakınlık aralığı veriyor; kesin maksimum mesafe ilan etmiyor.",,0.55,"27, 38], our threat model inherently requires the attacker to be physically present in a space adjacent to the victim within a limited yet practical proximity (e.g., 5.0 m–8.0 m). This scenario is naturally satisfied in apartments, hotels, and shared offices without requiring same-room device placement or malware installation, although the attack’s effectiveness depends on the propagation characteristics of the wireless medium and is influenced by intervening obstacles and co-channel devices in the environment."
117,draft_0165,val,Mobile Security,Kali_Linux_Wireless_Pentesting_and_Security_for_Beginners_@VPPOfficial.txt,What is the command used to initiate the deauthentication process to disconnect the client from the original access point?,aireplay-ng --deauth 0 -a 64:66:B3:6E:B0:8A wlan0mon,"After proper authentication, victim will see the webpage while browsing and would probably enter the passphrase which s/he uses for the wireless access point. Our attack is now ready just wait for the client to connect and see the credentials coming. In most cases, client might be already connected to the original AP. You need to disconnect the client as we did in the previous chapters using aireplay-ng utility. Open the text file where you saved the AP info and enter: Syntax: aireplay-ng --deauth 0 -a [BSSID] wlan0mon aireplay-ng --deauth 0 -a 64:66:B3:6E:B0:8A wlan0mon 04:07:21 Waiting for beacon frame (BSSID: 64:66:B3:6E:B0:8A) on channel 1 NB: this attack is more effective when targeting a connected wireless client (-c <client's mac>). 04:07:22 Sending DeAuth to broadcast -- BSSID: [64:66:B3:6E:B0:8A] 04:07:22 Sending DeAuth to broadcast -- BSSID: [64:66:B3:6E:B0:8A] 04:07:23 Sending DeAuth to broadcast -- BSSID: [64:66:B3:6E:B0:8A] 04:07:24 Sending DeAuth to broadcast -- BSSID: [64:66:B3:6E:B0:8A] 04:07:24 Sending DeAuth to broadcast -- BSSID: [64:66:B3:6E:B0:8A] 04:07:25 Sending DeAuth to broadcast -- BSSID: [64:66:B3:6E:B0:8A] For unlimited de-authentication requests. Limit the request with any +ve integer Router MAC Address aka BSSID We are using 0 so that every client will disconnect from that specific BSSID and connect to our AP as it matches real AP ESSID and is open type network. As soon a client connects to your AP you will see an activity response in the airbase-ng terminal window like this: 03:58:11 Created tap interface at0 03:58:11 Trying to set MTU on at0 to 1500 03:58:11 Trying to set MTU on wlan0mon to 1800 03:58:11 Access Point with BSSID 00:C0:CA:5A:34:B6 started. 04:08:24 Client 7C:E9:D3:30:9F:F1 associated (unencrypted) to ESSID: ""rootsh3ll"" 04:08:36 Client 7C:E9:D3:30:9F:F1 associated (unencrypted) to ESSID: ""rootsh3ll"" To simulate the client-side, I am using Firefox browser running on Ubuntu machine connected through fake Wi-Fi access point",,,,,YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,Wi-Fi deauthentication saldırısı mobil cihaz güvenliğinden ziyade ağ güvenliği. Ayrıca örneğe özel MAC adresi genel eğitim yanıtında gerekli değil.,Network Security,1.00,"which s/he uses for the wireless access point. Our attack is now ready just wait for the client to connect and see the credentials coming. In most cases, client might be already connected to the original AP. You need to disconnect the client as we did in the previous chapters using aireplay-ng utility. Open the text file where you saved the AP info and enter: Syntax: aireplay-ng --deauth 0 -a [BSSID] wlan0mon aireplay-ng --deauth 0 -a 64:66:B3:6E:B0:8A wlan0mon 04:07:21 Waiting for beacon frame (BSSID: 64:66:B3:6E:B0:8A) on channel 1 NB: this a",YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,Wi-Fi deauthentication saldırısı mobil cihaz güvenliğinden ziyade ağ güvenliği. Ayrıca örneğe özel MAC adresi genel eğitim yanıtında gerekli değil.,Network Security,1.00,"which s/he uses for the wireless access point. Our attack is now ready just wait for the client to connect and see the credentials coming. In most cases, client might be already connected to the original AP. You need to disconnect the client as we did in the previous chapters using aireplay-ng utility. Open the text file where you saved the AP info and enter: Syntax: aireplay-ng --deauth 0 -a [BSSID] wlan0mon aireplay-ng --deauth 0 -a 64:66:B3:6E:B0:8A wlan0mon 04:07:21 Waiting for beacon frame (BSSID: 64:66:B3:6E:B0:8A) on channel 1 NB: this a"
118,batch1000_0809,val,Mobile Security,The Native Menace_ Host-to-Browser Cross-Boundary Attacks vi.txt,What type of attack can an attacker perform using the bidirectional modification attack on the Webex native host?,"An attacker can perform a bidirectional modification attack, which allows them to manipulate meeting parameters forcing the victim to launch an attacker-controlled meeting.","When the user navigates to a Webex link, the extension sends an unencrypted launch_meeting request to the native host, containing the meeting link and password. The API call also includes meeting and desktop app parameters. An attacker can use the bidirectional modification attack to manipulate meeting parameters forcing the victim to launch an attacker-controlled meeting. The attacker has control over participant metadata, display names, and other profile attributes, which can facilitate social engineering attacks often used in AI-powered deepfake scams [59]. The attacker can also modify critical app configuration settings, including thirdPartyLogin options, telemetry collection URLs, and app update URL paths. It is also possible to conduct a unidirectional Extension Imitation attack on the Webex native host, but it is less stealthy since the app prompts the user for consent when launching a meeting. Here we discuss certain aspects of our attacks and experimental findings, and the underlying issues, which inform our mitigation strategies discussed in the following section. Trust boundary crossing. By design, the native messaging protocol crosses trust boundaries. Browser extensions operate with elevated privileges granted by users, including the ability to read and modify web page content, intercept requests and responses, and interact with web APIs. Native apps, meanwhile, operate with access to system resources. When an extension communicates with a native host, it bridges the browser’s isolated execution environment with that of native apps. Trust boundaries operate on the assumption that the data and content within each boundary are trustworthy; however, trust is not inherently transferable across boundaries. Web extensions receive explicit user-approved access to web content at installation time, while native host apps receive explicit or implicit access to system resources. Incorrect assumptions about trust transferability can lead to the omission of necessar",,,,,YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,"Webex yerel host güvenlik açığı, mobil güvenlikten ziyade uygulama/sistem güvenliği.",System Security,0.79,"use the bidirectional modification attack to manipulate meeting parameters forcing the victim to launch an attacker-controlled meeting. The attacker has control over participant metadata, display names, and other profile attributes, which can facilitate social engineering attacks often used in AI-powered deepfake scams [59]. The attacker can also modify critical app configuration settings, including thirdPartyLogin options, telemetry collection URLs, and app update URL paths. It is also possible to conduct a unidirectional Extension Imitation a",YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,"Webex yerel host güvenlik açığı, mobil güvenlikten ziyade uygulama/sistem güvenliği.",System Security,0.79,"use the bidirectional modification attack to manipulate meeting parameters forcing the victim to launch an attacker-controlled meeting. The attacker has control over participant metadata, display names, and other profile attributes, which can facilitate social engineering attacks often used in AI-powered deepfake scams [59]. The attacker can also modify critical app configuration settings, including thirdPartyLogin options, telemetry collection URLs, and app update URL paths. It is also possible to conduct a unidirectional Extension Imitation a"
119,batch_new500_0359,test,Mobile Security,TrafficInstruct_ A Multi-Task Network Traffic Dataset and Be.txt,What type of traffic is excluded from the collection process?,"System-shared traffic, traffic with uncertain app ownership, and low-quality Flows are excluded from the collection process.","The common 48-Session-per-app collection plan and quality-based retries. The feedback arrow denotes missing or failed activities and unsuitable data, not a statistical drift detector or adaptation of app/network conditions. quality. Each Session retains its raw capture, app-operation events, and UID/socket records. Checks cover capture integrity, app-operation records, traffic-to-app matching, bidirectional Flow quality, and the generated JSONL format. Systemshared traffic, traffic with uncertain app ownership, and lowquality Flows are excluded; failed attempts are kept as audit information rather than SFT samples. The controller arranges additional attempts for missing or unsuccessful Sessions and unsuitable captures. No concept-drift detection algorithm or traffic-feature threshold is used to establish a drift target or trigger these retries. Collection uses Android 13/API 33 arm64 emulators hosted on Apple Silicon macOS. The device-side capture listens on any for TCP/UDP, writes a temporary PCAP, and transfers it to the host through ADB after capture. UID/socket observation runs in parallel with capture; fixed UI scripts start after capture initialization. Capture duration, behavior waiting time, and the registration’s nominal duration are different settings and are not presented as one universal time limit. App data and caches are retained; force-stopping and restarting an app does not clear its state. Pinduoduo uses the authorized user-provided login state, and YouTube uses the dedicated emulator/network arrangement rather than a claimed common direct route for all Operation checks use foreground activity, UI state, screenshots, action counts, or media-session playback state as appropriate. They do not prove full media download or completion of every remote interaction. UID ownership matches remote endpoints and time windows, using a one-second matching tolerance by default. Candidates require unique ownership, at least five packets, and at least one packet in ",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.70,"and UID/socket records. Checks cover capture integrity, app-operation records, traffic-to-app matching, bidirectional Flow quality, and the generated JSONL format. Systemshared traffic, traffic with uncertain app ownership, and lowquality Flows are excluded; failed attempts are kept as audit information rather than SFT samples. The controller arranges additional attempts for missing or unsuccessful Sessions and unsuitable captures. No concept-drift detection algorithm or traffic-feature threshold is used to establish a drift target or trigger t",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.70,"and UID/socket records. Checks cover capture integrity, app-operation records, traffic-to-app matching, bidirectional Flow quality, and the generated JSONL format. Systemshared traffic, traffic with uncertain app ownership, and lowquality Flows are excluded; failed attempts are kept as audit information rather than SFT samples. The controller arranges additional attempts for missing or unsuccessful Sessions and unsuitable captures. No concept-drift detection algorithm or traffic-feature threshold is used to establish a drift target or trigger t"
120,batch_new500_0481,test,Mobile Security,JavaScript Security.txt,What is the recommended method for preventing CSRF attacks when exposing an API to external apps?,"When exposing an API to external apps, the recommended method for preventing CSRF attacks is by creating your own app ID and app secret, similar to how major Internet companies like Google and Facebook handle it. This involves requiring developers to sign up for developing accounts and attach an application ID and secret key for each app. On the server side, you should verify these IDs and secrets before processing any API calls.","CSRF using the <img> tags This is a classic example. Consider the following instance: Should you load a site that contains this img tag, chances are that a piece of data may get deleted unknowingly. Now that we have covered the basics of preventing CSRF attacks through the use of CSRF tokens, the next question you may have is: what if there are times when you need to expose an API to an external app? For example, Facebook's Graph API, Twitter's API, and so on, allow external apps not only to read, but also write data to How do we prevent malicious attacks in this situation? We'll cover this and more in Other forms of protection Using CSRF tokens may be a convenient way to protect your app from CSRF attacks, but it can be a hassle at times. As mentioned in the previous section, what about the times when you need to expose an API to allow mobile access? Or, your app is growing so quickly that you want to accelerate that growth by creating a Graph API How do you manage it then? In this section, we will go quickly over the techniques for protection. Creating your own app ID and app secret – OAuth-styled Creating your own app ID and app secret is similar to what the major Internet companies are doing right now: we require developers to sign up for developing accounts and to attach an application ID and secret key for each of the apps. Using this information, the developers will need to exchange OAuth credentials in order to make any API calls, as shown in the following screenshot: Google requires developers to sign up, and it assigns the client ID Cross-site Request Forgery On the server end, all you need to do is look for the application ID and secret key; if it is not present, simply reject the request. Have a look at the following screenshot: The same thing with Facebook; Facebook requires you to sign up, and it assigns app ID and app secret Checking the Origin header Simply put, you want to check where the request is coming from. This is a technique where you can che",,,,,ELE_ADAYI,KRITIK,App ID/client secret tek başına CSRF savunması değildir; kaynak bağlamı OAuth API kimlik doğrulamaya ait. Soru ve cevap teknik açıdan karışmış.,,0.67,"over the techniques for protection. Creating your own app ID and app secret – OAuth-styled Creating your own app ID and app secret is similar to what the major Internet companies are doing right now: we require developers to sign up for developing accounts and to attach an application ID and secret key for each of the apps. Using this information, the developers will need to exchange OAuth credentials in order to make any API calls, as shown in the following screenshot: Google requires developers to sign up, and it assigns the client",ELE_ADAYI,KRITIK,App ID/client secret tek başına CSRF savunması değildir; kaynak bağlamı OAuth API kimlik doğrulamaya ait. Soru ve cevap teknik açıdan karışmış.,,0.67,"over the techniques for protection. Creating your own app ID and app secret – OAuth-styled Creating your own app ID and app secret is similar to what the major Internet companies are doing right now: we require developers to sign up for developing accounts and to attach an application ID and secret key for each of the apps. Using this information, the developers will need to exchange OAuth credentials in order to make any API calls, as shown in the following screenshot: Google requires developers to sign up, and it assigns the client"
121,draft_0242,train,Network Security,Secrets in Preamble_ Practical and Robust PHY Information Hi.txt,What is the maximum number of secret bits that the proposed method can reliably embed within Wi-Fi packets across diverse channel conditions?,46,"Secrets in Preamble: Practical and Robust PHY Information Hiding for Wi-Fi Rui Pan, Guanxiong Shen, Member, IEEE, Linning Peng, Member, IEEE, Xinyu Qi, Junqing Zhang, Senior Abstract—Physical layer (PHY) information hiding refers to the concealment of secrets within PHY waveforms without compromising the integrity of the primary communication link, thereby enabling critical applications such as covert communication, PHY steganography, and watermarking. The widespread deployment of Wi-Fi has motivated substantial research interest in hiding secrets within Wi-Fi waveforms. However, existing WiFi information hiding approaches suffer from two limitations: restricted steganographic capacity and insufficient robustness against environmental variations, both of which severely constrain practical deployment. This work proposes to embed secrets within the Wi-Fi legacy short training field (L-STF). The designed method preserves the autocorrelation properties of WiFi L-STF, ensuring that packet detection and coarse frequency offset compensation functionalities remain uncompromised. The secret embedding and extraction processes employ a pair of neural networks designed to learn optimal embedding rules and maximize steganographic capacity. We developed a prototype system and conducted comprehensive experiments across both indoor and outdoor environments. Experimental results show that our method can reliably embed 46 secret bits within Wi-Fi packets across diverse channel conditions, achieving significantly enhanced steganographic capacity and environmental robustness. The hardware prototype study validates the feasibility of the proposed Wi-Fi information hiding scheme. Index Terms—Physical layer security, wireless steganography, Wi-Fi, covert communication Physical layer (PHY) information hiding embeds secret information into wireless signals by deliberately introducing subtle and imperceptible distortions into PHY waveforms. This technique enables a range of critical applicat",,,,,KANIT_ZAYIF_ON_TARAMA,ORTA,Yanıttaki ayırt edici terimlerin bir bölümü 2.000 karakterlik kaynak pasajında görünmüyor; tam belgeyle semantik kontrol gerekli.,,0.00,"across both indoor and outdoor environments. Experimental results show that our method can reliably embed 46 secret bits within Wi-Fi packets across diverse channel conditions, achieving significantly enhanced steganographic capacity and environmental robustness. The hardware prototype study validates the feasibility of the proposed Wi-Fi information hiding scheme. Index Terms—Physical layer security, wireless steganography, Wi-Fi, covert communication Physical layer (PHY) information hiding embeds secret information into wireless signals by de",KANIT_ZAYIF_ON_TARAMA,ORTA,Yanıttaki ayırt edici terimlerin bir bölümü 2.000 karakterlik kaynak pasajında görünmüyor; tam belgeyle semantik kontrol gerekli.,,0.00,"across both indoor and outdoor environments. Experimental results show that our method can reliably embed 46 secret bits within Wi-Fi packets across diverse channel conditions, achieving significantly enhanced steganographic capacity and environmental robustness. The hardware prototype study validates the feasibility of the proposed Wi-Fi information hiding scheme. Index Terms—Physical layer security, wireless steganography, Wi-Fi, covert communication Physical layer (PHY) information hiding embeds secret information into wireless signals by de"
122,batch1000_0846,train,Network Security,WiFi.HackProofYourWirelessNetwork.txt,What measures did NASA take to secure their wireless network by segmenting it and utilizing specific security protocols?,"NASA took several measures to secure their wireless network. They deployed a firewall segmenting their wireless and department networks. They hardened their wireless interfaces by using the strongest encryption available, disabling Source ID broadcast, and only allowing authorized MAC addresses on the wireless network. They utilized the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) on the firewall and disabled it on their Access Point to define which MAC addresses could receive an IP address and set the lease lifetime of the IP address. They also turned off all routing and forwarding between the wireless interface and the internal network. Additionally, they required users to authenticate via an SSL-protected Web site on the firewall server before gaining access to network resources, with authentication performed","This is what was done earlier this year by two researchers at NASA (for more tect their infrastructure, but still provide access through wireless, they deployed a ﬁrewall segmenting their wireless and department network.They most likely hardened their wireless interfaces to the extent of the equipments’ possibilities by utilizing the strongest encryption available to them, disabling SID broadcast, and only allowing authorized MAC addresses on the wireless network. They then utilized the Dynamic Host Conﬁguration Protocol (DHCP) on the ﬁrewall, and disabled it on their Access Point.This allowed them to expressly deﬁne which MAC addresses could receive an IP address, and what the lease lifetime of the IP address would be. The researchers then went on to turn off all routing and forwarding between the wireless interface and the internal network. If anyone happened to be able to A Security Primer • Chapter 2 connect to the wireless network, they would still have no access to the rest of the computing resources of the department.Anyone wishing to gain further access would have to go to an SSL protected Web site on the ﬁrewall server and authenticate as a valid user.The Web server would authenticate the user against a local RADIUS server, but they could have easily used any other form of user authentication (NT, SecurID, and so on). Once the user was properly authenticated, the ﬁrewall would change the ﬁrewall rules for the IP address that user was supposed to be assigned to, allowing full access to only the network resources they are authorized to access. Finally, once the lease expired or was released for any reason from the DHCP assigned IP address, the ﬁrewall rules would be removed and that user and their IP would have to reauthenticate through the Web interface to allow access to the network resources again. They have yet to release the actual implementation procedure they used, so again it is up to us, the users of wireless networks, to provide proper controls arou",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.77,"wireless interfaces to the extent of the equipments’ possibilities by utilizing the strongest encryption available to them, disabling SID broadcast, and only allowing authorized MAC addresses on the wireless network. They then utilized the Dynamic Host Conﬁguration Protocol (DHCP) on the ﬁrewall, and disabled it on their Access Point.This allowed them to expressly deﬁne which MAC addresses could receive an IP address, and what the lease lifetime of the IP address would be. The researchers then went on to turn off all routing and forwarding betw",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.77,"wireless interfaces to the extent of the equipments’ possibilities by utilizing the strongest encryption available to them, disabling SID broadcast, and only allowing authorized MAC addresses on the wireless network. They then utilized the Dynamic Host Conﬁguration Protocol (DHCP) on the ﬁrewall, and disabled it on their Access Point.This allowed them to expressly deﬁne which MAC addresses could receive an IP address, and what the lease lifetime of the IP address would be. The researchers then went on to turn off all routing and forwarding betw"
123,kaggle1000_0939,train,Network Security,CEH v9 Certified Ethical Hacker Version 9.txt,What is the minimum height required for a fence to deter a determined intruder?,A fence should be at least 8 feet tall to deter a determined intruder from entering.,"C. A fence should be at least 8 feet tall to deter a determined intruder from entering a 4. C. A bollard is a barrier that prevents cars and trucks from passing it to enter a facility. 5. A. Liability is a huge issue for dogs and security considering the fact that they may attack and cannot discern attackers without human intervention. 6. D. Mantraps are intended to control access and thus stop the occurrence of physical breaches as a result of piggybacking and tailgating. 7. B. A false wall is one that extends only to a drop ceiling and not to the actual ceiling. These types of walls can easily be climbed over to allow access to a previously 8. A. Defense in depth provides much better protection than a single layer. It also provides a means to slow down and frustrate an attacker. 9. B. Bollards can be used with fences to block a vehicle from crashing through a fence and making an easy-to-use entrance. 10. A. A type 2 error is also known as a false rejection error, where someone who should have had access was denied it. 11. B. Fingerprint systems are becoming more common on laptops and portable devices. They can also be used to authenticate individuals for access to facilities. 12. D. Audio and visual components are both vital. 13. D. A pick and a tension wrench are the minimum equipment included in a lock-pick 14. A. A publicly available phone number is not a security risk in many cases because the machine may be one that can be sent information from anywhere. 15. A. A drop ceiling is a false ceiling. 16. B. Portals are more commonly referred to by the term mantrap. 17. C. A cipher lock is a form of combination lock that requires a code to be entered in order to open the door. 18. A. Fences in many cases are the first line of defense that an intruder would encounter. 19. C. Encryption of an entire drive dilutes the value of the data if the drive is subject to theft; however, it will not keep the drive from being physically stolen. 20. B. Flash drives offer several ",,,,,ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,Fiziksel çit yüksekliği ağ güvenliği veri setinin teknik kapsamı dışında.,,1.00,C. A fence should be at least 8 feet tall to deter a determined intruder from entering a 4. C. A bollard is a barrier that prevents cars and trucks from passing it to enter a facility. 5. A. Liability is a huge issue for dogs and security considering the fact that they may attack and cannot discern attackers without human intervention. 6. D. Mantraps are intended to control access and thus stop the occurrence of physical breaches as a result of piggybacking and tailgating. 7. B.,ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,Fiziksel çit yüksekliği ağ güvenliği veri setinin teknik kapsamı dışında.,,1.00,C. A fence should be at least 8 feet tall to deter a determined intruder from entering a 4. C. A bollard is a barrier that prevents cars and trucks from passing it to enter a facility. 5. A. Liability is a huge issue for dogs and security considering the fact that they may attack and cannot discern attackers without human intervention. 6. D. Mantraps are intended to control access and thus stop the occurrence of physical breaches as a result of piggybacking and tailgating. 7. B.
124,kaggle1000_0523,train,Network Security,Computer Security Division 2010 Annual Report.txt,"What specific IETF RFC documents were added to the revised version of SP 800-81, Secure Domain Name System (DNS) Deployment Guide, published in April 2010?","The specific IETF RFC documents added to the revised version of SP 800-81, Secure Domain Name System (DNS) Deployment Guide, published in April 2010 include:

- DNNSEC Operational Practices (RFC 4641)
- Automated Updates for DNS Security (DNSSEC) Trust Anchors (RFC 5011)
- DNSSecurity (DNSSEC) Hashed Authenticated Denial of Existence (RFC 5155)
- HMAC SHA TSIG Algorithm Identifiers (RFC 463","To counter these threats, the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) developed a set of specifications for securing DNS called DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC) to provide origin au­ thentication and data integrity for all responses from the DNS. In partnership with the Department of Homeland Security, NIST has been actively involved in promoting the deployment of DNSSEC Computer Security Division Annual Report – 2010 The significant achievements in FY2010 are as follows: Publication of the revised version of SP 800-81 Revision 1, Secure Domain Name System (DNS) Deployment Guide, in April 2010, after two rounds of public comments. The major additions/changes in this revision are the following: Updated recommendations for all cryptographic opera­ tions relating to digital signing of DNS records, verifica­ tion of the signatures, Zone Transfer, Dynamic Updates, Key Management and Authenticated Denial of Exis­ The addition of IETF RFC documents that have formed the basis for the updated recommendations including: DNNSEC Operational Practices (RFC 4641), Automated Updates for DNS Security (DNSSEC) Trust Anchors (RFC 5011),DNSSecurity(DNSSEC)HashedAuthenticatedDe­ nial of Existence (RFC 5155) and HMAC SHA TSIG Algo­ rithm Identifiers (RFC 4635). Additional FIPS standards and NIST guidelines incorpo­ rated into the updated recommendations including: FIPS 198-1, Keyed-Hash Message Authentication Code (HMAC), FIPS 186-3, Digital Signature Standard, and SP 800-57 Part 1 and Part 3, Recommendations for Key using DNS Software offering NSD, in addition to Berkeley Internet Name Domain (BIND); Guidelines on procedures for migrating to a new Cryptographic Algorithm for signing of the Zone Guidelines for procedures for migrating to Next Se­ cure 3 (NSEC3) specifications from NSEC for providing authenticated denial of existence (Section 11.6); Deployment Guidelines for Split-Zone under different scenarios (Section 11.7). Assisting GSA in deploying DNSSEC on the .gov Top Level Domain",,,,,DUZELT,YUKSEK,"PDF OCR kaynağındaki yazım bozukluğu yanıtı etkilemiş (DNNSEC, DNSSecurity). Ayrıca son RFC maddesi cevapta kesilmiş.",,0.86,"Domain Name System (DNS) Deployment Guide, in April 2010, after two rounds of public comments. The major additions/changes in this revision are the following: Updated recommendations for all cryptographic opera­ tions relating to digital signing of DNS records, verifica­ tion of the signatures, Zone Transfer, Dynamic Updates, Key Management and Authenticated Denial of Exis­ The addition of IETF RFC documents that have formed the basis for the updated recommendations including: DNNSEC Operational Practices (RFC 4641), Automated Updates for DNS S",DUZELT,YUKSEK,"PDF OCR kaynağındaki yazım bozukluğu yanıtı etkilemiş (DNNSEC, DNSSecurity). Ayrıca son RFC maddesi cevapta kesilmiş.",,0.86,"Domain Name System (DNS) Deployment Guide, in April 2010, after two rounds of public comments. The major additions/changes in this revision are the following: Updated recommendations for all cryptographic opera­ tions relating to digital signing of DNS records, verifica­ tion of the signatures, Zone Transfer, Dynamic Updates, Key Management and Authenticated Denial of Exis­ The addition of IETF RFC documents that have formed the basis for the updated recommendations including: DNNSEC Operational Practices (RFC 4641), Automated Updates for DNS S"
