126,draft_0244,train,Network Security,Reddit Community Signals as a Supplementary Domain-Reputatio.txt,What percentage of the SpamAssassin Phishing Email Dataset is classified as legitimate (ham) emails?,70.3%,"Moreover, the integration of graph-based analysis has enabled reputation systems to account for network relationships among senders, recipients, and domains. However, these sophisticated approaches require substantial computational resources and continuous model updates. Community-based signals offer a potential complementary solution through their inherent adaptability to emerging threats with lower operational overhead. 3.1 Experimental Framework This study employs a comparative experimental framework designed to isolate the contribution of community consensus signals within email spam detection. The research design employs a tiered feature configuration approach, progressively augmenting baseline models with psychological scoring mechanisms and, finally, with Reddit-based community intelligence. This stratified methodology allows for precise attribution of performance gains to specific signal types rather than general model complexity. To ensure robustness against algorithmic bias, we evaluate findings across three distinct machine learning paradigms: Logistic Regression (LR) for linear interpretability, Random Forest (RF) for non-linear feature interactions, and Support Vector Machines (SVMs) for geometric validation of class boundaries. By triangulating results across these diverse architectures, we verify that observed improvements reflect genuine signal utility rather than artifacts of a specific model’s inductive bias. 3.2 Dataset Characteristics The primary dataset consists of the SpamAssassin Phishing Email Dataset [24], preprocessed for tabular analysis via Kaggle [25]. The final corpus comprises 5,582 emails from the early 2000s following rigorous cleaning protocols. The data exhibits a class imbalance of 70.3% legitimate (ham) and 29.7% spam, nec