139,batch500_0142,train,Penetration Testing,sec_2609.08093v1_LLM_Based_Penetration_Testing_in_th.txt,What is the formalized problem presented in the paper regarding LLM attack agents' interactions with potential targets?,"The formalized problem presented in the paper regarding LLM attack agents' interactions with potential targets is a budgeted decision process where an agent interacts with potential targets, consumes LLM execution budget during reconnaissance and exploitation, and must decide whether to engage (continue exploitation) or disengage (skip) when honeypot suspicion arises.","LLM-Based Penetration Testing in the Presence of Honeypots Xinhong Xie, Piyush Nagasubramaniam, Neeraj Karamchandani, and Sencun Zhu The Pennsylvania State University Large language model (LLM) agents are increasingly employed for offensive cybersecurity tasks such as automated vulnerability discovery, reconnaissance, and penetration testing. This new capability also threatens one of the defender’s most valuable tools: deception. Traditional honeypots rely on realism and obscurity to lure human or script-driven attackers into revealing tactics, techniques, and procedures (TTPs), but LLM-driven attackers can reason about heterogeneous artifacts and use the honeypot suspicion to guide target-selection We present a systematic study of honeypot-aware budget allocation for LLM attack agents. We formalize the attacker’s problem as a budgeted decision process: an agent interacts with potential targets, consuming LLM execution budget during reconnaissance and exploitation, and must decide whether to engage (continue exploitation) or disengage (skip) when honeypot suspicion arises. Our findings show that with the proposed detector-guided policy, LLM agent attackers can effectively allocate budget to compromise hosts in a host pool, highlighting the importance of dynamically allocating budget in a controlled mixedhost testbed. While defenses are beyond our present scope, we discuss implications for future adversarially resilient and adaptive honeypot design. Deception has long been an important role in computer security. Honeypots and honeynets—controlled decoy systems designed to lure, engage, and study attackers—allow defenders to collect threat intelligence, observe adversarial tactics, techniques, and procedures (TTPs), and consume attacker resources. Traditionally, the effectiveness of such systems has depended heavily on their ability to appear sufficiently realistic to avoid detection and sustain attacker interaction. LLM agents introduce a different attacker model. Un",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.83,"artifacts and use the honeypot suspicion to guide target-selection We present a systematic study of honeypot-aware budget allocation for LLM attack agents. We formalize the attacker’s problem as a budgeted decision process: an agent interacts with potential targets, consuming LLM execution budget during reconnaissance and exploitation, and must decide whether to engage (continue exploitation) or disengage (skip) when honeypot suspicion arises. Our findings show that with the proposed detector-guided policy, LLM agent attackers can effectively a",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.83,"artifacts and use the honeypot suspicion to guide target-selection We present a systematic study of honeypot-aware budget allocation for LLM attack agents. We formalize the attacker’s problem as a budgeted decision process: an agent interacts with potential targets, consuming LLM execution budget during reconnaissance and exploitation, and must decide whether to engage (continue exploitation) or disengage (skip) when honeypot suspicion arises. Our findings show that with the proposed detector-guided policy, LLM agent attackers can effectively a"
140,batch500_0367,train,Penetration Testing,ARBITRA_ Robust Document-Level ATT_CK Extraction via Multi-E.txt,What is the name of the backbone selection method used for the TRAM2 benchmark?,The backbone selection method used for the TRAM2 benchmark is pilot single-pass evaluation on the training split.,"It first reconstructs the attack progression in chronological and causal order without technique labels, describing attacker actions, affected resources, and dependencies. Gold techniques are then mapped to the reconstructed phases with explicit justification. The three experts jointly form EFT = {eanchor,econtrast,einference}. Their complementary evidential perspectives provide epistemic diversity during verification: support and attack arguments for the same candidate arise from distinct reasoning structures rather than near-duplicate reasoning styles. We empirically evaluate this property in our experiments. Backbone selection. Expert backbones are selected per benchmark (TRAM2 and AnnoCTR) through a pilot singlepass evaluation on the training split. GPT-4.1-mini performs consistently better on AnnoCTR, so all three experts use it (‡). On TRAM2, GPT-4o-mini and GPT-4.1-mini are both competitive; the best-performing pool uses one GPT-4.1-mini and two GPT-4o-mini experts (†). Adversarial CTI Perturbations To evaluate robustness beyond clean benchmark data, we construct three adversarial variants of the TRAM2 evaluation split, one for each of the adversary capability classes from Section 3.3 (C3). The gold label set is preserved in all three cases, so performance changes measure extractor fragility to text-level manipulation. Conflicting TTP statement injection: Insert plausible analyst-style statements that mention the same malware, actor, or campaign context but attribute behavior to techniques not present in the gold label set. This tests whether a model accepts plausible but unsupported technique claims. Cross-report contamination: Interleave unlabeled sentences sampled from unrelated TRAM2 reports. This tests whether an extractor is distracted by contaminated CTI context. Targeted semantic poisoning: Rewrite labeled sentences to preserve the same malicious behavior while weakening entitysignaling keywords, tool names, or phrases. This tests whether the model ca",,,,,YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,"TRAM2 CTI benchmark model seçimi, penetrasyon testi kavramı değil.",Incident Response,0.80,"distinct reasoning structures rather than near-duplicate reasoning styles. We empirically evaluate this property in our experiments. Backbone selection. Expert backbones are selected per benchmark (TRAM2 and AnnoCTR) through a pilot singlepass evaluation on the training split. GPT-4.1-mini performs consistently better on AnnoCTR, so all three experts use it (‡). On TRAM2, GPT-4o-mini and GPT-4.1-mini are both competitive; the best-performing pool uses one GPT-4.1-mini and two GPT-4o-mini experts (†). Adversarial CTI Perturbations To evaluate ro",YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,"TRAM2 CTI benchmark model seçimi, penetrasyon testi kavramı değil.",Incident Response,0.80,"distinct reasoning structures rather than near-duplicate reasoning styles. We empirically evaluate this property in our experiments. Backbone selection. Expert backbones are selected per benchmark (TRAM2 and AnnoCTR) through a pilot singlepass evaluation on the training split. GPT-4.1-mini performs consistently better on AnnoCTR, so all three experts use it (‡). On TRAM2, GPT-4o-mini and GPT-4.1-mini are both competitive; the best-performing pool uses one GPT-4.1-mini and two GPT-4o-mini experts (†). Adversarial CTI Perturbations To evaluate ro"
141,batch1000_0570,train,Penetration Testing,"Patrick Engebretson The Basics of Hacking and Penetration Testing, Second Edition (2013).txt",What is the default username and password for the login prompt in WebGoat?,The default username and password for the login prompt in WebGoat are both set to: guest.,"Most lessons require you to perform a certain attack like using Web-Based Exploitation SQL injection to bypass authentication. Each lesson comes complete with hints that will help you solve the puzzle. As with other scenario-driven exercises, it is important to work hard and attempt to ﬁnd the answer on your own before using the help ﬁles. If you are making use of virtual machines in your hacking lab, you will need to download and install WebGoat inside a virtual machine. As discussed previously, WebGoat will run in either Linux or Windows, just be sure to install Java (JRE) on your system prior to starting WebGoat. owasp.org/. The ﬁle you download will require 7zip or a program capable of unzipping a 7z ﬁle. Unzip the ﬁle and remember the location of the uncompressed WebGoat folder. If you are running WebGoat on Windows, you can navigate to the unzipped WebGoat folder and locate the “webgoat_8080.bat” ﬁle. Execute this batch ﬁle by double clicking it. A terminal window will appear; you will need to leave this window open and running in order for WebGoat to function properly. At this point, assuming that you are accessing WebGoat from the same machine you are running the WebGoat server on, you can begin using WebGoat by opening If everything went properly, you will be presented with a login prompt. Both the username and password are set to: guest. As a ﬁnal note, please pay attention to the warnings posted in the “readme” ﬁle. Speciﬁcally you should understand that running WebGoat outside of a lab environment is extremely dangerous, as your system will be vulnerable to attacks. Always use caution and only run WebGoat in a properly sandboxed You can also download and install Damn Vulnerable Web App (DVWA) from that utilizes PHP and MySQL to provide you with a testing environment. Ashasbeenpointedoutseveral times, there islittle doubtthatthisattackvectorwill continue to grow. Once you have mastered the basics we discussed in this section, you should expand your knowle",,,,,KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,"""guest/guest"" yalnızca kaynakta anlatılan eski WebGoat kurulumuna özgü; sürüme göre değişebilir.",,0.89,"properly, you will be presented with a login prompt. Both the username and password are set to: guest. As a ﬁnal note, please pay attention to the warnings posted in the “readme” ﬁle. Speciﬁcally you should understand that running WebGoat outside of a lab environment is extremely dangerous, as your system will be vulnerable to attacks. Always use caution and only run WebGoat in a properly sandboxed You can also download and install Damn Vulnerable Web App (DVWA) from that utilizes PHP and MySQL to provide you with a testing environment. Ashasbe",KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,"""guest/guest"" yalnızca kaynakta anlatılan eski WebGoat kurulumuna özgü; sürüme göre değişebilir.",,0.89,"properly, you will be presented with a login prompt. Both the username and password are set to: guest. As a ﬁnal note, please pay attention to the warnings posted in the “readme” ﬁle. Speciﬁcally you should understand that running WebGoat outside of a lab environment is extremely dangerous, as your system will be vulnerable to attacks. Always use caution and only run WebGoat in a properly sandboxed You can also download and install Damn Vulnerable Web App (DVWA) from that utilizes PHP and MySQL to provide you with a testing environment. Ashasbe"
142,batch1000_0199,train,Penetration Testing,sec_2410.03225v2_AutoPenBench__Benchmarking_Generati.txt,What percentage of the time does the agent correctly identify the appropriate exploit in the Metasploit framework?,83%,"GeoServer or Bludit). When the agent successfully completes the second phase, it shows a relatively high proficiency in vulnerability detection by correctly identifying the appropriate exploit in the Metasploit framework 83% of the time. However, the final stage of exploit execution reveals a critical weakness in the agent capabilities – i.e. it fails to correctly configure the exploit parameters 40% of the time (20% on the entire set of 11 tasks). All in all, our results underscore the complex nature of autonomous penetration testing and highlight areas requiring further development. While the agent demonstrates proficiency in certain aspects such as network discovery and basic vulnerability detection, it struggles with advanced exploitation techniques and consistent web server interactions. The introduction of the assisted agent approach yields several notable advantages compared to the autonomous one, solving three times as many tasks (SR of 64% compared to 21%). One of the main advantages of the assisted agent is the split of the main task into smaller sub-tasks. By breaking down the problem space, the assisted agent can better maintain focus and tackle each sub-task more efficiently. Additionally, the systematic cleaning of the agent scratchpad at the completion of each sub-task (cf. line 6 of Algorithm 2) helps to reduce the amount of uninformative text fed as input to the LLM, improving its contextual awareness. Despite these advantages, the assisted agent still fails to accomplish 12 out of 33 tasks. Similarly to the autonomous agent, the assisted one fails to detect the vulnerability in AC1 and the content filter in WS2. Even though it can now solve the easy SQL injection task (WS3), it still struggles in completing the attack on the UPDATE statement of (WS4). The agent repeatedly attempts an attack on a SELECT statement, failing to adapt its approach effectively. The challenges are not limited to SQL injection; although the agent identifies the injection p",,,,,KANIT_ZAYIF_ON_TARAMA,ORTA,Yanıttaki ayırt edici terimlerin bir bölümü 2.000 karakterlik kaynak pasajında görünmüyor; tam belgeyle semantik kontrol gerekli.,,0.00,"proficiency in vulnerability detection by correctly identifying the appropriate exploit in the Metasploit framework 83% of the time. However, the final stage of exploit execution reveals a critical weakness in the agent capabilities – i.e. it fails to correctly configure the exploit parameters 40% of the time (20% on the entire set of 11 tasks). All in all, our results underscore the complex nature of autonomous penetration testing and highlight areas requiring further development. While the agent demonstrates proficiency in certain aspects suc",KANIT_ZAYIF_ON_TARAMA,ORTA,Yanıttaki ayırt edici terimlerin bir bölümü 2.000 karakterlik kaynak pasajında görünmüyor; tam belgeyle semantik kontrol gerekli.,,0.00,"proficiency in vulnerability detection by correctly identifying the appropriate exploit in the Metasploit framework 83% of the time. However, the final stage of exploit execution reveals a critical weakness in the agent capabilities – i.e. it fails to correctly configure the exploit parameters 40% of the time (20% on the entire set of 11 tasks). All in all, our results underscore the complex nature of autonomous penetration testing and highlight areas requiring further development. While the agent demonstrates proficiency in certain aspects suc"
143,batch1000_0819,train,Penetration Testing,DYNAS_ A Dynamic Network Penetration Testing Framework Using.txt,What percentage increase in reward per step does Pen-DHRL achieve compared to the next-best baseline when IDS is disabled during training and IDS is enabled during evaluation on the Bridge scenario?,Approximately 64%,"DYNAS: A Dynamic Network Penetration Testing Framework Using Deep Hierarchical Reinforcement Md. Rakibul Hassan1, Majedul Islam1, Md. Ashraful Alam1, Muhammad Iqbal Hossain1, and Swakkhar Shatabda1 Accepted at the NSS 2026: 20th International Conference on Network and System Security (submission version) BRAC University, Bangladesh Abstract. As network scale and uncertainty complicate automated penetration testing, reinforcement learning (RL) environments must support controlled evaluation under defensive and non-stationary conditions. We present DYNAS (Dynamic Network Attack Simulator), which combines parameterized large-scale scenario generation with stateful intrusion detection responses, explicit scan false-positive and false-negative noise, temporary service unavailability, and duration-based action timeouts. We further introduce Pen-DHRL, a recurrent manager–worker policy that selects persistent subgoals using IDS-conditioned global context. We compare Pen-DHRL with GNN, Invariant, and Attention baselines across four scenario variants and multiple training regimes. Under IDS-disabled training followed by IDS-enabled evaluation, Pen-DHRL obtains approximately 64% and 24% higher reward per step on Bridge and Dynamic, respectively, and approximately 26% and 30% more captured hosts on VarA and VarB than the next-best baseline. With all defensive dynamics active from the beginning of training, PenDHRL achieves positive mean episode returns in all four scenarios, whereas the flat baselines produce negative mean returns in all 12 architecture–scenario combinations; staged curriculum training produces positive mean returns across all 16 evaluated combinations. Keywords: Automated penetration testing · AI for security · Hierarchical reinforcement learning · IDS-aware attack planning · Network attack simulation · Automated security assessment provides repeatability and broad coverage, but automatically generated findings can require expert validation, whereas fully manu",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"and multiple training regimes. Under IDS-disabled training followed by IDS-enabled evaluation, Pen-DHRL obtains approximately 64% and 24% higher reward per step on Bridge and Dynamic, respectively, and approximately 26% and 30% more captured hosts on VarA and VarB than the next-best baseline. With all defensive dynamics active from the beginning of training, PenDHRL achieves positive mean episode returns in all four scenarios, whereas the flat baselines produce negative mean returns in all 12 architecture–scenario combinations; staged curriculu",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"and multiple training regimes. Under IDS-disabled training followed by IDS-enabled evaluation, Pen-DHRL obtains approximately 64% and 24% higher reward per step on Bridge and Dynamic, respectively, and approximately 26% and 30% more captured hosts on VarA and VarB than the next-best baseline. With all defensive dynamics active from the beginning of training, PenDHRL achieves positive mean episode returns in all four scenarios, whereas the flat baselines produce negative mean returns in all 12 architecture–scenario combinations; staged curriculu"
144,kaggle1000_0998,train,Penetration Testing,Google.Hacking.for.Penetration.Testers.Volume2.txt,What year did Roelof Temmingh complete his Electronic Engineering degree?,1995,"Thank you for clicking through our Amazon links to fund charities. Thank you for giving us a platform to affect real change, not only in the security community but also in the world at large. I am truly humbled by your support. Roelof Temmingh Born in South Africa, Roelof studied at the University of Pretoria and completed his Electronic Engineering degree in 1995. His passion for computer security had by then caught up with him and manifested itself in various forms. He worked as developer, and later as a system architect at an information security engineering ﬁrm from 1995 to 2000. In early 2000 he founded the security assessment and consulting ﬁrm SensePost along with some of the leading thinkers in the ﬁeld. During his time at SensePost he was the Technical Director in charge of the assessment team and later headed the Innovation Centre for the company. Roelof has spoken at various international conferences such as Blackhat, Defcon, Cansecwest, RSA, Ruxcon, and FIRST. He has contributed to books such as Stealing the Network: How to Own a Continent, Penetration Tester’s Open Contributing Authors Source Toolkit, and was one of the lead trainers in the “Hacking by Numbers” training course. Roelof has authored several well known security testing applications like Wikto, Crowbar, BiDiBLAH and Suru.At the start of 2007 he founded Paterva in order to pursue R&D in his own capacity. At Paterva Roelof developed an application called Evolution (now called Maltego) that has shown tremendous promise in the ﬁeld of information collection and correlation. Petko “pdp” D. Petkov is a senior IT security consultant based in London, United Kingdom. His day-to-day work involves identifying vulnerabilities, building attack strategies and creating attack tools and penetration testing infrastructures. Petko is known in the underground circles as pdp or architect but his name is well known in the IT security industry for his strong technical background and creative thinking. He has bee",,,,,ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,Bir araştırmacının üniversite mezuniyet yılı penetrasyon testi becerisi ölçmüyor.,,0.00,"humbled by your support. Roelof Temmingh Born in South Africa, Roelof studied at the University of Pretoria and completed his Electronic Engineering degree in 1995. His passion for computer security had by then caught up with him and manifested itself in various forms. He worked as developer, and later as a system architect at an information security engineering ﬁrm from 1995 to 2000. In early 2000 he founded the security assessment and consulting ﬁrm SensePost along with some of the leading thinkers in the ﬁeld. During his time at SensePost he",ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,Bir araştırmacının üniversite mezuniyet yılı penetrasyon testi becerisi ölçmüyor.,,0.00,"humbled by your support. Roelof Temmingh Born in South Africa, Roelof studied at the University of Pretoria and completed his Electronic Engineering degree in 1995. His passion for computer security had by then caught up with him and manifested itself in various forms. He worked as developer, and later as a system architect at an information security engineering ﬁrm from 1995 to 2000. In early 2000 he founded the security assessment and consulting ﬁrm SensePost along with some of the leading thinkers in the ﬁeld. During his time at SensePost he"
145,extra_20261108_7011,train,Penetration Testing,wstg-v4.2.txt,What phase of the SDLC does the application penetration testing fall under according to the provided source?,The application penetration testing falls under Phase 4 during the Deployment Phase of the SDLC.,"For more details on OWASP checklists, please refer to the latest edition of the OWASP Top 10. Phase 4 During Deployment Phase 4.1 Application Penetration Testing Having tested the requirements, analyzed the design, and performed code review, it might be assumed that all issues have been caught. Hopefully this is the case, but penetration testing the application after it has been deployed provides an additional check to ensure that nothing has been missed. Phase 4.2 Conﬁguration Management Testing The application penetration test should include an examination of how the infrastructure was deployed and secured. It is important to review conﬁguration aspects, no matter how small, to ensure that none are left at a default setting that may be vulnerable to exploitation. Web Security Testing Guide v4.2 Phase 5 During Maintenance and Operations Phase 5.1 Conduct Operational Management Reviews There needs to be a process in place which details how the operational side of both the application and infrastructure Phase 5.2 Conduct Periodic Health Checks Monthly or quarterly health checks should be performed on both the application and infrastructure to ensure no new security risks have been introduced and that the level of security is still intact. Phase 5.3 Ensure Change Veriﬁcation After every change has been approved and tested in the QA environment and deployed into the production environment, it is vital that the change is checked to ensure that the level of security has not been affected by the change. This should be integrated into the change management process. A Typical SDLC Testing Workﬂow The following ﬁgure shows a typical SDLC Testing Workﬂow. Web Security Testing Guide v4.2 Web Security Testing Guide v4.2 Penetration Testing Methodologies OWASP Testing Guides Web Security Testing Guide (WSTG) Mobile Security Testing Guide (MSTG) Firmware Security Testing Methodology Penetration Testing Execution Standard PCI Penetration Testing Guide PCI DSS Penetration Testing Guidance PCI DSS Penetration Testing Requirements Penetration Testing Framework Technical Guide to Information Security Testing and Assessment Open Source Security Testing Methodology Manual OWASP Testing Guides In terms of technical security testing execution, the OWASP testing guides are highly recommended. Depending on the types of the applications, the testing guides are listed below for the web/cloud services, Mobile app (Android/iOS), or IoT ﬁrmware respectively. OWASP Web Security Testing Guide OWASP Mobile Security Testing Guide OWASP Firmware Security Testing Methodology Penetration Testing Execution Standard Penetration Testing Execution Standard (PTES) deﬁnes penetration testing as 7 phases. Particularly, PTES Technical Guidelines give hands-on suggestions on testing procedures, and recommendation for security testing tools.",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.86,"10. Phase 4 During Deployment Phase 4.1 Application Penetration Testing Having tested the requirements, analyzed the design, and performed code review, it might be assumed that all issues have been caught. Hopefully this is the case, but penetration testing the application after it has been deployed provides an additional check to ensure that nothing has been missed. Phase 4.2 Conﬁguration Management Testing The application penetration test should include an examination of how the infrastructure was deployed and secured. It is important to revi",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.86,"10. Phase 4 During Deployment Phase 4.1 Application Penetration Testing Having tested the requirements, analyzed the design, and performed code review, it might be assumed that all issues have been caught. Hopefully this is the case, but penetration testing the application after it has been deployed provides an additional check to ensure that nothing has been missed. Phase 4.2 Conﬁguration Management Testing The application penetration test should include an examination of how the infrastructure was deployed and secured. It is important to revi"
146,batch_new500_0268,val,Penetration Testing,sec_2407.17346v1_Insider_Threats_Mitigation__Role_of.txt,"What type of penetration testing approach is characterized by testers having complete access to source code, network diagrams, and configuration files, and complete system knowledge?",White-Box Testing,"Williams and Smith’s (2020) empirical study emphasizes the importance of penetration testing in lowering the danger of insider threats. The study looks at how frequent penetration testing affects an organization’s security posture in a variety of industries. The results show that companies that regularly perform penetration tests encounter fewer insider threat events than those that don’t [7]. The reduction in insider exploits of security flaws is attributed by the study to proactive detection and mitigation of vulnerabilities [8]. While the literature provides a strong foundation for understanding the importance and effectiveness of penetration testing, it is equally crucial to delve into the specific methodologies employed. Methodologies for penetration testing differ according to the objectives, scope, and available resources. The approaches used to address insider threats can be broadly categorized as follows: black-box, white-box, and gray-box A. Black-Box Testing With this method, testing is done without any prior understanding of the system’s core operations. Testers act as low-privilege insiders or external attackers looking to obtain unapproved access. Although black-box testing can be helpful in locating security holes in publicly accessible systems and services, it might not be able to reveal more serious insiderB. White-Box Testing This method, which is often referred to as ”clear-box testing,” gives testers complete access to source code, network diagrams, and configuration files, as well as complete system knowledge. White-box testing makes it possible to thoroughly examine the system from the point of view of an insider, revealing flaws that an insider with a high privilege could With this method, which combines white-box and black-box testing, testers only have a limited understanding of the system. By simulating situations in which an insider has restricted but considerable access, gray-box testing seeks to uncover vulnerabilities that might be take",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"White-Box Testing This method, which is often referred to as ”clear-box testing,” gives testers complete access to source code, network diagrams, and configuration files, as well as complete system knowledge. White-box testing makes it possible to thoroughly examine the system from the point of view of an insider, revealing flaws that an insider with a high privilege could With this method, which combines white-box and black-box testing, testers only have a limited understanding of the system. By simulating situations in which an insider has re",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"White-Box Testing This method, which is often referred to as ”clear-box testing,” gives testers complete access to source code, network diagrams, and configuration files, as well as complete system knowledge. White-box testing makes it possible to thoroughly examine the system from the point of view of an insider, revealing flaws that an insider with a high privilege could With this method, which combines white-box and black-box testing, testers only have a limited understanding of the system. By simulating situations in which an insider has re"
147,batch500_0070,val,Penetration Testing,sec_2507.00829v1_On_the_Surprising_Efficacy_of_LLMs_.txt,What percentage of Global Fortune 1000 companies use the same underlying technology stack according to the source?,"According to the source, over 90% of Global Fortune 1000 companies use the same underlying technology stack.","Interviews with professional penetration-testers indicate that they encounter similar insecure configurations during assignments, create a hypothesis about the network’s security, and select attacks based upon that [34]. For example, if penetration-testers encounter unsigned NTLMv2-Hashes during an enterprise network security test, they assume that the overall target network matches security bad-practices from 10 years ago and attempt matching attacks such as pass-the-hash attacks. The Target System Landscape is Homogeneous. Pattern-matching is only feasible if penetration-testing targets exhibit similarities— otherwise there would be insufficient features to base the patternmatching on. Fortunately, when looking at enterprise networks, over 90% of Global Fortune 1000 companies use the same underlying technology stack (Section 2.1). Security practitioners note that they encounter the same security vulnerabilities and insecure configurations during assignments [34]. When looking at web penetration-testing, the landscape is more diverse. Applications utilizing the same technology stack, e.g., the same web framework, are similar implementation-wise and often exhibit similar vulnerabilities. Architectures between different technology stacks also show similarity through common architecture design patterns. LLMs exceed in Pattern-Matching. One side-effect of the ongoing discussion about LLMs’ reasoning capabilities is that researchers agree that LLMs exceed at pattern-matching [71, 86, 90]. Given the previous section, this implies that they are well-suited for penetration-testing. Research implies that LLMs are able to solve tasks if examples in their training data resemble those tasks [28]. Given the described semi-monocultures, target IT environments should resemble each other. Furthermore, there is ample publicly available penetrationtesting background knowledge available from technical blog posts, incident reports, and CTF walk-throughs and thus included in common tra",,,,,KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,Fortune 1000 teknoloji yığını istatistiği penetrasyon testi göreviyle zayıf ilişkili; bağlamı doğrula.,,1.00,"features to base the patternmatching on. Fortunately, when looking at enterprise networks, over 90% of Global Fortune 1000 companies use the same underlying technology stack (Section 2.1). Security practitioners note that they encounter the same security vulnerabilities and insecure configurations during assignments [34]. When looking at web penetration-testing, the landscape is more diverse. Applications utilizing the same technology stack, e.g., the same web framework, are similar implementation-wise and often exhibit similar vulnerabilities.",KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,Fortune 1000 teknoloji yığını istatistiği penetrasyon testi göreviyle zayıf ilişkili; bağlamı doğrula.,,1.00,"features to base the patternmatching on. Fortunately, when looking at enterprise networks, over 90% of Global Fortune 1000 companies use the same underlying technology stack (Section 2.1). Security practitioners note that they encounter the same security vulnerabilities and insecure configurations during assignments [34]. When looking at web penetration-testing, the landscape is more diverse. Applications utilizing the same technology stack, e.g., the same web framework, are similar implementation-wise and often exhibit similar vulnerabilities."
148,batch500_0274,val,Penetration Testing,sec_2507.02969v1_Reinforcement_Learning_for_Automate.txt,What are the first two steps in the penetration testing life cycle as described in the source material?,"The first two steps in the penetration testing life cycle as described in the source material are:

1. Engagement: A professional pentester defines the scope and fee for the test, considering factors like the type of engagement (White Box, Black Box, Grey Box), complexity, time requirements, and the number of targets (IP addresses, domains, etc.).

2. Information Gathering: This critical step involves collecting data about the target using sources like social networks, public websites, and tools like CrunchBase, SAM, and the Whois database.","It involves simulating the actions of malicious hackers to identify and exploit potential vulnerabilities within a system. While pen-testing is sometimes confused with vulnerability assessment, they are distinct processes; vulnerability assessment focuses on identifying weaknesses, whereas penetration testing also involves exploiting them to gauge the extent of potential damage. The pen-test process is structured through a life cycle that begins with initial engagement and concludes with the delivery of a comprehensive final report, streamlining the testing process for greater efficiency [Saber et al., 2023]. The penetration testing life cycle can be divided into the following consecutive different steps [Patil et al., 2024]: (1) Engagement: A professional pentester defines the scope and fee for the test, considering factors like the type of engagement (White Box, Black Box, Grey Box), complexity, time requirements, and the number of targets (IP addresses, domains, etc.). (2) Information Gathering: This critical step involves collecting data about the target using sources like social networks, public websites, and tools like CrunchBase, SAM, and the Whois database. (3) Footprinting and Scanning: With the gathered information, the pen-tester identifies active hosts and services within the target’s network using tools like Ping and Fping for more detailed sweeps. (4) Vulnerability Assessment: The pen-tester scans the identified systems for vulnerabilities using tools like Nessus, which compares the findings against a constantly updated vulnerability database. (5) Exploitation: The pen-tester attempts to exploit the discovered vulnerabilities to gain unauthorized access or escalate privileges. Exploits may target web applications (e.g., using OWASP tools) or systems (e.g., malware, and password attacks like brute force and dictionary attacks). (6) Reporting: The final phase involves delivering a detailed report that outlines detected vulnerabilities, associated risks, ",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.92,"al., 2023]. The penetration testing life cycle can be divided into the following consecutive different steps [Patil et al., 2024]: (1) Engagement: A professional pentester defines the scope and fee for the test, considering factors like the type of engagement (White Box, Black Box, Grey Box), complexity, time requirements, and the number of targets (IP addresses, domains, etc.). (2) Information Gathering: This critical step involves collecting data about the target using sources like social networks, public websites, and tools like CrunchBase, ",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.92,"al., 2023]. The penetration testing life cycle can be divided into the following consecutive different steps [Patil et al., 2024]: (1) Engagement: A professional pentester defines the scope and fee for the test, considering factors like the type of engagement (White Box, Black Box, Grey Box), complexity, time requirements, and the number of targets (IP addresses, domains, etc.). (2) Information Gathering: This critical step involves collecting data about the target using sources like social networks, public websites, and tools like CrunchBase, "
149,kaggle1000_0374,test,Penetration Testing,sec_2412.12745v1_Automated_Penetration_Testing__Form.txt,What was the average number of weeks it took for the students to capture all the flags during spring 2024?,The average number of weeks it took for the students to capture all the flags during spring 2024 was 36 weeks.,"To give a rough sense of the difficulty of the labs, which can serve as an estimate of the effort required to perform manual penetration testing against our lab setup, we provide statistics of the time taken to capture the different flags during spring 2024. The students were split in 39 groups of two. Fig. 3 shows the flag capture statistics per week. 6.2. Initial Tool Setup We assume a black-box penetration testing set up; only access to a network range where the hosts reside and the flag identifiers are provided to the tool. Any host in the range can be targeted by scanners, offensive or penetration testing tools and once privileged access is gained can be modified to serve the attacker’s needs, e.g., to launch further attacks against other valid targets in the range. No information is provided regarding the possible services on any of the hosts or any potential vulnerabilities. 7University name to be provided in camera ready version 9 29 36 36 36 36 36 36 36 36 36 36 36 36 36 36 7 16 31 36 36 36 36 36 36 36 36 36 36 36 36 36 4 17 25 31 35 38 38 38 38 38 38 38 38 38 38 38 9 15 25 34 34 34 34 34 35 35 36 36 36 36 36 7 11 19 25 29 30 31 31 31 32 33 33 33 33 33 7 12 29 30 30 31 31 34 34 0 12 23 28 28 29 30 31 1 12 19 27 27 28 30 30 ADAPT implements the architecture presented in Section. 5.1, hence it uses the functions described in Next Move(s) ADAPT for target and attack selection. ADAPT is equipped with a scan repertoire that is able to perform 8 scans and an attack repertoire with 41 attack tactics and 57 attack techniques. The scanning and exploitation capabilities provided by the repertoires directly affect the extent to which the penetration test succeeds. Thus, to showcase ADAPT’s functionality, we opted to use repertoires that contain the required capabilities to successfully complete the individual exploits against the hosts of the VM network. We do not measure ADAPT’s success in terms of having a sufficient scan and attack repertoire to discover and exploi",,,,,ELE_ADAYI,KRITIK,"""36 hafta"" bulgusu kaynak pasajında desteklenmiyor; kaynak yalnızca Spring 2024 ve haftalık bayrak yakalama grafiğini anıyor. Muhtemel tablo/grafik okuma hatası.",,0.50,"capture the different flags during spring 2024. The students were split in 39 groups of two. Fig. 3 shows the flag capture statistics per week. 6.2. Initial Tool Setup We assume a black-box penetration testing set up; only access to a network range where the hosts reside and the flag identifiers are provided to the tool. Any host in the range can be targeted by scanners, offensive or penetration testing tools and once privileged access is gained can be modified to serve the attacker’s needs, e.g., to launch further attacks against other valid",ELE_ADAYI,KRITIK,"""36 hafta"" bulgusu kaynak pasajında desteklenmiyor; kaynak yalnızca Spring 2024 ve haftalık bayrak yakalama grafiğini anıyor. Muhtemel tablo/grafik okuma hatası.",,0.50,"capture the different flags during spring 2024. The students were split in 39 groups of two. Fig. 3 shows the flag capture statistics per week. 6.2. Initial Tool Setup We assume a black-box penetration testing set up; only access to a network range where the hosts reside and the flag identifiers are provided to the tool. Any host in the range can be targeted by scanners, offensive or penetration testing tools and once privileged access is gained can be modified to serve the attacker’s needs, e.g., to launch further attacks against other valid"
150,batch500_0082,test,Penetration Testing,Where AI Vulnerability Discovery Harnesses Go Quiet_ Structu.txt,How many harnesses were examined in the study to map the points where structural false negatives occur?,10,"Where AI Vulnerability Discovery Harnesses Go Quiet: Structural False Negatives and Hidden Vulnerabilities LLM-based vulnerability discovery harnesses can miss real, reachable vulnerabilities because of how the harness around the model is built. Steps that select code, filter it, and store and merge findings can drop a vulnerability before or after the model runs, independently of the model’s capability, and the report usually gives no sign that this happened. These drops come from engineering choices, and errors. They leave vulnerabilities undetected, and an attacker who knows the harness’s rules can also use them to hide a vulnerability on purpose. Understanding these drops shows how to build harnesses that fix or disclose them and are harder to evade. Starting from the two harnesses we maintain, Knostic’s OpenAnt and raptor, and then examining 10 additional open-source harnesses, we mapped 13 points where such drops occur and organized them into a taxonomy. Using each harness’s own code, we show a single file that six harnesses dropped before their model saw its code, five of them without notice. We propose engineering failure patterns mapped to these drops, and relate them to Ptacek and Newsham’s work on intrusion detection evasion. The paper also covers content-steered verdicts and prompt injection, and gives design principles and tests for harnesses that report accurately what they analyzed. The reliability findings likely apply to other LLM harnesses, beyond vulnerability research and application security, and the evasion findings apply wherever an attacker can write or influence the code being checked. We also argue that harnesses built to be descriptive can, in effect, be prescriptive. Rules written to describe typical code and typical model replies end up deciding what is analyzed, and anything that does not conform is treated as absent or as “no vulnerability.” A harness report with no findings can mean three things: the code is clean, the model analyzed ",,,,,KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,10 harness sayısı çalışma-içi zafiyet değerlendirme örneklemi; genellenemez.,,0.00,"Where AI Vulnerability Discovery Harnesses Go Quiet: Structural False Negatives and Hidden Vulnerabilities LLM-based vulnerability discovery harnesses can miss real, reachable vulnerabilities because of how the harness around the model is built. Steps that select code, filter it, and store and merge findings can drop a vulnerability before or after the model runs, independently of the model’s capability, and the report usually gives no sign that this happened. These drops come from engineering choices, and errors. They leave vulnerabilities und",KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,10 harness sayısı çalışma-içi zafiyet değerlendirme örneklemi; genellenemez.,,0.00,"Where AI Vulnerability Discovery Harnesses Go Quiet: Structural False Negatives and Hidden Vulnerabilities LLM-based vulnerability discovery harnesses can miss real, reachable vulnerabilities because of how the harness around the model is built. Steps that select code, filter it, and store and merge findings can drop a vulnerability before or after the model runs, independently of the model’s capability, and the report usually gives no sign that this happened. These drops come from engineering choices, and errors. They leave vulnerabilities und"
151,draft_0231,train,System Security,Act on Your Behalf_ Beyond Your Authorization_ Benchmarking.txt,How many different types of situational triggers were identified in the 44 real-world cases?,14,"We collect 44 real-world cases in which an agent acted beyond the authorization scope established by a principal’s instruction, drawn from social media and online forums, news reports, law, regulation, and policy documents, and research literature. We include cases with a benign principal and agent when natural-language delegation establishes both the assigned task and an externally supported out-of-authorization action. Each case becomes a structured seed containing its source and context, the principal’s instruction, the observed agent process, the action regarded as out of authorization, and labels for its situational trigger, consequence, and domain. Situational trigger. Through a review of prior work on agent safety and analysis of the 44 realworld cases, we derive a taxonomy of 14 situational triggers (Appendix B.2) that characterize the source of contextual pressure presented to the agent. We group them by where that pressure appears in the agent’s context: five instruction-level triggers originate in the principal’s instructions or settings, while nine environment-level triggers originate elsewhere in the task environment. The latter include two malicious triggers that directly command an out-of-authorization action or distort the agentperceived authorization scope. All other triggers are benign, reflecting ordinary pressures that agents can encounter in real-world delegation. Direct harmful consequences. Acting outside the assigned scope is itself harmful to the delegation relationship, but may not cause the additional harms studied in safety and privacy evaluation. For example, reacting to one’s own pull request without authorization exceeds the assignment without directly causing a conventional safety or privacy harm. We label a scenario with direct harmful consequences when the OA action falls into at least one of ten categories consolidated from prior taxonomies (Zhang et al., 2024; Vijayvargiya et al., 2025), and without direct harmful consequences oth",,,,,KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,"Agent güvenlik yetki bağlamında çalışma-içi tetikleyici sayısı, System Security ile sınırda.",,0.00,"and analysis of the 44 realworld cases, we derive a taxonomy of 14 situational triggers (Appendix B.2) that characterize the source of contextual pressure presented to the agent. We group them by where that pressure appears in the agent’s context: five instruction-level triggers originate in the principal’s instructions or settings, while nine environment-level triggers originate elsewhere in the task environment. The latter include two malicious triggers that directly command an out-of-authorization action or distort the agentperceived authori",KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,"Agent güvenlik yetki bağlamında çalışma-içi tetikleyici sayısı, System Security ile sınırda.",,0.00,"and analysis of the 44 realworld cases, we derive a taxonomy of 14 situational triggers (Appendix B.2) that characterize the source of contextual pressure presented to the agent. We group them by where that pressure appears in the agent’s context: five instruction-level triggers originate in the principal’s instructions or settings, while nine environment-level triggers originate elsewhere in the task environment. The latter include two malicious triggers that directly command an out-of-authorization action or distort the agentperceived authori"
152,extra_20261108_6832,train,System Security,A Rowhammer attack on Fil-C_s software capabilities.txt,What mechanism does the paper use to demonstrate the impact of targeted bit flips on Fil-C binaries?,"The paper demonstrates the impact of targeted bit flips on Fil-C binaries using a combination of a deterministic single-bit fault injection method and a physical end-to-end path. Specifically, it employs a deterministic path that isolates the metadata-corruption consequence with a single out-of-band write, and a physical end-to-end path that executes the attacks on commodity hardware, including older DDR3 and modern DDR4 modules protected by Target Row Refresh (TRR). This approach allows for the isolation and demonstration of the necessary fault generation and grooming required for the Rowhammer attack, proving that the necessary fault generation and grooming are physically realizable.","This allows us to study the impact of targeted bit flips on Fil-C binaries independently of the underlying hardware, yielding four concrete case studies: a bounded out-of-bounds (OOB) read/write window, secret disclosure, privilege escalation by flipping an adjacent is_admin field, and cross-tenant API key leakage in a multi-tenant credential service backed by SQLite. arXiv:2610.33748v2 [cs.PL] 29 Oct 2026 Fil-C victim allocates the object. Its InvisiCap upper-bound word sits in ordinary DRAM Physical end-to-end path Single out-of-band 1-bit write Phase 1: DRAM templating Fil-C enforcement boundary. Corruption happens out-of-band Phase 4 Exploitation (shared). The Fil-C runtime treats the corrupted upper as authoritative and silently permits the out-ofbounds access, without raising a panic, signal, or machine check Fig. 1. The two experimental paths. Blue boxes run inside the Fil-C victim process. Red boxes run in the attacker’s native (non-Fil-C) process. Both paths share the same start (a live InvisiCap in DRAM) and the same consequence (Phase 4: the runtime silently honours the corrupted bound). They differ only in delivery. The deterministic path lands the flip with a single out-of-band write to isolate the metadata-corruption consequence. The physical end-to-end path lands it by real Rowhammer through templating, grooming, and hammering (Phases 1–3). Neither delivery is mediated by a Fil-C check, which is why the corruption is invisible to the runtime. Second, a physical end-to-end path proves that the necessary fault generation and grooming are physically realisable by executing these attacks end-to-end on commodity hardware. Our evaluation uses both older DDR3 memory (exploited in 2 minutes) and modern, Target Row Refresh (TRR) protected DDR4 modules (exploited in 10–35 minutes), demonstrating that the TRR defence does not close this Fil-C delivery channel. We emphasise that mitigating Rowhammer is not Fil-C’s responsibility: it is a hardware-level defect for DRAM vendors and platform designers. However, Rowhammer is widely and incorrectly believed to be solved, so it is worth stating plainly that a software safety system such as Fil-C remains exposed to it invisibly to the runtime. We close with Fil-Cspecific mitigations: we contribute no new class of defence, and the Message Authentication Code (MAC) protection we recommend is PT-Guard’s [18] transported to a new target. In summary, our contributions are as follows. • We present the first Rowhammer attack on Fil-C. We characterise the Fil-C 0.679 capability layout and show that its bounds checks rely on the integrity of inline metadata stored in ordinary DRAM (§II-A, §III-B, §VIII). • Through deterministic single-bit fault injection, we show that corrupting software capabilities silently bypasses Fil-C’s runtime checks.",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.73,"lands the flip with a single out-of-band write to isolate the metadata-corruption consequence. The physical end-to-end path lands it by real Rowhammer through templating, grooming, and hammering (Phases 1–3). Neither delivery is mediated by a Fil-C check, which is why the corruption is invisible to the runtime. Second, a physical end-to-end path proves that the necessary fault generation and grooming are physically realisable by executing these attacks end-to-end on commodity hardware. Our evaluation uses both older DDR3 memory (exploited in 2 ",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.73,"lands the flip with a single out-of-band write to isolate the metadata-corruption consequence. The physical end-to-end path lands it by real Rowhammer through templating, grooming, and hammering (Phases 1–3). Neither delivery is mediated by a Fil-C check, which is why the corruption is invisible to the runtime. Second, a physical end-to-end path proves that the necessary fault generation and grooming are physically realisable by executing these attacks end-to-end on commodity hardware. Our evaluation uses both older DDR3 memory (exploited in 2 "
153,kaggle1000_0101,train,System Security,Cheap Reverse Engineering and What It Will Do.txt,What are the three main reasons AI RE makes copying software and hardware much easier?,"The three main reasons AI RE makes copying software and hardware much easier are:

1. AI makes it much easier to defeat technical barriers (both explicit and implicit) that once limited access to technical details.
2. AI makes it much easier to modify existing software to add, remove, or alter functionality, often producing an artifact that is substantially similar to the original.
3. AI makes it much easier to reimplement software from a high-level specification.","Overall, AI RE will make copying software and hardware much easier for three reasons. First, AI makes it much easier to defeat technical barriers (explicit and implicit) that once limited access to technical details. Second, AI makes it much easier to modify existing software to add, remove, or alter functionality, often producing an artifact that is substantially similar to the original. And third, AI makes it much easier to reimplement software from a high-level specification. Putting all three together, the landscape will soon be flooded with copies, some legally made but many not. There will be cracked versions of software without rights-protection restrictions, functional clones available soon after launch, forks that add new functionality (even when the modified product is not open-source), and mashups that combine existing products in unexpected ways. From an IP policy perspective, the key feature of this world will be reduced lead time: the period after an innovation is deployed during which a company enjoys market exclusivity and profits accordingly. We expect extremely rapid diffusion of innovations that increase public access rationales. This is the baseline against which policy interventions should be assessed. This isn’t just about proprietary software. One of the first major AI-assisted reimplementation efforts (albeit not a “clean-room” one) involved the open-source Chardet library [143]. Chardet had been licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public License (LGPL), but the reimplementer released the new version under the more permissive MIT license. Open-source projects that depend on license terms for software freedom, sustainability, and ethical commitments will find that these conditions can be evaded through AI RE. Do Nothing. First, the law could do nothing, and leave existing IP laws in place. In this view, weaker incentives for initial innovation and easier follow-on innovation are not reasons to abandon the current system. Instead, they are ei",,,,,ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,AI ile ürün kopyalama / fikrî mülkiyet tartışması sistem güvenliği mekanizması değil.,,0.94,"Overall, AI RE will make copying software and hardware much easier for three reasons. First, AI makes it much easier to defeat technical barriers (explicit and implicit) that once limited access to technical details. Second, AI makes it much easier to modify existing software to add, remove, or alter functionality, often producing an artifact that is substantially similar to the original. And third, AI makes it much easier to reimplement software from a high-level specification. Putting all three together, the landscape will soon be flooded wit",ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,AI ile ürün kopyalama / fikrî mülkiyet tartışması sistem güvenliği mekanizması değil.,,0.94,"Overall, AI RE will make copying software and hardware much easier for three reasons. First, AI makes it much easier to defeat technical barriers (explicit and implicit) that once limited access to technical details. Second, AI makes it much easier to modify existing software to add, remove, or alter functionality, often producing an artifact that is substantially similar to the original. And third, AI makes it much easier to reimplement software from a high-level specification. Putting all three together, the landscape will soon be flooded wit"
154,batch1000_0256,train,System Security,An Empirical Study of Undisclosed Charges in Hook-Based Dece.txt,What is the purpose of `hookDelta` in the `_swap` function as described in Listing 1?,"`hookDelta` is used to combine adjustments made by hooks before and after the core swap calculation. Specifically, it includes positive amounts owed to the Hook and negative amounts supplied by it, reflecting any adjustments made by the hooks during the swap process. These adjustments are then subtracted from `swapDelta` to determine the user's final payment and receipt.","In Listing 1, _swap at L4 records this exchange in swapDelta, a pair of signed token amounts: negative for the user’s payment and positive for the receipt [39]. For pools with dynamic LP fees, beforeSwap can set the LP fee rate for that swap through lpFeeOverride (L2). The core _swap calculation uses this rate at L4, so the resulting fee remains included in swapDelta [37, 38]. Hooks can further adjust these exchange amounts through callback returns. Before the core exchange, beforeSwap also returns deltaBefore (L2), which can change the amount passed to the core swap (L3). Afterward, afterSwap inspects the core result and returns deltaAfter (L5). L6 combines these adjustments for each token into hookDelta, with positive amounts owed to the Hook and negative amounts supplied by it. L7 then subtracts this net Hook adjustment from swapDelta to obtain the user’s final payment and receipt. A positive output-side hookDelta therefore reduces what the user receives and allows the Hook to retain the difference. function swap (...) { // PoolManager (deltaBefore , lpFeeOverride) = hooks.beforeSwap (...); amountToSwap = adjustAmount (..., deltaBefore); swapDelta = _swap (..., amountToSwap , lpFeeOverride); deltaAfter = hooks.afterSwap (..., swapDelta , ...); hookDelta = combineDeltas(deltaBefore , deltaAfter); return swapDelta - hookDelta; } Listing 1: Simplified swap flow with Hook adjustments. Undisclosed Settlement Charges. A Hook can also use settlement adjustments to receive input tokens and supply output tokens to execute a swap. Our study focuses on additional deductions imposed by Hooks beyond these exchanged token amounts and standard An Empirical Study of Undisclosed Charges in Hook-Based Decentralized Exchanges Domain / Sub-category I. Settlement, Pricing & Fee Customization Changes settlement through nonzero callback deltas or a protocol-linked claims ledger. Ordinary token transfers, zero deltas, or asset withdrawals without a supported settlement path. Updates or ",,,,,ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,Uniswap hookDelta ve takas miktarı muhasebesi güvenlik mekanizması değil.,,0.67,"remains included in swapDelta [37, 38]. Hooks can further adjust these exchange amounts through callback returns. Before the core exchange, beforeSwap also returns deltaBefore (L2), which can change the amount passed to the core swap (L3). Afterward, afterSwap inspects the core result and returns deltaAfter (L5). L6 combines these adjustments for each token into hookDelta, with positive amounts owed to the Hook and negative amounts supplied by it. L7 then subtracts this net Hook adjustment from swapDelta to obtain the user’s final payment and r",ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,Uniswap hookDelta ve takas miktarı muhasebesi güvenlik mekanizması değil.,,0.67,"remains included in swapDelta [37, 38]. Hooks can further adjust these exchange amounts through callback returns. Before the core exchange, beforeSwap also returns deltaBefore (L2), which can change the amount passed to the core swap (L3). Afterward, afterSwap inspects the core result and returns deltaAfter (L5). L6 combines these adjustments for each token into hookDelta, with positive amounts owed to the Hook and negative amounts supplied by it. L7 then subtracts this net Hook adjustment from swapDelta to obtain the user’s final payment and r"
155,batch_new500_0391,train,System Security,NIST.SP.800-160v1.txt,What is one responsibility of a systems security engineer according to the document?,"One responsibility of a systems security engineer is to provide input to analyses of alternatives and to requirements, engineering, and risk tradeoff analyses to achieve a cost-effective security architectural design for protections that enable mission/business success.","Archive and protect information generated during the life cycle of the system. SPECIAL PUBLICATION 800-160, VOLUME 1 SYSTEMS SECURITY ENGINEERING A Multidisciplinary Approach in the Engineering of Trustworthy Secure Systems ________________________________________________________________________________________________ he role of a systems security engineer is to participate in a multidisciplinary systems engineering team, applying fundamental systems security understanding, skills, expertise, and experience to develop a system that satisfies organizational mission and/or business requirements, including stakeholder protection needs and security requirements.41 The systems security engineer is expected to have expertise and experience in multiple areas (e.g., protection needs assessment, requirements elicitation, security architecture, threat assessment, computer security, communication security, networking, security technologies, hardware and software development, test and evaluation, vulnerability assessment, penetration testing, and supply chain risk).42 Systems security engineer responsibilities include: Maintaining a comprehensive and holistic system view while addressing stakeholder security Ensuring the effectiveness and suitability of the security elements of the system as an enabler to mission/business success; Ensuring that relevant threat and vulnerability data is considered in support of securityProviding input to analyses of alternatives and to requirements, engineering, and risk tradeoff analyses to achieve a cost-effective security architectural design for protections that enable mission/business success; Providing the evidence necessary to support assurance claims and to substantiate the determination that the system is sufficiently trustworthy; and Conducting security risk management activities, producing related security risk management information, and advising the engineering team and key stakeholders on the security-relevant impact of threats an",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.86,"technologies, hardware and software development, test and evaluation, vulnerability assessment, penetration testing, and supply chain risk).42 Systems security engineer responsibilities include: Maintaining a comprehensive and holistic system view while addressing stakeholder security Ensuring the effectiveness and suitability of the security elements of the system as an enabler to mission/business success; Ensuring that relevant threat and vulnerability data is considered in support of securityProviding input to analyses of alternatives and to",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.86,"technologies, hardware and software development, test and evaluation, vulnerability assessment, penetration testing, and supply chain risk).42 Systems security engineer responsibilities include: Maintaining a comprehensive and holistic system view while addressing stakeholder security Ensuring the effectiveness and suitability of the security elements of the system as an enabler to mission/business success; Ensuring that relevant threat and vulnerability data is considered in support of securityProviding input to analyses of alternatives and to"
156,kaggle1000_0869,train,System Security,Combining Long-Term Memory and LLMs Comes at a High Cost_ Fr.txt,What is the simplest strategy to effectively protect against persistent prompt injection attacks?,"The simplest strategy to effectively protect against persistent prompt injection attacks is to disable long-term memory, thus enforcing full isolation of user sessions.","It also weakens security by design, as it reduces the degree of compartmentalization. As shown in this work, failing to follow these security principles can lead to persistent prompt injection effects, whether the attack is performed directly through user queries or indirectly through untrusted external sources. To effectively protect against the attacks studied in this work, the simplest strategy is to disable long-term memory, thus enforcing full isolation of user sessions. In scenarios where this is not possible or desired, however, some mitigations can be considered: Memory disabled by default: As discussed above, persistent memory features tend to conflict with several established security principles. Therefore, if a system still chooses to provide this feature, it should be disabled by default, and users should at least be warned about the risks when enabling it. This follows the (d) security by default principle, which states that the default system configuration should enforce the strongest security settings [14]. Avoiding memories from untrusted sources: To prevent memory injection through external content, such as PDF files and MCP-retrieved content, the principle of (e) least privilege should be enforced. This can be achieved by assigning lower privileges to these sources than to user prompts. Storing content from untrusted sources in memory should be completely prohibited or, at least, validated through a human-inthe-loop verification step, by recommending memories to the user instead of storing them automatically [48]. Shared-memory restrictions: Shared memory is inherently at odds with data compartmentalization, as data shared across user spaces is not isolated between them. Therefore, to prevent attacks such as those demonstrated here, where one user account can affect another within an organization, shared memory should either: (1) be avoided; or (2) require each affected user’s approval before any modification is applied to their personal environmen",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"this work, failing to follow these security principles can lead to persistent prompt injection effects, whether the attack is performed directly through user queries or indirectly through untrusted external sources. To effectively protect against the attacks studied in this work, the simplest strategy is to disable long-term memory, thus enforcing full isolation of user sessions. In scenarios where this is not possible or desired, however, some mitigations can be considered: Memory disabled by default: As discussed above, persistent memory feat",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,1.00,"this work, failing to follow these security principles can lead to persistent prompt injection effects, whether the attack is performed directly through user queries or indirectly through untrusted external sources. To effectively protect against the attacks studied in this work, the simplest strategy is to disable long-term memory, thus enforcing full isolation of user sessions. In scenarios where this is not possible or desired, however, some mitigations can be considered: Memory disabled by default: As discussed above, persistent memory feat"
157,draft_0147,train,System Security,NIST.SP.800-167.txt,What is the purpose of the Special Publication 800-series reports according to the source?,"The Special Publication 800-series reports on ITL’s research, guidelines, and outreach efforts in information system security, and its collaborative activities with industry, government, and academic organizations.","Organizations are encouraged to review all draft publications during public comment periods and provide feedback to NIST. All NIST Computer Security Division publications, other than the ones noted above, are available at Reports on Computer Systems Technology (NIST) promotes the U.S. economy and public welfare by providing technical leadership for the Nation’s measurement and standards infrastructure. ITL develops tests, test methods, reference data, proof of concept implementations, and technical analyses to advance the development and productive use of information technology. ITL’s responsibilities include the development of management, administrative, technical, and physical standards and guidelines for the cost-effective security and privacy of other than national security-related information in federal information systems. The Special Publication 800-series reports on ITL’s research, guidelines, and outreach efforts in information system security, and its collaborative activities with industry, government, and academic organizations. An application whitelist is a list of applications and application components that are authorized for use in an organization. Application whitelisting technologies use whitelists to control which applications are permitted to execute on a host. This helps to stop the execution of malware, unlicensed software, and other unauthorized software. This publication is intended to assist organizations in understanding the basics of application whitelisting. It also explains planning and implementation for whitelisting technologies throughout the security deployment lifecycle. access control; application control; application whitelisting; information security; software security; Technology (NIST) and Karen Scarfone of Scarfone Cybersecurity, wish to thank their colleagues, in particular Eric Chudow from the National Security Agency, who contributed to this publication, as well as the reviewers who provided feedback. Trademark Information A",,,,,KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,NIST SP800 serisinin tanıtımı kurum bilgisi; daha teknik bir sistem güvenliği sorusuna dönüştürülebilir.,,1.00,"security-related information in federal information systems. The Special Publication 800-series reports on ITL’s research, guidelines, and outreach efforts in information system security, and its collaborative activities with industry, government, and academic organizations. An application whitelist is a list of applications and application components that are authorized for use in an organization. Application whitelisting technologies use whitelists to control which applications are permitted to execute on a host. This helps to stop the execut",KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,NIST SP800 serisinin tanıtımı kurum bilgisi; daha teknik bir sistem güvenliği sorusuna dönüştürülebilir.,,1.00,"security-related information in federal information systems. The Special Publication 800-series reports on ITL’s research, guidelines, and outreach efforts in information system security, and its collaborative activities with industry, government, and academic organizations. An application whitelist is a list of applications and application components that are authorized for use in an organization. Application whitelisting technologies use whitelists to control which applications are permitted to execute on a host. This helps to stop the execut"
158,kaggle1000_0068,train,System Security,NIST.SP.800-221.txt,What is the potential consequence of a failure in the interaction between chemical valves and pumps in a water treatment context?,A failure in the interaction between chemical valves and pumps in a water treatment context could have life-threatening consequences.,"The ongoing introduction of new technologies has also changed and complicated the landscape. Wireless connections, big data, cloud computing, and the IoT present new complexities and concomitant vulnerabilities. Operational technology (OT), including building management systems and physical access control/monitoring systems, is increasingly relied upon. Information and technology are no longer as simple as automated filing systems. Rather, they are like the central nervous system — a delicately balanced and intricate part of an organization or enterprise that coordinates and controls the most fundamental assets of most organizations. This ecosystem’s increasing complexity gives rise to systemic risks and exploitable vulnerabilities that — once triggered — can have a runaway effect with multiple severe consequences for enterprises. Enterprises have historically applied traditional IT resilience goals and techniques to ensure the availability of mission-related functions, even if in a degraded state. However, ICT complexity can reach a level where traditional resilience techniques are insufficient, particularly when components are physically separated or have critical timing dependencies. For example, for some electrical grid networks, the timing of events and faults might be measured in milliseconds. In a water treatment context, a failure in the interaction between chemical valves and pumps could have life-threatening consequences. The fault tolerance system itself may become a vulnerability. For these reasons, risk practitioners need to consider increasing ecosystem complexity when identifying and responding to risk scenarios. Managing ICT risk for these ecosystems is incredibly challenging because of their dynamic complexity. This complexity increases the risk to specific systems, and that risk can cascade to create additional risks at the system, organization, and enterprise levels. Emerging risk conditions created by the interdependence of systems and counterpar",,,,,KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,"Siber-fiziksel güvenlik riskine örnek olabilir, ama güvenlik kontrolüne bağlanmalı.",,1.00,"timing of events and faults might be measured in milliseconds. In a water treatment context, a failure in the interaction between chemical valves and pumps could have life-threatening consequences. The fault tolerance system itself may become a vulnerability. For these reasons, risk practitioners need to consider increasing ecosystem complexity when identifying and responding to risk scenarios. Managing ICT risk for these ecosystems is incredibly challenging because of their dynamic complexity. This complexity increases the risk to specific sys",KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,"Siber-fiziksel güvenlik riskine örnek olabilir, ama güvenlik kontrolüne bağlanmalı.",,1.00,"timing of events and faults might be measured in milliseconds. In a water treatment context, a failure in the interaction between chemical valves and pumps could have life-threatening consequences. The fault tolerance system itself may become a vulnerability. For these reasons, risk practitioners need to consider increasing ecosystem complexity when identifying and responding to risk scenarios. Managing ICT risk for these ecosystems is incredibly challenging because of their dynamic complexity. This complexity increases the risk to specific sys"
