167,kaggle1000_0273,train,Web Security,When POST Becomes GET_ Investigating HTTP Method Interchange.txt,What percentage of the tested sites showed high-confidence interchangeable POST and GET requests according to the Method Interchange Detection Engine (MIDE) experiment?,"According to the experiment performed on the Tranco top 10k, 4.1% of the tested sites showed high-confidence interchangeable POST and GET requests. Additionally, 12.7% of the sites with captured POSTs and 14.9% of the sites with replayed POSTs exhibited high-confidence interchangeable requests.","When POST Becomes GET: Investigating HTTP Method Interchangeability and Its Security Implications University of Trento Seif Errahmane Hannachi Higher School of Computer Science of Sidi Bel Abbes s.hannachi@esi-sba.dz University of Trento Abstract—HTTP method semantics are a common boundary between safe retrieval and state-changing operations; however, deployed web applications do not always enforce this boundary consistently. In this paper, we study HTTP method interchangeability, a behavior where a POST request can be rewritten as a GET request to the same endpoint, with POST body parameters moved into the query string, while preserving an equivalent observed response. We present Method Interchange Detection Engine (MIDE), an automated pipeline that crawls web applications in a browser, records POST requests generated by page loads and user-interface interactions, converts supported POST bodies into GET candidates, and compares replayed responses with the original POST response. To avoid counting generic or parameter-independent responses as interchangeable, MIDE also sends a no-query GET baseline. We treat as high-confidence evidence of interchangeability only the cases where the converted GET matches the POST, while the baseline does not. We perform a large-scale unauthenticated experiment on the Tranco top 10k. We find high-confidence interchangeable requests on 407 of 10,000 tested sites (4.1%), 407 of 3,200 sites with captured POSTs (12.7%), and 407 of 2,734 sites with replayed POSTs (14.9%). A targeted CrossSite Request Forgery (CSRF) token-replay check finds cases where invalid tokens are rejected for POST but accepted for the equivalent GET. We also observe Web Cache Deception (WCD) conditions and cacheable GET responses for interchangeable request instances. These results demonstrate that method interchangeability is observable in real web applications and can expose method-scoped defense gaps. Index Terms—HTTP method interchangeability, HTTP methods, CSRF",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.76,"POST, while the baseline does not. We perform a large-scale unauthenticated experiment on the Tranco top 10k. We find high-confidence interchangeable requests on 407 of 10,000 tested sites (4.1%), 407 of 3,200 sites with captured POSTs (12.7%), and 407 of 2,734 sites with replayed POSTs (14.9%). A targeted CrossSite Request Forgery (CSRF) token-replay check finds cases where invalid tokens are rejected for POST but accepted for the equivalent GET. We also observe Web Cache Deception (WCD) conditions and cacheable GET responses for interchangeab",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.76,"POST, while the baseline does not. We perform a large-scale unauthenticated experiment on the Tranco top 10k. We find high-confidence interchangeable requests on 407 of 10,000 tested sites (4.1%), 407 of 3,200 sites with captured POSTs (12.7%), and 407 of 2,734 sites with replayed POSTs (14.9%). A targeted CrossSite Request Forgery (CSRF) token-replay check finds cases where invalid tokens are rejected for POST but accepted for the equivalent GET. We also observe Web Cache Deception (WCD) conditions and cacheable GET responses for interchangeab"
168,kaggle1000_0475,train,Web Security,"Black Hat Python, 2nd Edition.txt",What is the purpose of encoding the data dictionary to a bytes object when making a POST request using urllib.parse?,"The purpose of encoding the data dictionary to a bytes object when making a POST request using urllib.parse is to prepare the data for transmission in the request body. Specifically, the `urllib.parse.urlencode(info).encode()` line encodes the dictionary `info` containing the login credentials ('user': 'tim', 'passwd': '31337') into a URL-encoded string, which is then converted to a bytes object (`data`). This byte string is then passed as the `data` parameter to the `Request` object to send the credentials in the POST request.","It also adds URLparsing capability with the subpackage urllib.parse. To make an HTTP request with this package, you can code the request as a context manager using the with statement. The resulting response should contain a byte string. Here’s how to make 1 import urllib.parse import urllib.request 3 with urllib.request.urlopen(url) as response: # GET 4 content = response.read() Here we import the packages we need 1 and define the target URL 2. Then, using the urlopen method as a context manager, we make the request 3 and read the response 4. To create a POST request, pass a data dictionary to the request object, encoded as bytes. This data dictionary should have the keyvalue pairs that the target web app expects. In this example, the info dictionary contains the credentials (user, passwd) needed to log in to info = {'user': 'tim', 'passwd': '31337'} 1 data = urllib.parse.urlencode(info).encode() # data is now of 2 req = urllib.request.Request(url, data) with urllib.request.urlopen(req) as response: # POST 3 content = response.read() We encode the data dictionary that contains the login credentials to make it a bytes object 1, put it into the POST request 2 that transmits the credentials, and receive the web app response to our The requests Library Ev