170,batch_new500_0281,train,Web Security,OWASP_Application_Security_Verification_Standard_5.0.0_en (1).txt,What method should be used for HTTP requests to sensitive functionality to defend against browser-based request forgery attacks?,"HTTP requests to sensitive functionality should use appropriate HTTP methods such as POST, PUT, PATCH, or DELETE, and not methods defined by the HTTP specification as ""safe"" such as HEAD, OPTIONS, or GET.","This mechanism will be critical for endpoints designed to be called from a different origin, but it can also be a useful request forgery prevention mechanism for endpoints which are not designed to be called from a different origin. Verify that, if the application does not rely on the CORS preflight mechanism to prevent disallowed cross‑origin requests to use sensitive functionality, these requests are validated to ensure they originate from the application itself. This may be done by using and validating anti‑forgery tokens or requiring extra HTTP header fields that are not CORS‑safelisted request‑header fields. This is to defend against browser‑based request forgery attacks, commonly known as cross‑site request forgery (CSRF). Verify that, if the application relies on the CORS preflight mechanism to prevent disallowed cross‑origin use of sensitive functionality, it is not possible to call the functionality with a request which does not trigger a CORS‑preflight request. This may require checking the values of the ‘Origin’ and ‘Content‑Type’request header fields or using an extra header field that is not a CORS‑safelisted header‑field. Application Security Verification Standard Verify that HTTP requests to sensitive functionality use appropriate HTTP methods such as POST, PUT, PATCH, or DELETE, and not methods defined by the HTTP specification as “safe”such as HEAD, OPTIONS, or GET. Alternatively, strict validation of the Sec‑Fetch‑* request header fields can be used to ensure that the request did not originate from an inappropriate cross‑origin call, a navigation request, or a resource load (such as an image source) where this is not expected. Verify that separate applications are hosted on different hostnames to leverage the restrictions provided by same‑origin policy, including how documents or scripts loaded by one origin can interact with resources from another origin and hostname‑based restrictions on cookies. Verify that messages received by the postMessage i",,,,,DUZELT,YUKSEK,Güvenli HTTP yöntemi seçimi önemli ancak tek başına CSRF önlemi değildir; kaynakta bağlamı tam kontrol edilmeli.,,1.00,"a CORS‑safelisted header‑field. Application Security Verification Standard Verify that HTTP requests to sensitive functionality use appropriate HTTP methods such as POST, PUT, PATCH, or DELETE, and not methods defined by the HTTP specification as “safe”such as HEAD, OPTIONS, or GET. Alternatively, strict validation of the Sec‑Fetch‑* request header fields can be used to ensure that the request did not originate from an inappropriate cross‑origin call, a navigation request, or a resource load (such as an image source) where this is not expected.",DUZELT,YUKSEK,Güvenli HTTP yöntemi seçimi önemli ancak tek başına CSRF önlemi değildir; kaynakta bağlamı tam kontrol edilmeli.,,1.00,"a CORS‑safelisted header‑field. Application Security Verification Standard Verify that HTTP requests to sensitive functionality use appropriate HTTP methods such as POST, PUT, PATCH, or DELETE, and not methods defined by the HTTP specification as “safe”such as HEAD, OPTIONS, or GET. Alternatively, strict validation of the Sec‑Fetch‑* request header fields can be used to ensure that the request did not originate from an inappropriate cross‑origin call, a navigation request, or a resource load (such as an image source) where this is not expected."
171,extra_20261108_1865,train,Web Security,OWASP Application Security Verification Standard 4.0.3-en.txt,"What mechanism should be implemented to detect and alert on abnormal numbers of requests, such as by IP, user, or total per hour/day?","The application should implement monitoring mechanisms to detect and alert on abnormal numbers of requests, such as by IP address, user agent, or total requests per hour or day. This can be achieved through rate limiting, anomaly detection systems, or by setting up threshold alerts based on request patterns.","Verify the application can detect and alert on abnormal numbers of requests, such as by IP, user, total per hour or day, or whatever makes sense for the Verify that regular backups of important data are performed and that test restoration of data is performed. Verify that backups are stored securely to prevent data from being stolen or V8.2 Client-side Data Protection Verify the application sets sufficient anti-caching headers so that sensitive data is not cached in modern browsers. Verify that data stored in browser storage (such as localStorage, sessionStorage, IndexedDB, or cookies) does not contain sensitive data. Verify that authenticated data is cleared from client storage, such as the browser DOM, after the client or session is terminated. OWASP Application Security Verification Standard 4.0.3 V8.3 Sensitive Private Data This section helps protect sensitive data from being created, read, updated, or deleted without authorization, particularly in bulk quantities. Compliance with this section implies compliance with V4 Access Control, and in particular V4.2. For example, to protect against unauthorized updates or disclosure of sensitive personal information requires adherence to V4.2.1. Please comply with this section and V4 for full coverage. Note: Privacy regulations and laws, such as the Australian Privacy Principles APP-11 or GDPR, directly affect how applications must approach the implementation of storage, use, and transmission of sensitive personal information. This ranges from severe penalties to simple advice. Please consult your local laws and regulations, and consult a qualified privacy specialist or lawyer as required. Verify that sensitive data is sent to the server in the HTTP message body or headers, and that query string parameters from any HTTP verb do not contain Verify that users have a method to remove or export their data on demand. Verify that users are provided clear language regarding collection and use of supplied personal information and that users have provided opt-in consent for the use of that data before it is used in any way. Verify that all sensitive data created and processed by the application has been identified, and ensure that a policy is in place on how to deal with sensitive data. (C8) Verify accessing sensitive data is audited (without logging the sensitive data itself), if the data is collected under relevant data protection directives or where logging of access is required. Verify that sensitive information contained in memory is overwritten as soon as it is no longer required to mitigate memory dumping attacks, using zeroes Verify that sensitive or private information that is required to be encrypted, is encrypted using approved algorithms that provide both confidentiality and Verify that sensitive personal information is subject to data retention classification, such that old or out of date data is deleted automatically, on a schedule, or as the situation requires.",,,,,KAYNAK_KONTROL,ORTA,Temel izleme isteği doğru fakat yanıt kaynakta açık olmayan araç/stratejiler ekliyor; kaynaklı yalınlaştır.,,0.41,"Verify the application can detect and alert on abnormal numbers of requests, such as by IP, user, total per hour or day, or whatever makes sense for the Verify that regular backups of important data are performed and that test restoration of data is performed. Verify that backups are stored securely to prevent data from being stolen or V