172,draft_0262,train,Web Security,AquaSense-ICS_ A Hybrid Real-Simulated Cyber-Physical Datase.txt,How many kilometers of pipes are modeled in the simulated network?,16.8,"EPANET-MSX Simulation Framework EPANET-MSX (Multi-Species eXtension) is an open-source extension of the U.S. EPA EPANET hydraulic solver that enables multi-species reactive transport modeling in pressurized water distribution networks (WDNs) [18, 19]. Unlike standard EPANET, which supports only single-species water-quality tracking, EPANET-MSX allows the simultaneous representation of coupled chemical reactions involving multiple water quality species in both the bulk water and pipe-wall (biofilm) domains. The simulation framework integrates hydraulic dynamics with multi-species chemical evolution, capturing the following interacting phenomena: • Chlorine (Cl) decay: first-order bulk degradation coupled with biofilm wall demand. • pH variation: dynamic equilibration driven by disinfec- • Turbidity (TURB): evolution of suspended particle load • Dissolved Oxygen (DO): consumption through oxidation reactions coupled with chlorine dynamics. • Conductivity (COND): variation in ionic concentration along the distribution network. • Trihalomethane formation (THM): disinfection by-products (DBPs) formed as a chlorine-dependent reaction pathway, regulated under EPA/WHO drinking water standards. • Wall Chlorine Demand (ClD): biofilm-induced chlorine consumption on internal pipe surfaces. The MSX configuration file defines the reaction kinetics governing each species in both bulk and wall phases, using coupled ordinary differential equations (ODEs) solved simultaneously at each time step across all network nodes and pipe 4.2. Hydraulic Network Topology The simulated network (Fig. 3) represents a single pressure zone of a municipal distribution system, comparable to a district metered area (DMA). We model one zone rather than a full city network because utilities operate, monitor, and isolate their systems at the zone level. Sensor placement, anomaly detection, and incident response are therefore normally carried out at this scale. The network has 16.8 km of pipes with diameters",,,,,ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,Su şebekesi boru uzunluğu web güvenliği kavramı değil.,,0.00,"nodes and pipe 4.2. Hydraulic Network Topology The simulated network (Fig. 3) represents a single pressure zone of a municipal distribution system, comparable to a district metered area (DMA). We model one zone rather than a full city network because utilities operate, monitor, and isolate their systems at the zone level. Sensor placement, anomaly detection, and incident response are therefore normally carried out at this scale. The network has 16.8 km of pipes with diameters",ALAN_DISI,YUKSEK,Su şebekesi boru uzunluğu web güvenliği kavramı değil.,,0.00,"nodes and pipe 4.2. Hydraulic Network Topology The simulated network (Fig. 3) represents a single pressure zone of a municipal distribution system, comparable to a district metered area (DMA). We model one zone rather than a full city network because utilities operate, monitor, and isolate their systems at the zone level. Sensor placement, anomaly detection, and incident response are therefore normally carried out at this scale. The network has 16.8 km of pipes with diameters"
173,kaggle1000_0643,train,Web Security,Web.Application.Security.For.Dummies.txt,What authentication schemes does the scanner need to support according to the given information?,"The scanner needs to support various authentication schemes such as Basic, Digest, HTTP Negotiate, HTML Form-based, Single Sign On, and Client SSL Certificates.","These include network transport protocols such as HTTP and SSL/TLS, plus proxy support, which enables multiple machines to use a shared Internet connection. ✓ Authentication. You want a scanner that supports various authentication schemes such as Basic, Digest, HTTP Negotiate, HTML Form-based, Single Sign On, and Client SSL Certificates. Seek support for custom implementa- ✓ Session management. A scanner needs the ability to maintain live, valid sessions for each web application. This is true for web crawling during the discovery phase, and for testing the application by sending it HTTP requests. The scanner needs session management capabilities through the use of tokens for easier authentication of users; this includes support for session management token type, token detection configuration, and token refresh. ✓ Crawling. This spidery term is the process of a scanner browsing through page by page of a website. The scanner needs to support granular configuration so it can efficiently address precise criteria for finding all vulnerabilities on every page. Crawling functionality should include identification of hostnames, support automated form submission, detect error pages and custom 404 responses, support redirects, plus identify and accept cookies and AJAX applications. ✓ Parsing. This process entails mapping the structure of a web application and its functionality. The scanner should support the usual web content types (such as HTML, JavaScript, XML, ActiveX Objects, Java Applets, Flash, and CSS), support character encoding, tolerate partial or disorganized content and customizations, and extract dynamic content with code that executes on individual clients. ✓ Testing. Testing is the meat and potatoes functionality of a scanner. It should enable granular configuration Part III: Using Automated Scanning to Test Web Applications 25 for precise control over what the scanner tests, such as IPs, URL patterns, file extensions, and so on. Testing capabilities should inc",,,,,YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,Web tarayıcısının desteklediği kimlik doğrulama şemaları.,Web Security,1.00,"machines to use a shared Internet connection. ✓ Authentication. You want a scanner that supports various authentication schemes such as Basic, Digest, HTTP Negotiate, HTML Form-based, Single Sign On, and Client SSL Certificates. Seek support for custom implementa- ✓ Session management. A scanner needs the ability to maintain live, valid sessions for each web application. This is true for web crawling during the discovery phase, and for testing the application by sending it HTTP requests. The scanner needs session management capabilities through",YANLIS_KONU,ORTA,Web tarayıcısının desteklediği kimlik doğrulama şemaları.,Web Security,1.00,"machines to use a shared Internet connection. ✓ Authentication. You want a scanner that supports various authentication schemes such as Basic, Digest, HTTP Negotiate, HTML Form-based, Single Sign On, and Client SSL Certificates. Seek support for custom implementa- ✓ Session management. A scanner needs the ability to maintain live, valid sessions for each web application. This is true for web crawling during the discovery phase, and for testing the application by sending it HTTP requests. The scanner needs session management capabilities through"
174,batch500_0430,train,Web Security,What It Is_ Not What It Says_ Securing Agentic Browsing Inte.txt,"How many elements are attributed to third parties by GENTREE compared to the browser on 1,270 real webpages?","GENTREE attributes 2.3 times as many elements to third parties as the browser on 1,270 real webpages.","What It Is, Not What It Says: Securing Agentic Browsing Interactions with Web Semantics Washington University in St. Louis Abstract—AI agents increasingly browse the web on users’ behalf, yet they consume webpages as screenshots or text, exposing their LLMs to unvetted third-party content. Browsers enforce semantics under which what an element can do depends on what it is and where it comes from, not on what it says. Agents flatten these semantics, so injected text can persuade the LLM to exercise the user’s authority for content that has none. As LLMs remain easy to persuade, the agent’s interface to the web must itself ensure the integrity of what the agent reads, interprets, and does. We propose INTACT, which runs on an unmodified browser. INTACT builds GENTREE, a bespoke representation that captures provenance and web semantics. INTACT passes the page via GENTREE to the LLM one element at a time, so the LLM decides on each element without any other element’s text. Its harness executes each step only within the capabilities of the step’s element and asks the user before acting on third-party content. On 1,270 real webpages, GENTREE attributes 2.3× as many elements to third parties as the browser, which misattributes 58.7% of them to the first party. On 1,091 of these webpages with injected attacks, only 12.8% of attacks get through INTACT’s planner, each at most as a consent request, against 73.1% for a conventional planner. Isolation disrupts functionality on only 3.7% of tasks, and a parallel schedule could remove most of AI agents increasingly act on users’ behalf, and the web gives them an API to nearly every online service. Agentic browsers (e.g., Comet [1], Claude in Chrome [2], and Gemini in Chrome [3]) and frameworks (e.g., Browser Use [4]) read pages, click, fill forms, and navigate across sites, often within the user’s authenticated sessions. This reach carries a fundamental risk, as web content is unvetted. Pages are assembled at runtime by parties the",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.89,"of the step’s element and asks the user before acting on third-party content. On 1,270 real webpages, GENTREE attributes 2.3× as many elements to third parties as the browser, which misattributes 58.7% of them to the first party. On 1,091 of these webpages with injected attacks, only 12.8% of attacks get through INTACT’s planner, each at most as a consent request, against 73.1% for a conventional planner. Isolation disrupts functionality on only 3.7% of tasks, and a parallel schedule could remove most of AI agents increasingly act on users’ beh",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.89,"of the step’s element and asks the user before acting on third-party content. On 1,270 real webpages, GENTREE attributes 2.3× as many elements to third parties as the browser, which misattributes 58.7% of them to the first party. On 1,091 of these webpages with injected attacks, only 12.8% of attacks get through INTACT’s planner, each at most as a consent request, against 73.1% for a conventional planner. Isolation disrupts functionality on only 3.7% of tasks, and a parallel schedule could remove most of AI agents increasingly act on users’ beh"
175,batch500_0500,train,Web Security,A Hybrid Information Flow Analysis Framework for Android Hyb.txt,What specific type of vulnerability is identified when the URL points to a host served by the app itself?,"The specific type of vulnerability identified is a callback-injection pattern, which is familiar from JSONP.","It reports the flow as both js/xss and js/client-side-unvalidated-url-redirection. Taken at face value, whoever controls this value controls which code runs in the WebView. On the Java side, the method delegates to IITC_FileManager.getFileRequestPrefix(), which returns the constant ""//file-request.iitcm.localhost/"". The forward flow therefore carries no attacker-influenced data, and the alert is not exploitable as stated. Following the request further shows a different flow. The URL is protocol-relative and points at a host the app serves itself. IITC_WebViewClient.shouldInterceptRequest hands every http(s) request to a *.iitcm.localhost host to the file manager. For the file-request host, a FileRequest stores the first URL path segment as mFunctionName and opens a system file chooser. After the user picks a file, it streams back a script of the form <mFunctionName>('<URL-encoded filename>', '<Base64 file content>'); Manuscript submitted to ACM A Hybrid Information Flow Analysis Framework for Android Hybrid Apps The filename is URL-encoded and the content Base64-encoded, but mFunctionName is inserted verbatim. Uri.getPathSegments() also percent-decodes it. The callback name thus makes a round trip, i.e, from JavaScript to Java →executed JavaScript, with no validation. This is the callback-injection pattern familiar from JSONP. In the main frame it grants nothing new, because a script that can choose the request path can already execute code. The Java side does not check which frame issued the request, so any content that can make the WebView fetch this host can trigger the file chooser and supply the callback. It still cannot read a file unless the user selects one. We therefore rate the round-trip weakness low. It matters methodologically more than practically, because it is a JS-to-Java-to-JS flow, and none of the analyses in this paper models that direction. Answer to RQ4. MifDroid with CodeQL backend is able to find 30 vulnerabilities with the instrumented JavaS",,,,,KANIT_ZAYIF_ON_TARAMA,ORTA,Yanıttaki ayırt edici terimlerin bir bölümü 2.000 karakterlik kaynak pasajında görünmüyor; tam belgeyle semantik kontrol gerekli.,,0.50,"Java →executed JavaScript, with no validation. This is the callback-injection pattern familiar from JSONP. In the main frame it grants nothing new, because a script that can choose the request path can already execute code. The Java side does not check which frame issued the request, so any content that can make the WebView fetch this host can trigger the file chooser and supply the callback. It still cannot read a file unless the user selects one. We therefore rate the round-trip weakness low. It matters methodologically more than practically,",KANIT_ZAYIF_ON_TARAMA,ORTA,Yanıttaki ayırt edici terimlerin bir bölümü 2.000 karakterlik kaynak pasajında görünmüyor; tam belgeyle semantik kontrol gerekli.,,0.50,"Java →executed JavaScript, with no validation. This is the callback-injection pattern familiar from JSONP. In the main frame it grants nothing new, because a script that can choose the request path can already execute code. The Java side does not check which frame issued the request, so any content that can make the WebView fetch this host can trigger the file chooser and supply the callback. It still cannot read a file unless the user selects one. We therefore rate the round-trip weakness low. It matters methodologically more than practically,"
176,batch500_0012,val,Web Security,Collaborative Vulnerability Metadata Acceptance Process (CVMAP) for CVE Numbering Authorities (CNAs) and Authorized Data Publishers.txt,What are the five submission categories that can be provided within the CVE record JSON?,"The five submission categories that can be provided within the CVE record JSON are:

1. CVSS v3.1 Base Metric Group
2. CVSS v2 Base Metric Group
3. Reference Link Tags","This ensures that proper standards and procedures have been applied to the analysis of CVE record metadata based on the information supplied. Once the analyzed CVE record has been reviewed and approved, the CVE record metadata is then published for public access. External Submission Workflow The External Submission process for both the CNA and the ADP begins by editing the CVE record JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) as noted in Appendices C, D, E, and F and following process is the only mechanism in place for providing CVE record metadata. CNAs and ADPs will not have direct access to the NVD administrative site. Once the content has been submitted, the workflow for CNAs will be dependent on the type of CVE record metadata (referred to as submission categories) provided and the acceptance level achieved. Specific details on this process and the acceptance level criteria are further defined below. The content submitted by an ADP is utilized by the NVD analyst as reference data and displayed on the public NVD website Additional details are provided in the submission categories and acceptance levels sections later Submission Categories There are five submission categories that can be provided within the CVE record JSON. There are no dependencies between the submission categories, and each submission category is optional. Both CNAs and ADPs may choose which submission categories to contribute. The categories, each of which is discussed in more detail later in this section, are as follows: • CVSS v3.1 Base Metric Group • CVSS v2 Base Metric Group • Reference Link Tags CVSS v3.1 Base Metric Group The CVSS v3.1 Base Metric Group consists of eight metrics: Attack Vector, Attack Complexity, Privileges Required, User Interaction, Scope, Confidentiality Impact, Integrity Impact, and Availability Impact. Values selected for each of these metrics are used to compute the CVSS v3.1 Base Metric score. See the CVSS v3.1 Specification Document1 for more detailed information. See Appe",,,,,ELE_ADAYI,KRITIK,Soru beş kategori istiyor; yanıt yalnızca üç öğe listeliyor. Kaynak pasajı da üç maddeden sonra kesilmiş.,,1.00,"details are provided in the submission categories and acceptance levels sections later Submission Categories There are five submission categories that can be provided within the CVE record JSON. There are no dependencies between the submission categories, and each submission category is optional. Both CNAs and ADPs may choose which submission categories to contribute. The categories, each of which is discussed in more detail later in this section, are as follows: • CVSS v3.1 Base Metric Group • CVSS v2 Base Metric Group • Reference Link Tags CV",ELE_ADAYI,KRITIK,Soru beş kategori istiyor; yanıt yalnızca üç öğe listeliyor. Kaynak pasajı da üç maddeden sonra kesilmiş.,,1.00,"details are provided in the submission categories and acceptance levels sections later Submission Categories There are five submission categories that can be provided within the CVE record JSON. There are no dependencies between the submission categories, and each submission category is optional. Both CNAs and ADPs may choose which submission categories to contribute. The categories, each of which is discussed in more detail later in this section, are as follows: • CVSS v3.1 Base Metric Group • CVSS v2 Base Metric Group • Reference Link Tags CV"
