inations appear throughout 2026. New combinations keep appearing. The number of injections collected and sampled per quarter injections. Yet each quarter of 2026 adds combinations never seen before, 42, 72, and 19, raising the InjecAgent-style instructions add the most",,,,,KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,Saldırı enjeksiyonlarının sayısı bir benchmark özelliği; web güvenliği mekanizması değil.,,1.00,"split, and count each attack attribute once per attacked page. the full benchmark and test split. Bar segments distinguish injections from production websites and BrowseSafe. Each panel uses a separate scale. (b) Injection placements Tool-availability lure System-prompt exfiltration Indirect hypothetical Browser-state tampering Fabricated auth. thread Ranking/review poisoning Phishing-link propagation Encoding obfuscation Emotional manipulation User-generated block Shared-document body Code-hosting artifact Structured-data block Link text / hre",KONU_KONTROL,ORTA,Saldırı enjeksiyonlarının sayısı bir benchmark özelliği; web güvenliği mekanizması değil.,,1.00,"split, and count each attack attribute once per attacked page. the full benchmark and test split. Bar segments distinguish injections from production websites and BrowseSafe. Each panel uses a separate scale. (b) Injection placements Tool-availability lure System-prompt exfiltration Indirect hypothetical Browser-state tampering Fabricated auth. thread Ranking/review poisoning Phishing-link propagation Encoding obfuscation Emotional manipulation User-generated block Shared-document body Code-hosting artifact Structured-data block Link text / hre"
178,batch1000_0016,val,Web Security,sec_2508.20816v1_Multi_Agent_Penetration_Testing_AI_.txt,What is the success rate of MAPTA in Server-Side Template Injection (SSTI) challenges?,MAPTA achieved an 85% success rate in Server-Side Template Injection (SSTI) challenges.,"This performance approaches XBOW’s reported 84.6% coverage in July 2024, achieving within 7.7 percentage points of the commercial system’s claimed performance. Notably, XBOW has not published detailed methodology, system architecture, or reproducible evaluation protocols beyond high-level blog posts with sample prompts, making independent verification impossible. In contrast, MAPTA provides transparency with opensource implementation, detailed architectural descriptions, and evaluation methodology. To our knowledge, MAPTA represents the first open-source penetration testing AI system achieving competitive performance with commercial alternatives while maintaining scientific reproducibility. Injection Vulnerability Performance. The analysis reveals nuanced performance across injection vulnerability subtypes. Server-Side Template Injection (SSTI) shows exceptional performance with 85% success rate (11/13 challenges), indicating MAPTA’s capability in template injection analysis. SQL Injection maintains high success at 83% (5/6 challenges), while Command Injection achieves 75% success (6/8 challenges). However, Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) demonstrates lower success at 57% (13/23 challenges) despite being the largest category, and Blind SQL Injection shows 0% success rate (0/3 challenges), representing the most challenging category for the current system. Authorization and Authentication. Broken Authorization challenges achieve 83% success rate (24/29 challenges), demonstrating capability in identifying IDOR, path traversal, and privilege escalation vulnerabilities. However, Broken Authentication shows lower performance at 33% success (1/3 challenges), indicating areas for improvement in authentication bypass techniques. High-Performance Categories. Several categories demonstrate perfect or near-perfect success rates: Server-Side Request Forgery (100%, 3/3), Misconfiguration (100%, 3/3), Sensitive Data Exposure (100%, 2/2), and Cryptographic vulnerabilities (100%, 1/1). T",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.89,"Vulnerability Performance. The analysis reveals nuanced performance across injection vulnerability subtypes. Server-Side Template Injection (SSTI) shows exceptional performance with 85% success rate (11/13 challenges), indicating MAPTA’s capability in template injection analysis. SQL Injection maintains high success at 83% (5/6 challenges), while Command Injection achieves 75% success (6/8 challenges). However, Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) demonstrates lower success at 57% (13/23 challenges) despite being the largest category, and Blind SQL Injec",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.89,"Vulnerability Performance. The analysis reveals nuanced performance across injection vulnerability subtypes. Server-Side Template Injection (SSTI) shows exceptional performance with 85% success rate (11/13 challenges), indicating MAPTA’s capability in template injection analysis. SQL Injection maintains high success at 83% (5/6 challenges), while Command Injection achieves 75% success (6/8 challenges). However, Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) demonstrates lower success at 57% (13/23 challenges) despite being the largest category, and Blind SQL Injec"
179,batch500_0240,test,Web Security,Fetch Me If You Can_ A Comprehensive Analysis of Fetch Metad.txt,What type of requests were included in the analysis when developing the crawler?,"Requests made during page load, as well as those referenced by anchor tags and frames, were included in the analysis.","Testing-Based Policy Inference Methodology In this section, we introduce our measurement methodology, the three primary components: the crawler, and the heuristic and exhaustive policy profilers, providing a comprehensive overview of each. We also performed a systematic study on the support of Fetch Metadata policies in popular open source server-side frameworks, CMSes and webservers. We refer to the insights gained from this study throughout this section and provide the details in Appendix A. Fetch Metadata policies can be enforced on individual endpoints, so detecting their deployment requires analyzing a representative set of endpoints per website. We develop a crawler using Playwright’s Firefox browser in headless mode to visit the landing page of each target website, and extract all endpoints. From these, we focus on those most likely to be protected by FM policies, guided by our expert knowledge, insights from our policy-support study (Appendix A), and online resources [4,39,68]. Endpoints referenced in <form> tags are typically associated with state-changing operations (e.g., login or logout) and are therefore more likely to be protected against attacks like CSRF. We also included requests made during page load, as well as those referenced by anchor tags and frames. While these may not directly trigger state changing operations, they can still be protected by FM policies designed to mitigate threats such as Clickjacking and XS-Leaks. Furthermore, we stored cookies, form parameters, and CSRF-Tokens relevant for each endpoint. This information helps us replicate the original requests as close as possible during our measurement, reducing potential inconsistencies that can cause requests to be rejected by the server. We identify tokens using the methodology described in [58]. Note that we only consider same-site endpoints for each crawled page. Next, we exclude resource names from the URLs collected, keeping only the path components since FM policies are typicall",,,,,ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.91,"protected against attacks like CSRF. We also included requests made during page load, as well as those referenced by anchor tags and frames. While these may not directly trigger state changing operations, they can still be protected by FM policies designed to mitigate threats such as Clickjacking and XS-Leaks. Furthermore, we stored cookies, form parameters, and CSRF-Tokens relevant for each endpoint. This information helps us replicate the original requests as close as possible during our measurement, reducing potential inconsistencies that ca",ILK_TARAMADA_BELIRGIN_HATA_YOK,DUSUK,"Soru-cevapta ilk taramada belirgin çelişki yakalanmadı. Bu doğruluk, uzman onayı veya güncellik doğrulaması DEĞİLDİR.",,0.91,"protected against attacks like CSRF. We also included requests made during page load, as well as those referenced by anchor tags and frames. While these may not directly trigger state changing operations, they can still be protected by FM policies designed to mitigate threats such as Clickjacking and XS-Leaks. Furthermore, we stored cookies, form parameters, and CSRF-Tokens relevant for each endpoint. This information helps us replicate the original requests as close as possible during our measurement, reducing potential inconsistencies that ca"
180,batch500_0400,test,Web Security,Narratives Mislead_ An Empirical Study of How LLMs Misjudge.txt,"What is the median severity-level accuracy for the CWE type ""access control"" (CWE-284) according to the provided data?","The median severity-level accuracy for the CWE type ""access control"" (CWE-284) is 62.5%.","A high score needs one plausible exploit path; a low score needs the absence of worse paths, which a report rarely establishes. Upward drift is thus expected whenever such evidence is missing, not only a calibration fault (→OP2). b) Vulnerability types: context-dependent classes are hardest.: Level accuracy for the eleven CWE types with at least five benchmark reports, across all 34 configurations (Fig. 4) has a median ranging from 28.6% for unrestricted file upload (CWE-434) to 62.5% for access control (CWE-284). The hardest classes, file upload, SSRF (CWE-918), missing authorization (CWE-862), SQL injection (CWE-89), and XSS (CWE-79), share a property: their severity depends on context that a report rarely states in full, such as what an uploaded file can execute, which internal services a forged request can reach, or in which context a script renders [34]. These are the cases in which the model must decide what to assume about missing information, the situation addressed by boundary B3 in Sect. VI. Sample sizes per class are small (5–18 except XSS reports assigned to the class (of 240) every LLM assigns Low far less often than it occurs Fig. 3. How many reports each predictor assigns to each severity class under few-shot prompting, against the true count (black bar). Counts are derived from per-class precision and recall over valid outputs; the None class (one severity-level accuracy (%) over 34 configurations CWE-284 access control (8) CWE-94 code injection (11) CWE-29 path traversal (\..\) (8) CWE-20 input validation (10) CWE-22 path traversal (5) CWE-89 SQL injection (6) CWE-862 missing authz. (6) CWE-434 file upload (7) Fig. 4. Severity-level accuracy by CWE type (benchmark reports in parentheses). Each dot is one of the 34 configurations; bars span the interquartile range and black ticks mark the median. Shaded: the five hardest types, whose severity depends on context a report rarely states. with 69), so differences between neighbouring classes should not b",,,,,KAYNAK_KONTROL,ORTA,"%62,5 değeri verilen pasajın görünür metninde yok; grafik/tabloda olabilir. Sayısal yanıt kaynak pasajından doğrulanamıyor.",,1.00,"and recall over valid outputs; the None class (one severity-level accuracy (%) over 34 configurations CWE-284 access control (8) CWE-94 code injection (11) CWE-29 path traversal (\..\) (8) CWE-20 input validation (10) CWE-22 path traversal (5) CWE-89 SQL injection (6) CWE-862 missing authz. (6) CWE-434 file upload (7) Fig. 4. Severity-level accuracy by CWE type (benchmark reports in parentheses). Each dot is one of the 34 configurations; bars span the interquartile range and black ticks mark the median. Shaded: the five hardest types, whose sev",KAYNAK_KONTROL,ORTA,"%62,5 değeri verilen pasajın görünür metninde yok; grafik/tabloda olabilir. Sayısal yanıt kaynak pasajından doğrulanamıyor.",,1.00,"and recall over valid outputs; the None class (one severity-level accuracy (%) over 34 configurations CWE-284 access control (8) CWE-94 code injection (11) CWE-29 path traversal (\..\) (8) CWE-20 input validation (10) CWE-22 path traversal (5) CWE-89 SQL injection (6) CWE-862 missing authz. (6) CWE-434 file upload (7) Fig. 4. Severity-level accuracy by CWE type (benchmark reports in parentheses). Each dot is one of the 34 configurations; bars span the interquartile range and black ticks mark the median. Shaded: the five hardest types, whose sev"
